window.QUIZ_DATA = [{"id":1,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"Which of the following is a value type in C#?","options":["A) System.String","B) System.Int32","C) System.Array","D) System.Delegate"],"answer":"B","explanation":"System.Int32 (int) is a struct, which is a value type stored directly on the stack or inline inside an object. String, Array, and Delegate are reference types stored on the managed heap."},{"id":2,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What will be the result of the following code snippet?\nint a = 10;\nint b = 3;\ndouble result = a / b;\nConsole.WriteLine(result);","options":["A) 3.3333333333333335","B) 3","C) 3.0","D) Compilation Error"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Because both operands 'a' and 'b' are integers, integer division is performed, yielding integer 3. That value is then implicitly converted to double 3.0 before being stored in 'result'."},{"id":3,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is the correct syntax for declaring a nullable integer in C#?","options":["A) nullable<int> x;","B) int? x;","C) Nullable.Int x;","D) int x = null?;"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'int?' is the syntactic shorthand in C# for 'Nullable<int>', allowing the value type int to represent null alongside standard integer values."},{"id":4,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"Which operator in C# is known as the null-coalescing operator?","options":["A) ?.","B) ??","C) ?:","D) !."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The '??' operator is the null-coalescing operator. It returns the left-hand operand if it is not null; otherwise, it evaluates and returns the right-hand operand."},{"id":5,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"How do you declare a two-dimensional rectangular array of integers with 3 rows and 4 columns?","options":["A) int[][] matrix = new int[3][4];","B) int[,] matrix = new int[3, 4];","C) int[3, 4] matrix = new int[,];","D) Array<int, 2> matrix = new Array(3, 4);"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In C#, 'int[,]' declares a rectangular multidimensional array, initialized as 'new int[3, 4]'. 'int[][]' declares a jagged array (an array of arrays)."},{"id":6,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is the purpose of the 'checked' keyword in C#?","options":["A) To ensure reference types are not null before dereferencing","B) To explicitly enable arithmetic overflow checking at runtime, throwing an OverflowException","C) To verify type compatibility before casting","D) To validate method arguments against null or empty values"],"answer":"B","explanation":"By default, arithmetic operations on integral types do not check for overflow. Wrapping them in a 'checked' block forces the runtime to raise an OverflowException if an operation exceeds bounds."},{"id":7,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"Which of the following loops evaluates its condition AFTER the body has executed at least once?","options":["A) while loop","B) for loop","C) do-while loop","D) foreach loop"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A 'do-while' loop is a post-test loop, ensuring that the code inside the loop executes at least once before the condition is checked."},{"id":8,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is the scope and type of a variable declared with the 'var' keyword?","options":["A) Dynamically typed at runtime and can change type later","B) Statically and strongly typed at compile time inferred from initialization expression","C) Always of type System.Object","D) A variant type compatible with COM objects only"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'var' is implicitly typed at compile-time. The C# compiler determines the exact type based on the right-hand initialization expression, making it strictly strongly-typed."},{"id":9,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What does the null-conditional operator '?.' do when evaluating 'person?.Address?.City' if 'person' is null?","options":["A) Throws a NullReferenceException immediately","B) Returns null without evaluating Address or City","C) Returns an empty string \"\"","D) Returns default integer 0"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The null-conditional operator '?.' short-circuits: if the left-hand operand is null, it immediately returns null without evaluating the remainder of the chain."},{"id":10,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"Which conversion requires an explicit cast in C#?","options":["A) Converting int to double","B) Converting short to int","C) Converting double to int","D) Converting byte to long"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Converting 'double' to 'int' is a narrowing conversion that could cause loss of fractional data or overflow, requiring an explicit cast: (int)myDouble."},{"module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is string immutability in C#?","options":["A) String objects cannot be modified after creation; modifying operations create a new string on the heap","B) Strings can only be allocated on the stack","C) String variables cannot be reassigned","D) Strings are automatically encrypted in memory"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Strings in C# are immutable reference types. Any operation that appears to modify a string (such as Replace or ToUpper) actually creates and returns a brand-new string instance on the managed heap.","id":11,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is the result of evaluating 'default(int)' and 'default(string)' in C#?","options":["A) 0 and null","B) null and \"\"","C) -1 and null","D) 0 and \"\""],"answer":"A","explanation":"The default literal or operator produces the default value of a type: 0 for numeric value types like int, and null for reference types like string.","id":12,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"Which bitwise operator performs a bitwise exclusive OR (XOR) in C#?","options":["A) ^","B) |","C) &","D) ~"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The caret '^' operator computes the bitwise (or logical) exclusive OR of its operands, returning 1 only if exactly one of the bits is 1.","id":13,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is string interpolation syntax introduced in C# 6.0?","options":["A) $\"Hello {name}, you have {count} items\"","B) @\"Hello {0}\", name","C) String.Format(\"Hello %s\", name)","D) #\"Hello {name}\""],"answer":"A","explanation":"String interpolation uses the '$' prefix before a string literal, allowing expressions embedded inside curly braces '{expression}' to be formatted and evaluated directly.","id":14,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 1: Review of Visual C# Syntax","question":"What is the requirement when executing a 'switch' statement on a string in C#?","options":["A) Each non-empty case block must explicitly terminate with a jump statement (such as break, return, or goto)","B) String switches are not supported in C#","C) Cases must be listed in alphabetical order","D) The switch statement cannot have a default case"],"answer":"A","explanation":"C# enforces the 'no fall-through' rule: every non-empty case block must terminate with an explicit jump statement such as break, return, throw, or goto case.","id":15,"subject":"C#"},{"id":16,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What is the key difference between the 'ref' and 'out' parameter modifiers in C#?","options":["A) 'out' parameters must be initialized before passing, whereas 'ref' parameters do not","B) 'ref' parameters must be initialized before passing, whereas 'out' parameters must be assigned a value inside the method before returning","C) 'out' parameters pass by value, while 'ref' passes by reference","D) 'ref' can only be used with value types, while 'out' can only be used with reference types"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A 'ref' argument must be initialized by the caller prior to passing. An 'out' argument does not need to be initialized before the call, but the called method is required to assign it before returning."},{"id":17,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"In C# exception handling, when is the 'finally' block executed?","options":["A) Only if an exception occurs and is successfully caught","B) Only if no exception occurs","C) Always, regardless of whether an exception occurs or is caught","D) Only if an unhandled exception crashes the application"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The 'finally' block always executes when control leaves the try block, whether normally, through a caught exception, or when rethrowing, ensuring cleanup of unmanaged resources."},{"id":18,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What is the recommended way to rethrow the original exception inside a catch block while preserving the original stack trace?","options":["A) throw ex;","B) throw;","C) return ex;","D) throw new Exception(ex.Message);"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Using 'throw;' without specifying an exception object rethrows the currently active exception and preserves the original call stack trace. 'throw ex;' resets the stack trace to the catch block."},{"id":19,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"Which of the following is an example of a named argument call in C#?","options":["A) PrintData(name: \"Alice\", age: 30);","B) PrintData(\"Alice\" as name, 30 as age);","C) PrintData(@name = \"Alice\", @age = 30);","D) PrintData(ref \"Alice\", out 30);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Named arguments in C# allow passing parameters by name using the syntax 'parameterName: value', decoupling caller parameter order from method definition order."},{"id":20,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What restriction applies to optional parameters in a C# method signature?","options":["A) They must appear before all required parameters","B) They must appear after all required parameters","C) They cannot have default compile-time constant values","D) They can only be used with reference types"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Optional parameters (parameters with default values) must appear after all required parameters at the end of the parameter list (unless followed by a 'params' array)."},{"id":21,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"Which class in System.Diagnostics provides methods and properties that only emit output in builds compiled with the DEBUG symbol defined?","options":["A) System.Diagnostics.Trace","B) System.Diagnostics.Debug","C) System.Diagnostics.EventLog","D) System.Diagnostics.PerformanceCounter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"System.Diagnostics.Debug calls are decorated with [Conditional(\"DEBUG\")] and are stripped out by the compiler in Release builds, whereas System.Diagnostics.Trace works in both Debug and Release."},{"id":22,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"How do you specify multiple catch blocks for different exception types?","options":["A) Order catch blocks from the most derived (specific) exception to the most general (System.Exception)","B) Order catch blocks starting with System.Exception first","C) Order does not matter because the runtime checks all catch blocks simultaneously","D) Catch blocks can only handle one exception type per try block"],"answer":"A","explanation":"C# catch blocks are evaluated sequentially from top to bottom. Specific derived exceptions (e.g. FileNotFoundException) must precede general exceptions (e.g. IOException, Exception) or a compiler error occurs."},{"id":23,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What is the purpose of the 'params' keyword in a method parameter?","options":["A) It permits passing parameters by reference","B) It allows passing a variable number of arguments as a single comma-separated list or an array","C) It marks the method as asynchronous","D) It enforces that parameters cannot be modified"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'params' allows callers to pass comma-separated arguments or an array of the specified type without explicitly instantiating an array. It must be the last parameter."},{"id":24,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"Which class in System.Diagnostics allows you to route trace and debug output to files, consoles, or custom destinations?","options":["A) TraceListener","B) TraceRouter","C) EventBroadcaster","D) LogSink"],"answer":"A","explanation":"TraceListener (such as DefaultTraceListener, TextWriterTraceListener, ConsoleTraceListener) receives messages from Trace or Debug and writes them to designated targets."},{"id":25,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What exception is thrown when an index is outside the bounds of an array?","options":["A) ArgumentOutOfRangeException","B) IndexOutOfRangeException","C) NullReferenceException","D) InvalidOperationException"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Accessing an element of an array with an index less than zero or greater than/equal to the array's Length throws System.IndexOutOfRangeException."},{"module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What does an Exception Filter ('when' clause) accomplish in a C# catch block?","options":["A) Catches an exception only if a specified boolean expression evaluates to true without unwinding the stack if false","B) Converts exceptions into warnings","C) Re-throws the exception after a specified delay","D) Silently suppresses the exception"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Exception filters ('catch (HttpException ex) when (ex.ErrorCode == 404)') evaluate a condition before unwinding the call stack, preserving stack state if the condition is not met.","id":26,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What base class should custom application exceptions inherit from in modern .NET?","options":["A) System.Exception","B) System.ApplicationException","C) System.SystemException","D) System.Error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Microsoft guidelines recommend deriving custom exceptions from System.Exception. System.ApplicationException was originally intended for user code but is now officially discouraged.","id":27,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What attribute in System.Runtime.CompilerServices automatically injects the name of the calling method into an optional parameter?","options":["A) [CallerMemberName]","B) [CallingMethod]","C) [MethodName]","D) [SourceContext]"],"answer":"A","explanation":"[CallerMemberName] instructs the compiler to automatically pass the member name of the caller as the default value of an optional string parameter, commonly used in INotifyPropertyChanged.","id":28,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What method in System.Environment terminates a process immediately without executing any finally blocks or finalizers?","options":["A) Environment.FailFast()","B) Environment.Exit()","C) Process.GetCurrentProcess().Kill()","D) Thread.CurrentThread.Abort()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Environment.FailFast(message) terminates the process immediately, writing an entry to the Windows Event Log and bypassing all finally blocks, finalizers, and exception handlers to prevent corruption.","id":29,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 2: Methods, Exceptions & Monitoring","question":"What does a TraceSwitch object allow developers to do?","options":["A) Dynamically filter trace and debug output levels (Off, Error, Warning, Info, Verbose) using configuration files without recompiling","B) Toggle between 32-bit and 64-bit compilation","C) Switch between synchronous and asynchronous logging","D) Route database connections"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.Diagnostics.TraceSwitch provides a multi-level switch to control tracing and debugging levels (Off, Error, Warning, Info, Verbose) declaratively via App.config.","id":30,"subject":"C#"},{"id":31,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What does XAML stand for in .NET GUI application development?","options":["A) eXtensible Application Markup Language","B) eXtended Application Management Logic","C) XML Application Model Layout","D) eXecutable Application Markup Logic"],"answer":"A","explanation":"XAML stands for Extensible Application Markup Language, an XML-based declarative language used in WPF, UWP, and Xamarin/MAUI to define UI layouts and hierarchies."},{"id":32,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"Which WPF layout panel positions child elements in rows and columns?","options":["A) StackPanel","B) Canvas","C) Grid","D) WrapPanel"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The Grid panel arranges child controls into tabular cells defined by RowDefinitions and ColumnDefinitions, assigning elements via Grid.Row and Grid.Column attached properties."},{"id":33,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"Which WPF panel positions child elements using explicit X and Y coordinates?","options":["A) Canvas","B) DockPanel","C) StackPanel","D) Grid"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Canvas allows absolute positioning of child elements relative to its boundaries using Canvas.Left, Canvas.Top, Canvas.Right, and Canvas.Bottom attached properties."},{"id":34,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What is a Routed Event in WPF that travels UP the element tree from the source element to the root?","options":["A) Direct event","B) Tunneling event","C) Bubbling event","D) Broadcast event"],"answer":"C","explanation":"A Bubbling event starts at the element where the event originated and moves upward through ancestor elements to the visual tree root. Tunneling events travel downward and typically start with 'Preview'."},{"id":35,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"How do you stop a routed event from continuing its propagation along the element tree?","options":["A) Set e.Handled = true;","B) Call e.Cancel();","C) Set e.StopBubbling = true;","D) Call e.Dispose();"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Setting the 'Handled' property of the RoutedEventArgs instance to 'true' marks the event as processed, preventing most downstream or upstream handlers from firing."},{"id":36,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"In WPF XAML, what does the prefix 'x:' in 'x:Name' designate?","options":["A) An attached property in CSS","B) The standard XAML namespace (http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml)","C) An XML schema validation tag","D) An asynchronous data binding"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'x:' prefix maps to the core XAML schema, which defines language features like x:Name, x:Key, x:Class, and x:Type."},{"id":37,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"Which WPF layout control automatically wraps child elements to the next line when space runs out?","options":["A) StackPanel","B) WrapPanel","C) DockPanel","D) UniformGrid"],"answer":"B","explanation":"WrapPanel positions child elements sequentially from left to right or top to bottom, breaking content to the next line when edge boundaries are exceeded."},{"id":38,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What is the relationship between the XAML markup file (e.g. MainWindow.xaml) and its code-behind file (MainWindow.xaml.cs)?","options":["A) They represent two unrelated classes that communicate through sockets","B) They are two parts of the same partial class compiled together into one type","C) The code-behind inherits from the XAML class","D) The XAML file dynamically interprets the code-behind at runtime"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Both files define the same class using the 'partial' keyword. The compiler merges the declarative XAML (transformed into BAML and generated C#) with your code-behind class."},{"id":39,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"Which WPF control is best suited for allowing the user to select one option from a mutually exclusive group?","options":["A) CheckBox","B) RadioButton","C) ListBox","D) TextBox"],"answer":"B","explanation":"RadioButton controls that share a GroupName are mutually exclusive; selecting one automatically deselects all other radio buttons in the group."},{"id":40,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What method must be called in the code-behind constructor of a WPF Window to parse the XAML and wire up controls?","options":["A) LoadComponent()","B) InitializeComponent()","C) RenderXAML()","D) BindElements()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"InitializeComponent() is generated by the build tool (BAML compiler) to instantiate the UI visual tree, locate elements, and wire up event handlers defined in XAML."},{"module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What is a DependencyProperty in WPF?","options":["A) A property backed by a centralized property store that supports data binding, styling, animations, and default value inheritance","B) A property that depends on an external database connection","C) A private field with a public getter only","D) An auto-property generated by the C# compiler"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Dependency Properties register with the WPF property system (DependencyProperty.Register) to enable rich features including data binding, styles, animation storyboards, and property value inheritance.","id":41,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"Which interface must a ViewModel or model class implement to notify WPF data-bound UI controls that a property value has changed?","options":["A) INotifyPropertyChanged","B) IObservable","C) IDataBindingSource","D) IPropertyListener"],"answer":"A","explanation":"INotifyPropertyChanged defines the PropertyChanged event. Raising this event notifies the WPF binding engine to update the corresponding UI elements.","id":42,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"Which binding mode ensures that changes in the UI automatically update the source data object AND changes in the source update the UI?","options":["A) TwoWay","B) OneWay","C) OneWayToSource","D) OneTime"],"answer":"A","explanation":"BindingMode.TwoWay synchronizes data in both directions: source to target and target to source, standard for interactive controls like TextBox.Text.","id":43,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What interface is implemented to create command objects that bind UI actions (like Button clicks) to ViewModel logic in WPF?","options":["A) ICommand","B) IAction","C) IDelegateCommand","D) IRoutedAction"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.Windows.Input.ICommand defines Execute(), CanExecute(), and CanExecuteChanged, decoupling UI trigger controls from the execution logic.","id":44,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 3: Graphical Applications (XAML/WPF)","question":"What is the purpose of the DataContext property in a WPF element hierarchy?","options":["A) It establishes the default source object for all data bindings on the element and its child visual tree","B) It specifies the SQL Server database connection string","C) It defines the primary color theme","D) It handles graphics rendering on the GPU"],"answer":"A","explanation":"DataContext defines the data source for data binding. Child elements in the visual tree inherit their parent's DataContext unless explicitly overridden.","id":45,"subject":"C#"},{"id":46,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"Which access modifier restricts access to members only within the same class or derived classes in the same assembly?","options":["A) protected","B) internal","C) protected internal","D) private protected"],"answer":"D","explanation":"'private protected' (introduced in C# 7.2) restricts access to derived types that are declared within the same assembly. (Note: 'protected internal' allows access from any derived class OR any class in the same assembly)."},{"id":47,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What is the primary benefit of generic collections like List<T> over non-generic collections like ArrayList?","options":["A) Generic collections do not consume heap memory","B) Type safety at compile-time and avoidance of boxing/unboxing overhead for value types","C) Generic collections can store different types in the same instance","D) Automatic database synchronization"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Generic collections (System.Collections.Generic) provide compile-time type validation and eliminate the performance cost of boxing and unboxing value types when inserting or retrieving elements."},{"id":48,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"Which generic collection stores elements as key/value pairs with fast O(1) average lookup by key?","options":["A) List<KeyValuePair<TKey, TValue>>","B) Dictionary<TKey, TValue>","C) Queue<T>","D) LinkedList<T>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Dictionary<TKey, TValue> is implemented as a hash table, providing amortized O(1) time complexity for key-based insertions, deletions, and lookups."},{"id":49,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What is an auto-implemented property in C#?","options":["A) A property that calculates its value dynamically from other properties","B) A property where the compiler automatically generates a private, anonymous backing field: public int Age { get; set; }","C) A static property shared across all instances","D) A property mapped directly to an external database table"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Auto-implemented properties (e.g., 'public string Name { get; set; }') allow concise property declaration where the C# compiler creates the private backing store field behind the scenes."},{"id":50,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"Which collection class represents a First-In, First-Out (FIFO) collection?","options":["A) Stack<T>","B) Queue<T>","C) SortedSet<T>","D) HashSet<T>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Queue<T> operates on a FIFO basis using Enqueue() and Dequeue(). Stack<T> operates on a LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) basis using Push() and Pop()."},{"id":51,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What happens when you try to add a duplicate key to a Dictionary<TKey, TValue> using the Add() method?","options":["A) The existing key's value is silently overwritten","B) An ArgumentException is thrown","C) The new entry is silently discarded","D) The key is appended with an incrementing integer"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Calling 'dictionary.Add(key, value)' throws an ArgumentException if the key already exists. In contrast, using the indexer 'dictionary[key] = value' overwrites the existing value."},{"id":52,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What is a static constructor in C# and when does it execute?","options":["A) It is called each time an instance of the class is created","B) It is executed once automatically before any instance is created or any static member is referenced","C) It requires parameters to configure global class settings","D) It can be called explicitly using ClassName.Constructor()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A static constructor has no access modifiers and no parameters. The CLR invokes it exactly once before the class is first accessed or instantiated in an AppDomain."},{"id":53,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"Which collection class ensures that no duplicate elements exist and offers high-performance set operations (union, intersection)?","options":["A) List<T>","B) ArrayList","C) HashSet<T>","D) Queue<T>"],"answer":"C","explanation":"HashSet<T> stores distinct elements without duplicates, providing high-performance set operations like UnionWith, IntersectWith, and ExceptWith using hashing."},{"id":54,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What does object initializer syntax allow in C#?","options":["A) Initializing properties/fields of an object at creation time without explicitly invoking a parameterized constructor: var p = new Person { Name = \"John\", Age = 25 };","B) Allocating objects directly to unmanaged memory","C) Automatically serializing an object to XML upon instantiation","D) Defining constructor overloads dynamically at runtime"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Object initializer syntax allows assigning values to any accessible fields or properties of an object upon creation within braces '{ ... }' after the 'new' expression."},{"id":55,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"How can you constrain a generic type parameter T to only accept classes that implement IDisposable and have a public parameterless constructor?","options":["A) where T : IDisposable, new()","B) where T == IDisposable && new()","C) [Constraint(IDisposable, DefaultConstructor)]","D) <T extends IDisposable : new>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Generic constraints in C# use the 'where' clause. The 'new()' constraint specifies a parameterless constructor and must always be listed last: 'where T : IDisposable, new()'."},{"module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What does the 'yield return' statement do inside a method returning IEnumerable<T>?","options":["A) Generates an iterator state machine that yields elements one at a time on demand as the caller enumerates","B) Returns the entire collection as an array immediately","C) Suspends the thread for a specified duration","D) Yields CPU execution to the operating system"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'yield return' enables custom lazy iteration. The compiler synthesizes a state machine class implementing IEnumerator<T> that produces values on demand when MoveNext() is called.","id":56,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What is the difference between IComparable<T> and IComparer<T>?","options":["A) IComparable<T> defines a type's default internal comparison method (CompareTo); IComparer<T> defines an external comparison strategy (Compare) passed into sort methods","B) IComparable is for value types; IComparer is for reference types","C) IComparer is deprecated in modern C#","D) There is no functional difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"IComparable<T> provides natural ordering implemented directly by the class via CompareTo(T other). IComparer<T> is implemented by a separate helper class to provide alternate sorting criteria.","id":57,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"Which collection maintains its elements in sorted order by key with O(log n) insertion and lookup?","options":["A) SortedDictionary<TKey, TValue>","B) Dictionary<TKey, TValue>","C) List<T>","D) Queue<T>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SortedDictionary<TKey, TValue> is implemented as a binary search tree (red-black tree), keeping keys sorted with O(log n) retrieval, insertion, and deletion complexity.","id":58,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"In C# generic interfaces, what does the 'out' keyword signify on a type parameter (e.g. IEnumerable<out T>)?","options":["A) Covariance: allows using a more derived type than originally specified (T is used only in output positions)","B) Contravariance: allows using a less derived type","C) The parameter is passed by reference","D) The collection cannot be read"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'out' marks a generic type parameter as covariant, meaning you can assign an IEnumerable<Derived> to an IEnumerable<Base>. T can only appear as method return values.","id":59,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 4: Classes & Type-Safe Collections","question":"What is the difference between List<T> and IReadOnlyList<T>?","options":["A) IReadOnlyList<T> exposes only Count and an indexer getter, preventing consumers from modifying the collection directly","B) IReadOnlyList cannot be enumerated","C) IReadOnlyList is stored in read-only ROM memory","D) List<T> cannot be passed into methods"],"answer":"A","explanation":"IReadOnlyList<T> provides a read-only view of an indexed collection, omitting Add, Remove, and indexer setters to prevent mutation by callers.","id":60,"subject":"C#"},{"id":61,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What keyword is used in a derived class to invoke a constructor or method of its base class?","options":["A) super","B) parent","C) base","D) this"],"answer":"C","explanation":"In C#, the 'base' keyword is used to access members of the base class or invoke base class constructors: 'public Derived() : base() { }'."},{"id":62,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What keyword must be placed on a base class method to allow a derived class to override it?","options":["A) abstract or static","B) virtual or abstract","C) sealed or dynamic","D) internal or partial"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In C#, methods are non-virtual by default. A base class method must be marked 'virtual' or 'abstract' to permit overriding in derived classes using the 'override' keyword."},{"id":63,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What is the difference between 'override' and 'new' when modifying a method in a derived class?","options":["A) 'override' creates a new method while 'new' replaces the virtual dispatch table","B) 'override' participates in polymorphic dynamic dispatch, while 'new' hides the base method without overriding it","C) 'new' can only be used with interfaces","D) There is no functional difference; they are interchangeable"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'override' modifies the virtual method table, enabling polymorphic behavior when called via a base reference. 'new' hides the inherited method; calling it through a base reference still executes the base implementation."},{"id":64,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"Can a C# class inherit from multiple base classes directly?","options":["A) Yes, C# supports full multiple implementation inheritance","B) No, C# supports single class inheritance, but a class can implement multiple interfaces","C) Yes, if all base classes are abstract","D) Only if the classes are marked with the [MultipleInheritance] attribute"],"answer":"B","explanation":"C# only allows single class inheritance (a class can only derive from one base class), but permits a class to implement any number of interfaces."},{"id":65,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What happens when a class is marked as 'sealed'?","options":["A) It cannot be instantiated","B) It cannot be inherited by another class","C) All its methods must be virtual","D) Its properties become read-only"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'sealed' keyword prevents other classes from inheriting from the sealed class. It can also be applied to overridden virtual methods to stop further overriding in subclasses."},{"id":66,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What does the 'as' operator do when casting an incompatible reference type?","options":["A) Throws an InvalidCastException","B) Returns null without throwing an exception","C) Triggers a compile-time syntax error","D) Generates a default instance of the target type"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'as' operator performs a safe cast on reference or nullable types. If the cast fails due to incompatible types, it evaluates to null instead of throwing an InvalidCastException."},{"id":67,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What is true regarding an 'abstract' class in C#?","options":["A) It can be instantiated directly using 'new'","B) It cannot be instantiated directly and is intended to serve as a common base class","C) It cannot contain any concrete methods with implementation","D) All derived classes must also be declared abstract"],"answer":"B","explanation":"An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly with 'new'. It can contain both abstract members (without bodies) and concrete members (with full implementations)."},{"id":68,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"How does the 'is' pattern-matching operator work in modern C# (e.g. 'if (obj is Person p)')?","options":["A) Checks compatibility, casts the object to type Person, and assigns it to 'p' if true","B) Only checks boolean equality of pointers","C) Clones obj into a new Person instance","D) Compares string representations of obj and Person"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The type-pattern 'is' operator tests whether 'obj' is of type Person, and if true, assigns the casted reference to the variable 'p' within the enclosing conditional scope."},{"id":69,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What is the key difference between an interface and an abstract class?","options":["A) Interfaces can have fields and constructors; abstract classes cannot","B) A class can implement multiple interfaces but inherit from only one abstract class; interfaces cannot define instance state (fields)","C) Abstract classes cannot have access modifiers on members","D) Interfaces are value types, while abstract classes are reference types"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A class can implement multiple interfaces, but only inherit from a single abstract class. Interfaces define a contract of members without instance fields, whereas abstract classes can hold state and constructors."},{"id":70,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What is the universal root class from which all types in .NET directly or indirectly derive?","options":["A) System.Type","B) System.ValueType","C) System.Object","D) System.Base"],"answer":"C","explanation":"System.Object (aliased as 'object') is the ultimate base class for all classes, structs, enums, and delegates in the .NET CLR type hierarchy."},{"module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What is Explicit Interface Implementation in C#?","options":["A) Implementing an interface member so it is only callable when the object is cast directly to that interface type: void IDisposable.Dispose() { }","B) Declaring all interface members with the public keyword","C) Writing interface code in a separate assembly","D) Implementing interfaces using reflection only"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Explicit interface implementation prefixes the member with the interface name without access modifiers. The member is hidden from the class's public interface and only accessible through an interface reference.","id":71,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"Can an overridden virtual method in a derived class be marked 'sealed'?","options":["A) Yes, 'sealed override' prevents further derived classes from overriding that specific method again","B) No, sealed can only be applied to entire classes","C) Yes, but only in abstract classes","D) Only if the method has no parameters"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Applying 'sealed override' to a method in a derived class stops the virtual inheritance chain, prohibiting any further subclasses from overriding that method.","id":72,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What does the 'protected internal' access modifier mean in C#?","options":["A) Accessible by any code in the same assembly OR by any derived class in any assembly","B) Accessible only by derived classes within the same assembly","C) Accessible only within the declaring class","D) Accessible by all classes in the entire solution"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'protected internal' is a union: members are accessible to any class in the same assembly, as well as to derived classes located in other external assemblies.","id":73,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"What happens if a derived class introduces a method with the exact same signature as a base class method without using 'override' or 'new'?","options":["A) The code compiles with a compiler warning that the method hides an inherited member, defaulting to 'new' behavior","B) A fatal compiler error occurs","C) The base method is overridden polymorphically","D) Both methods are merged into one"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Omitting 'override' or 'new' results in a compiler warning CS0108. The compiler assumes 'new' (hiding), but explicitly adding 'new' silences the warning.","id":74,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 5: Class Hierarchy & Inheritance","question":"How does C# avoid the 'Diamond Problem' of multiple inheritance common in C++?","options":["A) By strictly disallowing multiple class inheritance while allowing multiple interface implementation","B) By renaming colliding methods automatically","C) By ignoring the second base class","D) By executing both base methods in parallel"],"answer":"A","explanation":"By restricting classes to a single inheritance hierarchy while permitting implementation of multiple interfaces, C# completely eliminates state and constructor ambiguity found in multiple inheritance.","id":75,"subject":"C#"},{"id":76,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What is the primary difference between the System.IO.File class and the System.IO.FileInfo class?","options":["A) File provides static methods; FileInfo provides instance methods with cached file properties","B) File is used only for text files; FileInfo is used only for binary files","C) FileInfo requires administrative privileges; File does not","D) File operates asynchronously; FileInfo operates synchronously"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.IO.File is a static utility class best for one-off operations (each call checks security permissions). System.IO.FileInfo is an instance class that caches metadata and is optimized for repeated operations on a specific file."},{"id":77,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which class in System.IO is designed specifically for reading character data from a stream with encoding support?","options":["A) BinaryReader","B) StreamReader","C) FileStream","D) StringWriter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"StreamReader reads characters from a byte stream in a particular character encoding (such as UTF-8), providing methods like ReadLine() and ReadToEnd()."},{"id":78,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Why should FileStream and StreamReader objects be wrapped inside a 'using' statement in C#?","options":["A) To ensure that Dispose() is called immediately when leaving scope, releasing underlying OS file handles","B) To speed up disk rotation speed","C) To encrypt the file automatically on write","D) To bypass operating system file locks"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'using' statement translates to a try/finally block that guarantees Dispose() is called on the IDisposable object, deterministically releasing operating system file handles and unmanaged resources."},{"id":79,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which method of System.IO.File reads the entire contents of a text file into a single string?","options":["A) File.ReadLines()","B) File.ReadAllBytes()","C) File.ReadAllText()","D) File.ReadString()"],"answer":"C","explanation":"File.ReadAllText(path) opens a text file, reads all lines into a single string, and closes the file automatically."},{"id":80,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What is serialization in .NET?","options":["A) The process of converting an object's state into a stream of bytes or text format (XML/JSON) for storage or transmission","B) Compiling C# source code into MSIL bytecode","C) Organizing database tables into first normal form","D) Ensuring multi-threaded operations execute in strict sequential order"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Serialization is the process of converting the state of an object graph into a linear format (binary, XML, JSON) so it can be saved to disk, memory, or sent across a network."},{"id":81,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which class in System.IO provides static methods for manipulating file and directory path strings safely across operating systems?","options":["A) System.IO.DriveInfo","B) System.IO.Path","C) System.IO.FileSystemInfo","D) System.IO.DirectoryInfo"],"answer":"B","explanation":"System.IO.Path contains cross-platform static helper methods such as Path.Combine(), Path.GetExtension(), and Path.GetFileName() to handle directory path strings safely."},{"id":82,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which serializer in System.Xml.Serialization serializes only public fields and read/write properties of an object?","options":["A) BinaryFormatter","B) XmlSerializer","C) DataContractSerializer","D) SoapFormatter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"XmlSerializer only serializes public fields and public read/write properties of classes that have a public parameterless constructor. It does not serialize private or protected members."},{"id":83,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What method in FileStream forces buffered data to be written to the underlying physical storage device?","options":["A) Clear()","B) Flush()","C) Commit()","D) Sync()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The Flush() or FlushAsync() method flushes the stream's internal memory buffer to the underlying destination device, ensuring that all written data is committed."},{"id":84,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What is the benefit of File.ReadLines() over File.ReadAllLines() when processing a 10 GB log file?","options":["A) ReadLines() loads all lines into memory at once as a string[]","B) ReadLines() returns an IEnumerable<string> that streams lines lazily one by one, avoiding high memory consumption","C) ReadLines() automatically compresses the file","D) ReadLines() runs synchronously while ReadAllLines() is asynchronous"],"answer":"B","explanation":"File.ReadLines returns an IEnumerable<string> that yields lines lazily, reading from disk on demand. File.ReadAllLines allocates an entire string[] array in memory upfront, risking OutOfMemoryException on large files."},{"id":85,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which class is used to write primitive data types in binary format directly to a stream?","options":["A) BinaryWriter","B) StreamWriter","C) StringWriter","D) TextWriter"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.IO.BinaryWriter writes primitive data types (integers, booleans, floating-point numbers) in raw binary format to an underlying stream."},{"module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which stream in System.IO.Compression compresses data written to it using the standard DEFLATE compression algorithm?","options":["A) DeflateStream","B) ZipArchiveStream","C) CompressStream","D) PackedStream"],"answer":"A","explanation":"DeflateStream (and GZipStream) in System.IO.Compression wraps an underlying stream to compress or decompress data using the industry-standard Deflate algorithm.","id":86,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"Which stream class creates a stream whose backing store is physical memory rather than a disk file?","options":["A) MemoryStream","B) RamStream","C) BufferStream","D) VirtualStream"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.IO.MemoryStream encapsulates an in-memory byte array as a stream, ideal for temporary serialization or buffering without disk I/O overhead.","id":87,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What method in System.IO.Path retrieves the path to the current user's temporary folder?","options":["A) Path.GetTempPath()","B) Path.GetTemporaryDirectory()","C) Path.TempFolder","D) Directory.GetTemp()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Path.GetTempPath() returns the path of the current system user's temporary directory (e.g. C:\\Users\\User\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\).","id":88,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What parameter in FileStream constructors controls whether other processes can open the same file concurrently?","options":["A) FileShare (e.g. FileShare.Read, FileShare.None)","B) FileAccess","C) FileMode","D) FileOptions"],"answer":"A","explanation":"FileShare determines how the file will be shared by other processes (FileShare.None blocks others; FileShare.Read allows concurrent reading).","id":89,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 6: Reading and Writing Local Data","question":"What method allows moving the current read/write position to an arbitrary offset within a seekable stream?","options":["A) stream.Seek(offset, SeekOrigin)","B) stream.MoveTo(offset)","C) stream.PositionOffset(offset)","D) stream.Jump(offset)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Seek(long offset, SeekOrigin origin) method sets the position within the current stream relative to Begin, Current, or End.","id":90,"subject":"C#"},{"id":91,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"What does ORM stand for in software development?","options":["A) Object-Relational Mapping","B) Operational Relationship Model","C) Organized Record Management","D) Object Resource Monitoring"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ORM stands for Object-Relational Mapping, a software architecture technique that maps relational database tables, columns, and relationships to object-oriented classes and properties."},{"id":92,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"In Entity Framework, which primary class coordinates database connectivity and change tracking for entity models?","options":["A) SqlConnection","B) DbContext","C) ObjectBroker","D) DataAdapter"],"answer":"B","explanation":"DbContext is the primary class in Entity Framework that manages database connections, provides DbSet<TEntity> collections, configures models, and tracks changes to persist updates."},{"id":93,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"What is 'deferred execution' (lazy evaluation) in LINQ queries?","options":["A) The query executes on a background thread automatically","B) The query definition is not sent to the database until the results are enumerated (e.g. with foreach or ToList())","C) The query is cached forever in memory","D) The database aborts long-running queries"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In LINQ, query creation does not execute the query. Execution is deferred until the query variable is iterated over (e.g., using a foreach loop) or an immediate execution method like ToList(), ToArray(), or Count() is called."},{"id":94,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"Which LINQ method immediately forces execution of a query and returns results in a List<T>?","options":["A) .Where()","B) .Select()","C) .ToList()","D) .OrderBy()"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Methods like ToList(), ToArray(), and ToDictionary() force immediate execution of the LINQ query pipeline and materialize the results into memory."},{"id":95,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"In ADO.NET, which object provides a high-performance, read-only, forward-only stream of data from a SQL database?","options":["A) DataSet","B) DataTable","C) SqlDataReader","D) SqlDataAdapter"],"answer":"C","explanation":"SqlDataReader reads data rows in a forward-only, read-only manner directly from the database connection, minimizing memory footprint and overhead compared to DataSet."},{"id":96,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"How do you prevent SQL Injection attacks when executing queries in ADO.NET?","options":["A) Concatenate all user input directly into the SQL string","B) Use parameterized queries with SqlParameter objects","C) Store SQL strings in XML files","D) Run the query inside a try-catch block"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Parameterized queries (e.g., cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(\"@name\", userInput)) treat user input strictly as literal values rather than executable code, completely preventing SQL injection."},{"id":97,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"Which method must be called on a DbContext instance to persist all pending inserts, updates, and deletes to the database?","options":["A) context.Submit()","B) context.SaveChanges()","C) context.CommitTransaction()","D) context.Persist()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"SaveChanges() (or SaveChangesAsync()) examines the ChangeTracker for all modified, added, and deleted entities and issues the corresponding INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE SQL statements within a transaction."},{"id":98,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"What is the difference between LINQ method syntax and LINQ query syntax?","options":["A) Method syntax uses extension methods with lambdas (e.g. .Where(x => x.Active)); query syntax uses SQL-like clauses (from...where...select)","B) Query syntax compiles to faster machine code than method syntax","C) Method syntax can only be used with XML, not databases","D) Query syntax requires an active internet connection"],"answer":"A","explanation":"LINQ query syntax provides declarative 'from...where...select' keywords, which the compiler translates directly into equivalent fluent method syntax calls using extension methods and lambdas."},{"id":99,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"In Entity Framework Code First, what is used to define constraints such as [Required] or [MaxLength(50)] directly on entity properties?","options":["A) Data Annotations","B) XAML styles","C) SQL Triggers","D) Assembly Manifests"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Data Annotations (from System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations) provide declarative attributes like [Key], [Required], and [StringLength] to define validation rules and database schema mappings."},{"id":100,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"What is 'Eager Loading' in Entity Framework?","options":["A) Loading related navigation properties upfront in the initial query using the .Include() method","B) Loading child entities only when their property is first accessed at runtime","C) Downloading the entire database schema upon application startup","D) Running queries synchronously on the UI thread"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Eager Loading uses the .Include() method to load related entities as part of the initial database query via JOINs, avoiding the 'N+1 query problem' associated with lazy loading."},{"module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"What is the performance advantage of using '.AsNoTracking()' in Entity Framework read queries?","options":["A) Disables the DbContext Change Tracker, reducing memory allocation and CPU overhead for read-only queries","B) Bypasses network firewalls","C) Prevents other users from reading data","D) Encrypts query results"],"answer":"A","explanation":"When querying data for display without plans to update, calling .AsNoTracking() tells EF not to create tracking snapshot entries in memory, significantly improving performance.","id":101,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"How is optimistic concurrency control implemented in Entity Framework?","options":["A) Using a timestamp/rowversion column decorated with [Timestamp] or properties decorated with [ConcurrencyCheck]","B) Locking the entire database table during reads","C) Retrying failed transactions indefinitely","D) Using exclusive database transactions"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Optimistic concurrency compares the current rowversion/timestamp in the database with the original value at read time in the UPDATE WHERE clause, throwing a DbUpdateConcurrencyException if mismatched.","id":102,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"Why must navigation properties be declared as 'virtual' in Entity Framework models when lazy loading is enabled?","options":["A) To allow EF dynamic proxy classes to override the property and inject lazy-loading database queries when accessed","B) To allow properties to be null","C) To serialize properties to JSON","D) To store properties in RAM"],"answer":"A","explanation":"EF generates dynamic runtime proxy classes that inherit from the entity model and override virtual navigation properties to trigger database fetches upon first access.","id":103,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"Which command in the Package Manager Console applies pending Entity Framework Code First migrations to the target database?","options":["A) Update-Database","B) Add-Migration","C) Apply-Schema","D) Sync-Database"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'Update-Database' inspects the migration history table (__EFMigrationsHistory) and executes the pending migration scripts against the target database.","id":104,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 7: Accessing a Database","question":"What method on DbContext or DbSet allows executing raw SQL queries that map to entity types?","options":["A) dbSet.SqlQuery() (or FromSqlRaw in EF Core)","B) dbSet.ExecuteQuery()","C) dbContext.RunSql()","D) dbSet.NativeCommand()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SqlQuery() (in EF6) and FromSqlRaw() (in EF Core) execute raw SQL SELECT statements and map the returned result sets directly into strongly-typed entity instances.","id":105,"subject":"C#"},{"id":106,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Which modern class in System.Net.Http is recommended for sending HTTP requests and receiving HTTP responses in .NET?","options":["A) WebClient","B) HttpWebRequest","C) HttpClient","D) Socket"],"answer":"C","explanation":"HttpClient is the modern, flexible, asynchronous class for making HTTP requests in .NET. It is intended to be instantiated once and reused throughout the application lifecycle."},{"id":107,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"What protocol and format standard does WCF Data Services use to expose and consume data over the web?","options":["A) OData (Open Data Protocol) over HTTP","B) SOAP with binary TCP encoding","C) FTP over SSL","D) GraphQL over WebSockets"],"answer":"A","explanation":"WCF Data Services implements the OData (Open Data Protocol) standard, exposing data as RESTful resources using standard HTTP verbs (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and AtomPub/JSON formats."},{"id":108,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Which HTTP method should be used to retrieve data from a remote REST API without modifying server state?","options":["A) POST","B) GET","C) PUT","D) DELETE"],"answer":"B","explanation":"HTTP GET is safe and idempotent, used specifically to request representations of specified target resources from the server without causing side effects."},{"id":109,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"What asynchronous method of HttpClient sends an HTTP GET request and returns the response body directly as a string?","options":["A) GetStringAsync()","B) FetchTextAsync()","C) DownloadString()","D) ReadAsString()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HttpClient.GetStringAsync(uri) sends a GET request to the specified URI and returns the response body as a string asynchronously."},{"id":110,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Which HTTP status code indicates that the requested resource was successfully created on the server?","options":["A) 200 OK","B) 201 Created","C) 204 No Content","D) 301 Moved Permanently"],"answer":"B","explanation":"HTTP 201 Created indicates that the request succeeded and resulted in one or more new resources being created on the server (common response for POST operations)."},{"id":111,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"What serializer in System.Runtime.Serialization.Json can serialize objects into JSON format and deserialize JSON back into objects?","options":["A) DataContractJsonSerializer","B) BinaryFormatter","C) SoapFormatter","D) XmlSerializer"],"answer":"A","explanation":"DataContractJsonSerializer is a .NET Framework class that serializes objects decorated with [DataContract] and [DataMember] attributes to and from JSON."},{"id":112,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Why is it best practice to reuse a single instance of HttpClient rather than instantiating a new one for every request?","options":["A) HttpClient cannot be disposed","B) Creating and disposing new instances rapidly exhausts underlying operating system sockets (socket exhaustion in TIME_WAIT state)","C) Only one HttpClient can run per computer","D) It prevents compiler optimization"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Instantiating and disposing HttpClient per request leaves underlying OS TCP sockets in the TIME_WAIT state, rapidly leading to socket exhaustion under moderate to heavy loads."},{"id":113,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"In OData query options, which query string parameter is used to sort the retrieved results by a given property?","options":["A) $filter","B) $select","C) $orderby","D) $top"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The '$orderby' OData query option specifies an expression for ordering the records returned by the query (e.g., $orderby=Price desc)."},{"id":114,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"What class is used with HttpClient to send multipart form data such as file uploads?","options":["A) StringContent","B) MultipartFormDataContent","C) ByteArrayContent","D) StreamContent"],"answer":"B","explanation":"MultipartFormDataContent is a specialized HttpContent container for sending multi-part MIME form data (useful for uploading files alongside form fields)."},{"id":115,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Which method in HttpResponseMessage throws an HttpRequestException if the HTTP response indicates failure (status code outside 200-299)?","options":["A) CheckSuccess()","B) EnsureSuccessStatusCode()","C) ThrowOnFailure()","D) ValidateStatus()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"HttpResponseMessage.EnsureSuccessStatusCode() validates the IsSuccessStatusCode property and throws an HttpRequestException if the status code indicates an error."},{"module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"How do you attach a JWT Bearer authentication token to an HttpClient request?","options":["A) client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue(\"Bearer\", token);","B) client.Headers.Add(\"Bearer-Token\", token);","C) client.AddAuth(token);","D) client.SetSecurityToken(token);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Setting 'DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization' to an AuthenticationHeaderValue with scheme \"Bearer\" and parameter 'token' injects standard HTTP Authorization headers into outgoing requests.","id":116,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Which class configures connection pooling, cookie containers, and automatic decompression for an HttpClient?","options":["A) HttpClientHandler","B) WebRequestHandler","C) HttpConfiguration","D) SocketManager"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HttpClientHandler is passed to the HttpClient constructor to configure low-level transport settings including CookieContainer, UseCookies, and AutomaticDecompression.","id":117,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"What attribute in WCF is applied to an interface to declare it as an exposed service contract?","options":["A) [ServiceContract]","B) [WebMethod]","C) [RemoteService]","D) [WCFInterface]"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.ServiceModel.ServiceContractAttribute marks an interface or class as defining an exposed WCF service contract, with individual methods marked with [OperationContract].","id":118,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"How can you cancel a pending HttpClient asynchronous request if it exceeds a custom timeout?","options":["A) Pass a CancellationToken from a CancellationTokenSource configured with CancelAfter()","B) Call client.Abort()","C) Kill the thread","D) Dispose the operating system socket"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Methods like GetAsync, PostAsync, and SendAsync accept a CancellationToken. Using 'new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5))' cancels the request if the duration elapses.","id":119,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 8: Accessing Remote Data","question":"Which HTTP method should be used when updating an existing resource by replacing its entire state?","options":["A) PUT","B) GET","C) POST","D) PATCH"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HTTP PUT is idempotent and represents replacing the target resource completely. HTTP PATCH is used for partial updates, and POST is for creating new resources.","id":120,"subject":"C#"},{"id":121,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"In WPF XAML, what element is used to group reusable visual property settings (such as Background, FontSize, Margin) into a single definition?","options":["A) Style","B) Template","C) ResourceDictionary","D) Trigger"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A <Style> element allows you to set a collection of property values on controls of a specific TargetType using <Setter Property=\"...\" Value=\"...\" /> elements."},{"id":122,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is the difference between StaticResource and DynamicResource in WPF?","options":["A) StaticResource evaluates the resource once at compile/load time, while DynamicResource evaluates and updates the reference whenever the resource changes at runtime","B) DynamicResource works only with strings; StaticResource works with all types","C) StaticResource can only be defined in App.xaml","D) DynamicResource is deprecated in modern .NET"],"answer":"A","explanation":"StaticResource resolves the resource reference once during initial loading and will not detect subsequent changes. DynamicResource looks up the resource at runtime whenever the resource key or dictionary changes."},{"id":123,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is a ControlTemplate in WPF?","options":["A) A template that defines the visual appearance and visual tree structure of a control, separating appearance from control behavior","B) A C# class file template in Visual Studio","C) A database table design pattern","D) A style that only affects text elements"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A ControlTemplate replaces or defines the visual tree of a control (how it looks), completely decoupling visual representation from the control's programmatic functionality and behavior."},{"id":124,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is the purpose of a DataTemplate in WPF?","options":["A) To configure database connection strings in XAML","B) To define the visual presentation and layout of data objects (e.g. in a ListBox or ItemsControl)","C) To restrict user data input formats","D) To define server schemas"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A DataTemplate defines how data objects are visualized when bound to UI controls like ListBox, ComboBox, or ContentControl."},{"id":125,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is the difference between a UserControl and a CustomControl in WPF?","options":["A) A UserControl combines existing controls using XAML and code-behind; a CustomControl inherits directly from Control and relies on themes/generic.xaml for lookless templating","B) CustomControls cannot be styled with ControlTemplates","C) UserControls cannot have event handlers","D) There is no difference; they are synonymous"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UserControls are composed of existing controls with an associated XAML/code-behind file. CustomControls are lookless controls that inherit from Control and provide default styling via Themes/Generic.xaml."},{"id":126,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"Which property on a Style allows it to inherit property setters from an existing style?","options":["A) Inherits","B) BasedOn","C) Extends","D) ParentStyle"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'BasedOn' attribute allows a Style to inherit setters and behaviors from a base style: <Style TargetType=\"Button\" BasedOn=\"{StaticResource BaseButtonStyle}\">."},{"id":127,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is a Property Trigger in a WPF Style?","options":["A) A trigger that executes SQL queries upon mouse clicks","B) A rule that applies changes to property values when a specified property condition is met (e.g., IsMouseOver is True)","C) An event that raises an exception when a property is null","D) A hardware interrupt mechanism"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A Trigger monitors a dependency property (e.g., IsMouseOver == true) and applies setters while that condition is active, reverting to original values when the condition ceases."},{"id":128,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"Where should application-wide resources be defined so they are accessible to all windows and pages?","options":["A) MainWindow.xaml Resources","B) App.xaml Application.Resources","C) AssemblyInfo.cs","D) Program.cs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Resources placed in App.xaml under <Application.Resources> are global across the entire WPF application."},{"id":129,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What WPF element is used to present content inside a ControlTemplate where child elements of a ContentControl should appear?","options":["A) ContentPresenter","B) ItemsPresenter","C) Viewport3D","D) DataPresenter"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In a ControlTemplate for a ContentControl (such as a Button), the ContentPresenter acts as the placeholder indicating where the control's Content should be inserted and rendered."},{"id":130,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is a ResourceDictionary in WPF?","options":["A) A lookup table of SQL connection strings","B) A repository for WPF resources (such as brushes, styles, and templates) that can be merged across files","C) A dictionary mapping exception codes to error strings","D) An assembly cache"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A ResourceDictionary holds key-value pairs of WPF resources. It can be organized into standalone XAML files and merged into application resources using MergedDictionaries."},{"module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is an Attached Property in XAML (e.g. Grid.Row=\"1\")?","options":["A) A property defined by a parent layout element that can be attached to child elements to control how they are laid out","B) A property that attaches to an external database","C) A property bound to a file attachment","D) A property generated dynamically by reflection"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Attached properties allow child elements to declare values for properties defined by an ancestor control (such as Grid.Row, Grid.Column, or DockPanel.Dock).","id":131,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What is the Visual State Manager (VSM) in WPF/XAML?","options":["A) A framework that manages transitions between visual states (like Normal, MouseOver, Pressed, Disabled) on controls","B) A GPU driver optimizer","C) A window state manager for minimizing/maximizing","D) A source control integration tool"],"answer":"A","explanation":"VisualStateManager enables control template authors to define states (e.g., CommonStates: Normal, MouseOver, Pressed) and smooth transition animations between states.","id":132,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"Where should the default style and control template for a CustomControl be placed by convention?","options":["A) Themes/Generic.xaml","B) App.xaml","C) MainWindow.xaml","D) Assets/Styles.xaml"],"answer":"A","explanation":"WPF looks for custom control templates in the assembly's 'Themes/Generic.xaml' resource dictionary, which enables lookless templating and OS theme integration.","id":133,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What class in WPF coordinates timeline animations across multiple UI element properties?","options":["A) Storyboard","B) AnimationQueue","C) TimelineManager","D) KeyframeTrack"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A Storyboard is a container timeline that orchestrates animations (such as DoubleAnimation, ColorAnimation) and applies them to specific targets and properties.","id":134,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 9: UI Design & Styling","question":"What happens if a WPF Style specifies a TargetType without an explicit x:Key attribute?","options":["A) It becomes an implicit style applied automatically to all controls of that TargetType within its scope","B) A XAML parsing error occurs requiring an x:Key","C) The style is ignored","D) It only applies to the root Window"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A Style with a TargetType and no x:Key implicitly uses the TargetType as its key, automatically styling every matching control within that resource scope.","id":135,"subject":"C#"},{"id":136,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What keyword pair in C# allows writing asynchronous code that looks and behaves like synchronous code without blocking the calling thread?","options":["A) defer / resume","B) async / await","C) thread / wait","D) fork / join"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'async' and 'await' keywords enable asynchronous programming by having the compiler transform the method into a state machine that yields execution while waiting for tasks to complete."},{"id":137,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What return type should be used for an async method that does not return any value, except in event handlers?","options":["A) void","B) Task","C) Task<void>","D) Thread"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Async methods should return 'Task' (or 'Task<T>' if returning a value). 'async void' should only be used for top-level event handlers because exceptions in async void methods cannot be caught by callers."},{"id":138,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"How do you queue a CPU-bound operation to run on a background thread pool thread?","options":["A) Task.Run(() => DoWork());","B) Thread.Sleep(100);","C) Task.WaitAll();","D) GC.Collect();"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Task.Run queues the specified action to execute on the CLR ThreadPool and returns a Task representing that operation."},{"id":139,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What mechanism is used in .NET for cooperatively canceling running asynchronous operations and tasks?","options":["A) Thread.Abort()","B) CancellationToken and CancellationTokenSource","C) Process.Kill()","D) Task.Kill()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Cancellation in .NET is cooperative, driven by CancellationTokenSource (which signals cancellation) and CancellationToken (which tasks inspect via token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested())."},{"id":140,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What does PLINQ (Parallel LINQ) do when you call .AsParallel() on an IEnumerable?","options":["A) Converts the data into a binary stream","B) Partitions the data source and executes the query concurrently across multiple processor cores","C) Makes the collection immutable","D) Uploads the query to a remote SQL server"],"answer":"B","explanation":".AsParallel() binds the query to the PLINQ engine, which partitions the input sequence and processes chunks in parallel across multiple worker threads to maximize CPU utilization."},{"id":141,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What does the 'lock' statement in C# accomplish?","options":["A) Locks a file on disk against write access","B) Obtains a mutual-exclusion lock on a reference object (using Monitor.Enter/Exit) to prevent multiple threads from executing a critical section simultaneously","C) Encrypts an object in memory","D) Freezes memory to prevent garbage collection"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'lock' statement obtains an exclusive monitor lock on a given reference object for the duration of the code block, ensuring thread-safe access to shared mutable resources."},{"id":142,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What happens if a background thread attempts to directly modify a WPF UI control (like a TextBox.Text)?","options":["A) The UI updates smoothly without issues","B) An InvalidOperationException is thrown stating that the calling thread cannot access the object because a different thread owns it","C) The operating system reboots","D) The control becomes read-only"],"answer":"B","explanation":"WPF controls have thread affinity and can only be accessed by the thread that created them (the UI thread). Accessing them from another thread throws an InvalidOperationException unless dispatched via Dispatcher."},{"id":143,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"How can a background worker thread safely update a WPF UI control?","options":["A) Use Dispatcher.Invoke() or Dispatcher.BeginInvoke()","B) Call Thread.Yield()","C) Use GC.KeepAlive()","D) Wrap the assignment in a lock statement"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Dispatcher associated with the UI thread provides Invoke() (synchronous) and BeginInvoke() (asynchronous) to execute delegates safely on the UI thread."},{"id":144,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What class in System.Threading.Tasks is used to run iterations of a loop in parallel?","options":["A) Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach","B) ThreadLoop.Run","C) TaskIterator.Execute","D) AsyncLoop.Start"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach in System.Threading.Tasks execute loop iterations concurrently using threads from the thread pool."},{"id":145,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What is a 'deadlock' in concurrent programming?","options":["A) When a thread completes its execution successfully","B) A situation where two or more threads are permanently blocked, each waiting for a lock held by the other","C) When memory leaks cause an application crash","D) An unhandled NullReferenceException"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A deadlock occurs when two or more threads cannot proceed because each is waiting for the other to release a lock or resource, causing an indefinite freeze."},{"module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"Why should library developers use '.ConfigureAwait(false)' when awaiting asynchronous calls?","options":["A) It prevents capturing the original SynchronizationContext, avoiding deadlocks when consumer applications synchronously block on async code","B) It runs the code on the GPU","C) It disables exception handling","D) It speeds up internet download speeds"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ConfigureAwait(false) tells the awaiter that the continuation does not need to resume on the original SynchronizationContext (like the UI thread), eliminating deadlocks when callers use .Result or .Wait().","id":146,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"Which class in System.Threading provides lock-free atomic operations like Increment, Decrement, and Exchange on shared variables?","options":["A) Interlocked","B) Monitor","C) Mutex","D) Semaphore"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.Threading.Interlocked provides atomic operations (such as Interlocked.Increment, CompareExchange) executed directly in hardware without thread-locking overhead.","id":147,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What is the primary difference between Task.WhenAll and Task.WhenAny?","options":["A) WhenAll completes when all supplied tasks complete; WhenAny completes as soon as the first task among them completes","B) WhenAll is synchronous; WhenAny is asynchronous","C) WhenAny cancels remaining tasks automatically","D) WhenAll can only take two tasks"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Task.WhenAll returns a Task that completes only after every task in the collection finishes. Task.WhenAny returns a Task that completes as soon as any single task finishes.","id":148,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"Which synchronization primitive in .NET asynchronously limits the number of threads that can access a resource concurrently?","options":["A) SemaphoreSlim (via WaitAsync)","B) lock statement","C) Mutex","D) Monitor"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SemaphoreSlim is a lightweight throttling construct that supports async-friendly awaiting via 'await semaphore.WaitAsync()', unlike Monitor or lock which block threads.","id":149,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 10: Performance & Responsiveness","question":"What is the benefit of returning ValueTask<T> instead of Task<T> in high-throughput asynchronous methods that often complete synchronously?","options":["A) ValueTask<T> is a struct and avoids heap allocation when the operation completes synchronously (e.g. from cache)","B) ValueTask<T> automatically runs on multiple CPU cores","C) ValueTask<T> never throws exceptions","D) ValueTask<T> can only be used with integers"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ValueTask<T> is a value type (struct). If an asynchronous method completes synchronously (e.g. reading from an internal buffer), it avoids allocating a Task object on the heap.","id":150,"subject":"C#"},{"id":151,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What is the role of the 'dynamic' keyword introduced in C# 4.0?","options":["A) It allows compile-time type verification","B) It bypasses compile-time type checking; operations on dynamic objects are resolved at runtime via the Dynamic Language Runtime (DLR)","C) It converts value types to pointers","D) It dynamically allocates memory on the stack"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'dynamic' keyword bypasses compile-time type checking. Member lookups, invocations, and operations are dispatched at runtime by the DLR (Dynamic Language Runtime)."},{"id":152,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What class in System.Dynamic allows you to dynamically add and remove properties at runtime?","options":["A) ExpandoObject","B) DynamicObject","C) FlexibleType","D) RuntimeClass"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ExpandoObject represents an object whose members can be dynamically added, updated, and removed at runtime, behaving like a dynamic dictionary."},{"id":153,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What attribute is used in Platform Invoke (P/Invoke) to call functions exported by unmanaged native DLLs (e.g. Win32 APIs)?","options":["A) [NativeImport]","B) [DllImport]","C) [COMImport]","D) [UnmanagedFunction]"],"answer":"B","explanation":"System.Runtime.InteropServices.DllImportAttribute is applied to extern static methods to call unmanaged functions exported from native dynamic link libraries (like user32.dll, kernel32.dll)."},{"id":154,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What wrapper does .NET create to allow managed code to interact with an unmanaged COM component?","options":["A) COM Callable Wrapper (CCW)","B) Runtime Callable Wrapper (RCW)","C) Unmanaged Proxy Bridge (UPB)","D) Native Stub Adapter (NSA)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The CLR creates a Runtime Callable Wrapper (RCW) to proxy calls from managed .NET code to unmanaged COM components, managing reference counting and marshaling."},{"id":155,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What interface must a class implement to participate in the standard .NET dispose pattern for releasing unmanaged resources?","options":["A) IClosable","B) IDisposable","C) IFinalizable","D) ICleanable"],"answer":"B","explanation":"IDisposable requires implementing a single method: 'void Dispose()', which allows deterministic cleanup of unmanaged resources such as handles, sockets, and memory pointers."},{"id":156,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What method should be called inside a Dispose() method to inform the Garbage Collector that it does not need to call the object's finalizer?","options":["A) GC.Collect()","B) GC.SuppressFinalize(this)","C) GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers()","D) GC.ReRegisterForFinalize(this)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"GC.SuppressFinalize(this) removes the object from the CLR finalization queue, saving the performance overhead of running the finalizer during garbage collection."},{"id":157,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"How many generations does the .NET garbage collector manage for the ephemeral managed heap?","options":["A) 2 (Gen 0, Gen 1)","B) 3 (Gen 0, Gen 1, Gen 2)","C) 4 (Gen 0, Gen 1, Gen 2, Gen 3)","D) 5"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The CLR GC divides the managed heap into three generations: Generation 0 (short-lived newly allocated objects), Generation 1 (buffer generation between short-lived and long-lived), and Generation 2 (long-lived objects and LOH)."},{"id":158,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What syntax is used to define a finalizer (destructor) in C#?","options":["A) finalize() { }","B) ~ClassName() { }","C) dispose(bool finalizing) { }","D) destroy ClassName() { }"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In C#, a finalizer is declared using the tilde symbol followed by the class name: ~ClassName() { }. The compiler translates this into an override of Object.Finalize()."},{"id":159,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What is marshaling in .NET interop?","options":["A) The process of converting data types between managed and unmanaged representations during cross-boundary calls","B) Organizing classes into alphabetical order","C) Converting IL into native machine instructions","D) Verifying digital signatures"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Marshaling is the process by which the CLR's interop marshaler converts types between managed code representations (e.g. C# string) and native unmanaged formats (e.g. LPWSTR, char*)."},{"id":160,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What happens when you force a garbage collection using GC.Collect() in production applications without a specific requirement?","options":["A) It permanently prevents all memory leaks","B) It degrades application performance by suspending active threads and disrupting GC self-tuning heuristics","C) It frees unmanaged memory buffers automatically","D) It increases CPU cache efficiency"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Calling GC.Collect() disrupts the GC's self-tuning algorithms, promotes ephemeral objects into older generations prematurely, and pauses threads, hurting overall throughput."},{"module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"Which class in Microsoft.Win32.SafeHandles is recommended for encapsulating unmanaged operating system handles safely against handle leaks?","options":["A) SafeHandle","B) IntPtr","C) NativeHandle","D) HandleRef"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SafeHandle wraps operating system resource handles (like file handles or window handles), guaranteeing reliable cleanup during garbage collection and preventing recycling attacks.","id":161,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What keyword in unsafe C# blocks prevents the Garbage Collector from relocating a managed object in memory while an unmanaged pointer references it?","options":["A) fixed","B) pin","C) lock","D) hold"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'fixed' statement pins a movable managed object in memory, fixing its memory address so that pointers can safely reference it without the GC relocating it during compaction.","id":162,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"Which calling convention is the standard default for Windows Win32 API functions in P/Invoke?","options":["A) CallingConvention.StdCall","B) CallingConvention.Cdecl","C) CallingConvention.FastCall","D) CallingConvention.ThisCall"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CallingConvention.StdCall is the standard calling convention for Win32 API calls, where the callee cleans up the stack.","id":163,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What method in System.GC returns the approximate number of bytes of memory currently allocated in the managed heap?","options":["A) GC.GetTotalMemory(forceFullCollection)","B) GC.GetAllocatedBytes()","C) GC.MemoryUsage()","D) GC.QueryHeapSize()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GC.GetTotalMemory(bool forceFullCollection) retrieves the estimated number of bytes currently allocated in the managed memory heap.","id":164,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 11: Integrating with Unmanaged Code","question":"What interface allows custom dynamic types to participate in the Dynamic Language Runtime (DLR) binding mechanism?","options":["A) IDynamicMetaObjectProvider","B) IDynamicObject","C) IDlrBinder","D) IRuntimeTypeProvider"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Types implementing IDynamicMetaObjectProvider (like DynamicObject and ExpandoObject) can customize their dynamic behavior by returning a DynamicMetaObject that controls operations at runtime.","id":165,"subject":"C#"},{"id":166,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What is Reflection in .NET?","options":["A) Reversing the bits of a byte array","B) The ability to inspect assemblies, modules, types, and metadata at runtime, and dynamically invoke methods or create instances","C) Automatic UI mirror imaging for right-to-left languages","D) Backing up database transaction logs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Reflection (System.Reflection) provides programmatic access to metadata, enabling runtime type discovery, dynamic object instantiation, and member invocation."},{"id":167,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"How do you obtain a Type object for a known class 'Person' at compile time versus for an instance 'p' at runtime?","options":["A) typeof(Person) and p.GetType()","B) Person.Type and p.Type","C) Type.For(Person) and p.TypeOf()","D) Reflect(Person) and Reflect(p)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'typeof(Person)' is evaluated at compile time on a known type name. 'p.GetType()' is an instance method inherited from System.Object evaluated at runtime."},{"id":168,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"Which class in System.Reflection is used to dynamically create an instance of a type at runtime?","options":["A) DynamicFactory","B) Activator.CreateInstance()","C) InstanceCreator","D) TypeBuilder"],"answer":"B","explanation":"System.Activator.CreateInstance() dynamically creates an instance of a specified Type, invoking the appropriate constructor with matching arguments."},{"id":169,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What base class must all custom attributes inherit from in C#?","options":["A) System.Attribute","B) System.Metadata","C) System.Annotation","D) System.Decorator"],"answer":"A","explanation":"All custom attributes in .NET must directly or indirectly derive from System.Attribute. By convention, their class names end with the 'Attribute' suffix."},{"id":170,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What attribute is applied to a custom attribute class to define where it can be used (e.g. classes, methods, properties)?","options":["A) [TargetElements]","B) [AttributeUsage]","C) [ApplyTo]","D) [MetaDataScope]"],"answer":"B","explanation":"[AttributeUsage] specifies the program elements (via AttributeTargets flags enum) on which the attribute is valid, as well as whether multiple instances or inheritance are permitted."},{"id":171,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What format does a .NET assembly version number adhere to?","options":["A) Major.Minor.Build.Revision","B) Year.Month.Day.Patch","C) Release.Candidate.Beta.Alpha","D) Generation.Epoch.Iteration.Tag"],"answer":"A","explanation":".NET assembly versions are 4-part numbers defined as: MajorVersion.MinorVersion.BuildNumber.RevisionNumber (e.g., 2.0.483.1)."},{"id":172,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What constitutes a 'Strong-Named' assembly in .NET?","options":["A) An assembly compiled with 64-bit optimizations","B) An assembly signed with a cryptographic key pair, consisting of its simple text name, version number, culture, and a public key token","C) An assembly that is password-protected on disk","D) An assembly stored in the Windows System32 directory"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A strong name consists of the assembly's identity—its simple text name, version number, and culture information—strengthened by a public key and a digital signature generated using a private key."},{"id":173,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What tool is used from the Visual Studio Developer Command Prompt to generate key pairs for strong-naming assemblies?","options":["A) sn.exe","B) sign.exe","C) gacutil.exe","D) ildasm.exe"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Strong Name tool (sn.exe) is used to create cryptographic key pairs (e.g., 'sn.exe -k keypair.snk') and manage signatures for strong-named assemblies."},{"id":174,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What is the Global Assembly Cache (GAC)?","options":["A) A central machine-wide cache where strong-named assemblies are registered to be shared by multiple applications","B) A browser cache for web pages","C) The L2 cache of the central processor","D) A cloud storage bucket in Azure"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Global Assembly Cache (GAC) is a machine-wide CLI code cache where designated strong-named assemblies are installed so they can be shared among multiple applications."},{"id":175,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"Which method on a Type object retrieves custom attributes applied to that type?","options":["A) type.GetCustomAttributes()","B) type.FetchAnnotations()","C) type.ReadDecorators()","D) type.InspectAttributes()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Type.GetCustomAttributes() (or Attribute.GetCustomAttributes()) queries and instantiates custom attributes applied to an assembly, type, or member."},{"module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"Which method of System.Reflection.Assembly returns the assembly that contains the code currently executing?","options":["A) Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly()","B) Assembly.GetCallingAssembly()","C) Assembly.GetEntryAssembly()","D) Assembly.LoadCurrent()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly() returns the Assembly object representing the assembly that contains the currently executing method.","id":176,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"Which BindingFlags combination is required to reflectively discover private instance members of a class?","options":["A) BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance","B) BindingFlags.Public | BindingFlags.Static","C) BindingFlags.PrivateOnly","D) BindingFlags.All"],"answer":"A","explanation":"To retrieve private instance fields or methods using reflection, you must specify both BindingFlags.NonPublic and BindingFlags.Instance.","id":177,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What attribute allows internal types and members of an assembly to be visible and accessible to another designated assembly (such as a unit test project)?","options":["A) [InternalsVisibleTo(\"TestProject\")]","B) [ExposeInternals(\"TestProject\")]","C) [AllowAssembly(\"TestProject\")]","D) [PublicTo(\"TestProject\")]"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleToAttribute grants a specified friend assembly access to internal types that are otherwise invisible outside the assembly.","id":178,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What is 'Delay Signing' of a .NET assembly?","options":["A) Reserving space in the PE header for the digital signature and embedding the public key at build time, while postponing private key signing until release","B) Signing an assembly after it has been deployed to client machines","C) Signing an assembly with an expired certificate","D) Encrypting an assembly with a timer"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Delay signing embeds the public key and reserves signature space during everyday development, allowing developers to build and test without exposing the company's secure private key.","id":179,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 12: Reusable Types & Assemblies","question":"What is the difference between AssemblyVersion and AssemblyFileVersion?","options":["A) AssemblyVersion is used by the CLR for assembly binding, strong naming, and compatibility; AssemblyFileVersion is informational for the Windows OS file explorer","B) AssemblyVersion changes daily; AssemblyFileVersion never changes","C) AssemblyFileVersion is used by the CLR loader","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"AssemblyVersion is embedded in the assembly identity and checked by the CLR loader during type resolution. AssemblyFileVersion is purely metadata displayed in Windows file properties.","id":180,"subject":"C#"},{"id":181,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What is the key characteristic of symmetric encryption?","options":["A) It uses two different keys: a public key to encrypt and a private key to decrypt","B) It uses the same shared secret key for both encryption and decryption","C) It cannot be decrypted once encrypted","D) It only works on ASCII text"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Symmetric encryption algorithms (such as AES and DES) use the exact same secret key for both encrypting plaintext and decrypting ciphertext."},{"id":182,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Which symmetric encryption algorithm is the modern industry standard recommended in .NET?","options":["A) DES","B) RC2","C) AES (Advanced Encryption Standard)","D) TripleDES"],"answer":"C","explanation":"AES (Advanced Encryption Standard), represented in .NET by Aes or AesManaged, is the worldwide cryptographic standard offering strong security with key sizes up to 256 bits."},{"id":183,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What is the purpose of an Initialization Vector (IV) in symmetric block cipher encryption?","options":["A) To ensure that identical plaintext blocks do not produce identical ciphertext blocks when encrypted with the same key","B) To compress the plaintext before encryption","C) To verify the digital signature","D) To store the password in plaintext"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An Initialization Vector (IV) provides entropy so that encrypting the same plaintext multiple times with the same key produces completely different ciphertext each time."},{"id":184,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What is asymmetric (public-key) encryption?","options":["A) An algorithm that uses one key for encryption and a different mathematically-related private key for decryption","B) An algorithm that only encrypts every second byte","C) An algorithm that does not use any keys","D) An algorithm restricted to local network traffic"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Asymmetric encryption uses a key pair: the public key is shared freely to encrypt data or verify signatures, while the private key is kept secret to decrypt data or generate signatures."},{"id":185,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Which class in System.Security.Cryptography is standard for implementing asymmetric encryption in .NET?","options":["A) AesManaged","B) RSACryptoServiceProvider","C) SHA256Managed","D) HMACSHA1"],"answer":"B","explanation":"RSACryptoServiceProvider (or RSA) implements the RSA public-key cryptographic algorithm for asymmetric encryption and digital signatures."},{"id":186,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What is a cryptographic hash function?","options":["A) A reversible function that compresses data into a smaller string","B) A one-way mathematical function that converts arbitrary data into a fixed-size digest, impossible to invert","C) A symmetric key distribution scheme","D) A mechanism to verify user UI clicks"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A cryptographic hash function (e.g. SHA-256) is a one-way deterministic algorithm that maps arbitrary data to a fixed-size byte array. It is computationally infeasible to reverse or find collisions."},{"id":187,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Why should passwords never be hashed without a 'salt'?","options":["A) Salt speeds up hashing calculations","B) Salt prevents precomputed dictionary and rainbow table attacks by adding unique random data to each password prior to hashing","C) Hashing fails without salt","D) Salt encrypts the password symmetrically"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A cryptographic salt is unique random data appended to a password before hashing. It prevents attackers from using precomputed lookup tables (rainbow tables) to reverse common passwords."},{"id":188,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Which stream class in System.Security.Cryptography links a data stream to cryptographic transformations?","options":["A) CryptoStream","B) SecureStream","C) CipherStream","D) HashStream"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CryptoStream defines a stream that links data streams to cryptographic transformations (encryptor or decryptor transforms created from ICryptoTransform)."},{"id":189,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What does a Digital Signature provide that simple hashing alone cannot provide?","options":["A) Faster data compression","B) Non-repudiation and sender authentication (proving who signed the message)","C) Symmetric key exchange","D) Automatic password recovery"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A digital signature hashes the data and encrypts the hash with the sender's private key. Anyone with the sender's public key can verify integrity AND confirm the sender's authenticity (non-repudiation)."},{"id":190,"subject":"C#","module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Which class in .NET represents an X.509 public key certificate?","options":["A) X509Certificate2","B) DigitalCertToken","C) PKICertificate","D) SecureCertObject"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2 represents an X.509 certificate in .NET, encapsulating the public key, issuer details, validity dates, and optionally private key."},{"module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Which class in System.Security.Cryptography is standard for deriving cryptographic keys and password hashes using the PBKDF2 algorithm?","options":["A) Rfc2898DeriveBytes","B) PasswordDeriveBytes","C) KeyDerivationService","D) ShaKeyGenerator"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Rfc2898DeriveBytes implements PBKDF2 (Password-Based Key Derivation Function 2), stretching passwords using HMAC-SHA-1/256 with repeated iterations and salt.","id":191,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"Why is Electronic Codebook (ECB) cipher mode considered insecure and strongly discouraged in symmetric encryption?","options":["A) Identical plaintext blocks are encrypted into identical ciphertext blocks, preserving visual patterns in data","B) It uses asymmetric keys","C) It cannot encrypt more than 64 bytes","D) It does not support AES"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ECB mode encrypts each block independently with the same key. Identical plaintext blocks produce identical ciphertext blocks, leaking structural patterns. Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) is preferred.","id":192,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What does an HMAC (Hash-based Message Authentication Code) guarantee?","options":["A) Both data integrity and authenticity using a shared secret key combined with a cryptographic hash function","B) Reversible symmetric data encryption","C) Automated SSL certificate renewal","D) User identity anonymity"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An HMAC (e.g. HMACSHA256) calculates a message digest using a secret key, verifying both that the data has not been altered (integrity) and was created by a party possessing the key (authenticity).","id":193,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What padding mode is the standard default for symmetric block ciphers in .NET?","options":["A) PaddingMode.PKCS7","B) PaddingMode.Zeros","C) PaddingMode.ANSIX923","D) PaddingMode.None"],"answer":"A","explanation":"PKCS7 is the standard default padding mode in .NET, padding incomplete data blocks with bytes whose value equals the number of padding bytes added.","id":194,"subject":"C#"},{"module":"Module 13: Encrypting and Decrypting Data","question":"What class in .NET builds and validates an X.509 certificate trust chain up to a trusted root Certificate Authority (CA)?","options":["A) X509Chain","B) CertificateValidator","C) TrustStoreManager","D) PKIChainBuilder"],"answer":"A","explanation":"System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Chain builds a certificate chain from an end-entity certificate up to the root authority, checking revocation lists (CRL) and trust status.","id":195,"subject":"C#"},{"id":196,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which primary file extension is used for the primary data file of a SQL Server database?","options":["A) .ndf","B) .ldf","C) .mdf","D) .bak"],"answer":"C","explanation":".mdf is the primary data file. .ndf is used for secondary data files, and .ldf is used for transaction log files."},{"id":197,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which system database serves as the template for all newly created user databases in a SQL Server instance?","options":["A) master","B) model","C) msdb","D) tempdb"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'model' database is used as the template whenever a new database is created. Any settings, tables, or permissions added to 'model' are automatically inherited by new databases."},{"id":198,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which system database is re-created from scratch every time the SQL Server service restarts?","options":["A) master","B) msdb","C) tempdb","D) model"],"answer":"C","explanation":"tempdb is wiped and reinitialized each time SQL Server restarts. It holds temporary user objects (#tables), internal work tables, and row version stores."},{"id":199,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which system database stores SQL Server Agent alerts, scheduled jobs, operator definitions, and backup history?","options":["A) master","B) msdb","C) Resource","D) tempdb"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'msdb' database is used by the SQL Server Agent service to store alert definitions, scheduled automation jobs, mail profiles, and backup/restore history."},{"id":200,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which SQL Server edition includes all features of Enterprise Edition but is licensed exclusively for development and testing purposes?","options":["A) Standard Edition","B) Express Edition","C) Developer Edition","D) Web Edition"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Developer Edition contains the full Enterprise feature set but is free of charge strictly for non-production development, testing, and building applications."},{"id":201,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"What is the primary administrative GUI tool used for querying, configuring, and managing SQL Server instances?","options":["A) SQL Server Configuration Manager","B) SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)","C) SQL Server Profiler","D) Database Engine Tuning Advisor"],"answer":"B","explanation":"SSMS (SQL Server Management Studio) is the primary integrated environment for managing SQL infrastructure, authoring queries, and administering databases."},{"id":202,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"What is an 'instance' of SQL Server?","options":["A) A single table inside a database","B) An independent installation of the SQL Server database engine service running on a machine","C) A cached query execution plan","D) A single user transaction"],"answer":"B","explanation":"An instance of SQL Server is a complete, independent installation of the Database Engine service with its own set of system and user databases, memory allocation, and security credentials."},{"id":203,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which system database records all system-level configuration information, logins, endpoints, and the locations of all other databases?","options":["A) master","B) msdb","C) model","D) tempdb"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'master' database records all system-level information for an instance, including server configurations, logins, and pointers to all other database files."},{"id":204,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"What does SSMS Object Explorer display?","options":["A) A hierarchical tree view of all server objects, databases, security logins, and server objects in an instance","B) A flat text log of queries executed by users","C) The physical hard drive sectors on the host","D) The Windows operating system registry"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Object Explorer in SSMS provides an expandable tree view of all database engine objects, tables, views, stored procedures, security logins, and server settings."},{"id":205,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"What is the maximum database size supported by SQL Server Express Edition?","options":["A) 2 GB","B) 4 GB","C) 10 GB","D) 50 GB"],"answer":"C","explanation":"SQL Server Express Edition imposes a hard maximum limit of 10 GB per relational database (excluding log files)."},{"module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which database recovery model logs all transactions fully to allow point-in-time restore to a specific second?","options":["A) Full Recovery Model","B) Simple Recovery Model","C) Bulk-Logged Recovery Model","D) Read-Only Model"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Full Recovery Model logs every transaction fully and keeps log records until a transaction log backup occurs, enabling point-in-time restore to any specific second.","id":206,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"What is a Virtual Log File (VLF) in SQL Server transaction log architecture?","options":["A) Smaller contiguous segments into which the physical transaction log file (.ldf) is internally divided by SQL Server","B) A virtual disk mapped from cloud storage","C) An in-memory temporary database table","D) A log file stored in tempdb"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SQL Server internally divides each transaction log file into smaller units called Virtual Log Files (VLFs). Excessive VLFs caused by frequent small autogrowth increments can degrade transaction performance.","id":207,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"What is the purpose of a Database Checkpoint in SQL Server?","options":["A) Flushes dirty data pages from the buffer cache in memory to disk and records the checkpoint in the transaction log to minimize recovery time","B) Checks the database for syntax errors","C) Verifies user passwords","D) Creates an automatic full database backup"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A checkpoint writes all in-memory modified ('dirty') pages to physical data files (.mdf/.ndf), establishing a known good point from which recovery must start in a crash.","id":208,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Why should tempdb data files be configured with multiple equally sized data files matching the physical CPU core count (up to 8)?","options":["A) To mitigate internal page allocation contention (PFS and SGAM page latch contention)","B) To double database storage capacity","C) To enforce data encryption","D) To enable automatic replication"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Configuring multiple equally-sized data files for tempdb reduces allocation contention on Page Free Space (PFS) and Shared Global Allocation Map (SGAM) tracking pages.","id":209,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 1: Introduction to Microsoft SQL Server","question":"Which feature in SSMS allows you to view the graphical query execution plan chosen by the query optimizer?","options":["A) Display Estimated / Include Actual Execution Plan (Ctrl+M / Ctrl+L)","B) SQL Profiler Trace","C) Activity Monitor Grid","D) Database Engine Advisor"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In SSMS, 'Display Estimated Execution Plan' (Ctrl+L) and 'Include Actual Execution Plan' (Ctrl+M) render graphical representations of the query execution operators chosen by the cost-based optimizer.","id":210,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":211,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What is the correct logical query processing order for SQL queries?","options":["A) SELECT -> FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> ORDER BY","B) FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> ORDER BY","C) FROM -> SELECT -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> ORDER BY","D) WHERE -> FROM -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> ORDER BY"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Logically, SQL Server processes clauses in this order: 1. FROM, 2. WHERE, 3. GROUP BY, 4. HAVING, 5. SELECT, 6. ORDER BY. This is why column aliases defined in SELECT cannot be referenced in WHERE or GROUP BY."},{"id":212,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"Why does the following query fail with an invalid column name error?\nSELECT UnitPrice * Quantity AS TotalCost FROM Orders WHERE TotalCost > 100;","options":["A) You cannot multiply columns in T-SQL","B) The WHERE clause is evaluated before the SELECT clause, so the alias 'TotalCost' does not exist yet","C) The ORDER BY clause is missing","D) AS is not a valid keyword for aliases"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Because the WHERE clause executes before the SELECT clause during logical query processing, aliases defined in the SELECT list are not yet available in the WHERE clause."},{"id":213,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What logic system does T-SQL predicate evaluation use when dealing with NULL values?","options":["A) Two-valued logic (TRUE, FALSE)","B) Three-valued logic (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN)","C) Fuzzy logic","D) Four-valued logic (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN, EMPTY)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"SQL uses three-valued predicate logic. Comparisons involving NULL evaluate to UNKNOWN. A WHERE clause only accepts rows where the predicate evaluates to TRUE, discarding FALSE and UNKNOWN."},{"id":214,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What is an ERD in database design?","options":["A) Entity-Relationship Diagram","B) Error Resolution Document","C) Extended Relational Database","D) Execution Resource Directory"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD) is a visual data model showing entities (tables), attributes (columns), and relationships (foreign key cardinalities) within a database."},{"id":215,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"In relational theory, what is a primary key?","options":["A) Any column with an integer data type","B) A column or set of columns that uniquely identifies each row in a table and cannot contain NULL values","C) The first column defined in a CREATE TABLE statement","D) An index that speeds up string lookups"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A primary key constraint uniquely identifies every tuple (row) in a relation and enforces entity integrity by prohibiting duplicate and NULL values."},{"id":216,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What is a foreign key?","options":["A) A key imported from an external MySQL server","B) A column or combination of columns in one table whose values match the primary or unique key of another table","C) An encrypted column for foreign currency transactions","D) A column that is always NULL"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A foreign key enforces referential integrity by requiring values in a child table's column to exist in the referenced parent table's primary or unique key."},{"id":217,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What is the mathematical foundation of the Relational Model introduced by Dr. E.F. Codd?","options":["A) Graph theory and linear algebra","B) Set theory and first-order predicate logic","C) Object-oriented programming","D) Turing machines"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The relational model is grounded mathematically in set theory (treating data as unordered sets of distinct elements) and predicate logic."},{"id":218,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What does the expression (NULL = NULL) evaluate to under standard ANSI SQL?","options":["A) TRUE","B) FALSE","C) UNKNOWN","D) NULL"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Under ANSI SQL (with ANSI_NULLS ON), NULL represents an unknown value. Comparing two unknown values yields UNKNOWN. To check for NULL, use 'IS NULL'."},{"id":219,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"Why is a table in a relational database inherently unordered?","options":["A) Because disk drives spin randomly","B) Because mathematically a relation is a set of tuples, and mathematical sets have no intrinsic ordering","C) SQL Server sorts all tables in descending order by default","D) Ordering is only allowed on views"],"answer":"B","explanation":"According to set theory, elements in a set have no defined order. Unless an explicit ORDER BY clause is specified in the query, SQL Server guarantees no specific row order in results."},{"id":220,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"Which clause is processed LAST in logical query processing?","options":["A) SELECT","B) FROM","C) ORDER BY","D) HAVING"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The ORDER BY clause is the final step in logical query processing, responsible for sorting the presentation output returned to the client application."},{"module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What does SARGable stand for in SQL query optimization?","options":["A) Search Argument Able (predicates that allow the query engine to efficiently use an index seek)","B) Structured Access Relational Gateway","C) System Allocated Record Group","D) Sequential Array Row Generator"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A SARGable (Search Argument Able) query uses predicates (such as 'col >= value') that enable the query optimizer to perform an index seek rather than an expensive full index or table scan.","id":221,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"Why is the predicate 'WHERE YEAR(OrderDate) = 2024' non-SARGable?","options":["A) Wrapping the indexed column inside a scalar function prevents the optimizer from using an index seek, forcing a full scan","B) The YEAR function is not supported in T-SQL","C) 2024 is an integer literal","D) It only works on Mondays"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Applying functions to columns in WHERE predicates prevents index seeks. Rewriting as 'WHERE OrderDate >= '2024-01-01' AND OrderDate < '2025-01-01'' makes it SARGable.","id":222,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"In logical query processing, what is the key difference between the ON clause and the WHERE clause in an OUTER JOIN?","options":["A) The ON clause determines matching for joining and preserves unmatched rows from the outer table; the WHERE clause filters rows AFTER the join, discarding unmatched preserved rows if conditions fail","B) ON is only for numbers; WHERE is for strings","C) WHERE runs before ON","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In an outer join, the ON clause specifies which rows match. The outer table preserves unmatched rows with NULLs. The WHERE clause filters after preservation, potentially eliminating outer rows.","id":223,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What is the result of evaluating 'NOT (UNKNOWN)' in three-valued predicate logic?","options":["A) UNKNOWN","B) TRUE","C) FALSE","D) NULL"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In three-valued logic, the negation of UNKNOWN remains UNKNOWN: NOT(UNKNOWN) = UNKNOWN. Because only TRUE satisfies WHERE filters, both are filtered out.","id":224,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 2: Introduction to T-SQL Querying","question":"What is the primary difference between a Relation in relational theory and a Table in SQL Server?","options":["A) A relation cannot have duplicate tuples and has no column ordering; an unconstrained SQL table can contain duplicate rows and physical column order","B) A table cannot have foreign keys","C) Relations cannot store text","D) They are mathematically identical in all aspects"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Relational theory mandates distinct tuples (sets) and unordered attributes. SQL tables can hold duplicate rows (unless constrained by PK/Unique) and enforce physical column positions.","id":225,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":226,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What keyword is used in the SELECT clause to eliminate duplicate rows from the output?","options":["A) UNIQUE","B) DISTINCT","C) SEPARATE","D) GROUP"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The DISTINCT keyword filters out duplicate rows from the query results so that only unique rows are returned."},{"id":227,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What is the recommended ANSI standard syntax for assigning an alias to a column in a SELECT query?","options":["A) SELECT col AS AliasName FROM Table;","B) SELECT AliasName = col FROM Table;","C) SELECT col : AliasName FROM Table;","D) SELECT col -> AliasName FROM Table;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The ANSI SQL standard syntax is 'column_name AS alias_name'. (While 'alias = col' and 'col alias' work in T-SQL, 'AS' is standard and most readable)."},{"id":228,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What happens when you concatenate a string with a NULL value using the '+' operator when CONCAT_NULL_YIELDS_NULL is ON?","options":["A) The NULL is treated as an empty string ''","B) The entire resulting expression evaluates to NULL","C) A runtime error is thrown","D) It returns the string 'NULL'"],"answer":"B","explanation":"By default (CONCAT_NULL_YIELDS_NULL ON), 'Hello' + NULL yields NULL. In contrast, the built-in CONCAT() function treats NULL arguments as empty strings."},{"id":229,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What does the CONCAT('SQL', NULL, 'Server') function return in T-SQL?","options":["A) NULL","B) 'SQLServer'","C) 'SQL NULL Server'","D) Error"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The CONCAT function introduced in SQL Server 2012 automatically converts NULL arguments into empty strings, producing 'SQLServer'."},{"id":230,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What type of CASE expression is shown below?\nCASE CategoryID WHEN 1 THEN 'Beverage' WHEN 2 THEN 'Condiment' ELSE 'Other' END","options":["A) Searched CASE expression","B) Simple CASE expression","C) Coalesce expression","D) Predicate CASE expression"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A Simple CASE compares a single expression (CategoryID) against multiple scalar values (WHEN 1 THEN ...). A Searched CASE uses boolean predicates (WHEN Price > 10 THEN ...)."},{"id":231,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What does a Searched CASE expression allow that a Simple CASE expression does not?","options":["A) It allows different boolean comparison operators (<, >, BETWEEN, LIKE, IS NULL) in each WHEN clause","B) It runs faster by compiling to native C code","C) It can only return integer values","D) It can modify database data directly"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Searched CASE expressions evaluate distinct boolean conditions in each WHEN clause (e.g. WHEN Salary > 50000 THEN 'High'), providing far greater flexibility than simple equality checks."},{"id":232,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"How do you test if a column 'Region' contains a NULL value in a WHERE clause?","options":["A) WHERE Region = NULL","B) WHERE Region IS NULL","C) WHERE Region == NULL","D) WHERE ISNULL(Region)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Because NULL represents an unknown value, 'Region = NULL' evaluates to UNKNOWN and matches no rows. The predicate 'IS NULL' must be used."},{"id":233,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What is the result if no ELSE clause is specified in a CASE expression and none of the WHEN conditions match?","options":["A) 0","B) An empty string ''","C) NULL","D) A runtime exception is thrown"],"answer":"C","explanation":"If no ELSE branch is provided and no WHEN conditions evaluate to true, the CASE expression defaults to returning NULL."},{"id":234,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"Which of the following characters requires quoting or bracket delimiters when used inside a column alias?","options":["A) Underscore (_)","B) Letters (A-Z)","C) Space (e.g., [Order Total])","D) Numbers (0-9) not at the beginning"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Identifiers containing spaces, special characters, or reserved keywords must be enclosed in brackets like '[Order Total]' or double quotes to be valid identifiers."},{"id":235,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"Why is 'SELECT *' considered bad practice in production queries?","options":["A) It transfers unnecessary network bandwidth, prevents index-only covering scans, and breaks code if schema columns change","B) It causes an automatic table lock that halts all users","C) It cannot be used with a WHERE clause","D) It is deprecated and removed in modern SQL Server"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SELECT * incurs extra I/O and network overhead, prevents the query optimizer from utilizing covering non-clustered indexes, and can break application bindings when columns are added or reordered."},{"module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"Why does the query 'SELECT DISTINCT City FROM Customers ORDER BY PostalCode;' fail with an error?","options":["A) When DISTINCT is present, ORDER BY expressions must appear in the SELECT list to avoid ambiguous ordering of merged duplicate rows","B) DISTINCT cannot be used with ORDER BY","C) PostalCode is not an integer","D) DISTINCT requires two columns"],"answer":"A","explanation":"If multiple rows share the same City but have different PostalCodes, SQL Server cannot determine which PostalCode to order by. When DISTINCT is specified, ORDER BY items must be in the SELECT list.","id":236,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"How do you perform a case-sensitive string comparison in a query on a database configured with case-insensitive collation?","options":["A) Use the COLLATE clause: WHERE LastName COLLATE Latin1_General_CS_AS = 'Smith'","B) Use the UPPER() function on both sides","C) Add the CASE_SENSITIVE hint","D) Wrap the string in NCHAR"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The COLLATE clause overrides default collation at the expression level. Specifying a '_CS_AS' (Case-Sensitive, Accent-Sensitive) collation enforces case-sensitive comparisons.","id":237,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"What does 'SELECT TOP (10) PERCENT * FROM Orders ORDER BY OrderDate DESC;' return if the table contains 45 rows?","options":["A) 5 rows (ceiling of 4.5)","B) 4 rows","C) 10 rows","D) 45 rows"],"answer":"A","explanation":"TOP (N) PERCENT rounds up fractional row counts to the next whole integer: 10% of 45 is 4.5, which rounds up to 5 rows.","id":238,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"Can CASE expressions be nested inside other CASE expressions?","options":["A) Yes, CASE expressions can be nested up to 10 levels deep","B) No, nesting CASE is a syntax error in T-SQL","C) Only inside stored procedures","D) Only in SELECT, not in WHERE"],"answer":"A","explanation":"T-SQL allows nesting CASE expressions inside THEN or ELSE clauses up to a maximum nesting level of 10.","id":239,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 3: Writing SELECT Queries","question":"Which setting causes string concatenation with '+' and NULL to yield NULL?","options":["A) SET CONCAT_NULL_YIELDS_NULL ON;","B) SET ANSI_NULLS OFF;","C) SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON;","D) SET ARITHABORT ON;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"When CONCAT_NULL_YIELDS_NULL is ON (the ANSI standard default), concatenating a NULL value with a string produces NULL. If OFF, NULL is treated as an empty string.","id":240,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":241,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What type of join returns ONLY rows where there is a match in both joined tables based on the join predicate?","options":["A) LEFT OUTER JOIN","B) FULL OUTER JOIN","C) INNER JOIN","D) CROSS JOIN"],"answer":"C","explanation":"INNER JOIN evaluates the ON predicate and retains only the rows where the predicate is TRUE in both tables, discarding non-matching rows."},{"id":242,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"If Table A has 5 rows and Table B has 10 rows, how many rows will a CROSS JOIN between Table A and Table B produce?","options":["A) 15 rows","B) 50 rows","C) 10 rows","D) 5 rows"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A CROSS JOIN produces the Cartesian product of two tables. Every row of Table A is paired with every row of Table B: 5 * 10 = 50 rows."},{"id":243,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What does a LEFT OUTER JOIN between Customers (left) and Orders (right) return?","options":["A) Only customers who have placed orders","B) All customers, matching order details where available, and NULLs in order columns for customers with no orders","C) Only orders that have no matching customer","D) An error if any customer has no orders"],"answer":"B","explanation":"LEFT OUTER JOIN preserves all rows from the left table (Customers). For rows with no match in the right table (Orders), NULL values are supplied for all right-table columns."},{"id":244,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"How do you find customers who have NEVER placed an order using an outer join?","options":["A) SELECT c.* FROM Customers c LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID WHERE o.OrderID IS NULL;","B) SELECT c.* FROM Customers c INNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID WHERE o.OrderID = 0;","C) SELECT c.* FROM Customers c RIGHT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID;","D) SELECT c.* FROM Customers c CROSS JOIN Orders o WHERE c.CustomerID <> o.CustomerID;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Using a LEFT JOIN to Orders and filtering in the WHERE clause for 'o.OrderID IS NULL' selects all preserved customer rows that had no matching order rows."},{"id":245,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What is a 'Self-Join'?","options":["A) Joining a table to itself by giving it two different aliases","B) A join performed automatically by the query optimizer without an ON clause","C) A join between a table and a temporary table with the same name","D) A join that updates its own primary key"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A self-join joins a table to itself using distinct aliases (e.g. Employees e JOIN Employees m ON e.ManagerID = m.EmployeeID), often used to model hierarchical data."},{"id":246,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What type of join returns all rows from both tables, filling in NULLs wherever a match does not exist on either side?","options":["A) CROSS JOIN","B) FULL OUTER JOIN","C) INNER JOIN","D) NATURAL JOIN"],"answer":"B","explanation":"FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both the left and right tables, producing NULLs for missing sides."},{"id":247,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What is the consequence of placing a filter on the right table in the WHERE clause rather than in the ON clause of a LEFT OUTER JOIN?","options":["A) The query runs faster with no change in results","B) The outer join is effectively converted into an inner join because NULL rows from unmatched left records are eliminated by the WHERE condition","C) SQL Server raises a syntax error","D) All right table rows are duplicated"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Filtering the right table in the WHERE clause (e.g., WHERE o.OrderDate >= '2024-01-01') discards rows where o.OrderDate is NULL, effectively converting the outer join into an inner join."},{"id":248,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"When joining 3 tables together in a single query, how many JOIN operators and ON clauses are required?","options":["A) 1 JOIN, 1 ON clause","B) 2 JOINs, 2 ON clauses","C) 3 JOINs, 1 ON clause","D) 3 JOINs, 3 ON clauses"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Each binary join operation links two tables (or the intermediate result of a previous join and a new table). Joining N tables requires (N - 1) JOIN operators and ON clauses."},{"id":249,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"Why must table aliases be used when referencing columns with identical names across joined tables?","options":["A) To avoid ambiguous column name errors","B) To force table indexing","C) To format column output","D) To encrypt the column names"],"answer":"A","explanation":"When multiple joined tables contain columns with identical names (such as CustomerID), qualifying them with table names or aliases (e.g. c.CustomerID) prevents 'Ambiguous column name' compiler errors."},{"id":250,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"Can an ON clause contain multiple conditions connected with AND / OR operators?","options":["A) No, ON can only contain a single equality comparison","B) Yes, the ON clause can contain complex boolean expressions including multiple conditions, ranges, and inequalities","C) Only if using CROSS JOIN","D) Only in SQL Server Enterprise Edition"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The ON clause takes any valid boolean predicate. You can combine multiple equality checks and inequality conditions using AND, OR, and NOT."},{"module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What is a 'Non-Equi Join' in T-SQL?","options":["A) A join that uses comparison operators other than equality (=), such as <, >, <=, >=, or BETWEEN in the ON clause","B) A join that produces no rows","C) A join between tables on different servers","D) A join that ignores indexes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A non-equi join matches rows based on inequality operators (e.g. e.Salary BETWEEN g.LowSalary AND g.HighSalary) rather than strict equality.","id":251,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"How can two rows with NULL values in their join keys be matched together in an INNER JOIN?","options":["A) ON a.KeyCol = b.KeyCol OR (a.KeyCol IS NULL AND b.KeyCol IS NULL)","B) Standard '=' automatically matches NULL to NULL in joins","C) Set ANSI_NULLS to OFF in the query","D) Joins cannot match NULL values under any circumstances"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Because NULL = NULL evaluates to UNKNOWN, matching NULL rows in a join requires explicitly adding 'OR (a.KeyCol IS NULL AND b.KeyCol IS NULL)' or using ISNULL()/COALESCE().","id":252,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"Does the syntactic order of tables in multiple INNER JOINs dictate the physical order of execution chosen by the query optimizer?","options":["A) No, inner joins are associative and commutative; the cost-based optimizer reorders them to find the most efficient execution plan","B) Yes, tables are always joined strictly from left to right as written","C) Yes, unless FORCE ORDER is specified","D) Inner joins can only process 2 tables at a time"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Because inner joins are logically commutative and associative, the query optimizer evaluates different join orders and access paths, selecting the lowest-cost plan regardless of written order.","id":253,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What is the equivalent formulation of 'TableA RIGHT OUTER JOIN TableB ON ...' using a LEFT OUTER JOIN?","options":["A) TableB LEFT OUTER JOIN TableA ON ...","B) TableA LEFT OUTER JOIN TableB ON ...","C) TableA FULL OUTER JOIN TableB ON ...","D) TableB CROSS JOIN TableA"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A RIGHT OUTER JOIN is the exact mirror image of a LEFT OUTER JOIN with the tables reversed: 'B LEFT JOIN A' preserves all rows from B, identical to 'A RIGHT JOIN B'.","id":254,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 4: Querying Multiple Tables with JOINs","question":"What does CROSS JOIN with a derived VALUES table constructor allow you to do without a physical auxiliary table?","options":["A) Generate multiple virtual rows on the fly to multiply or unpivot rows (e.g. CROSS JOIN (VALUES (1), (2), (3)) AS v(num))","B) Delete duplicate rows","C) Back up table data","D) Create permanent clustered indexes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CROSS JOIN (VALUES ('A'), ('B')) AS t(col) generates virtual rows on the fly, commonly used for unpivoting data or expanding rows without temporary tables.","id":255,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":256,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What is the default sort direction when ORDER BY is specified without ASC or DESC?","options":["A) DESC (descending)","B) ASC (ascending)","C) Random","D) Order of insertion"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In SQL Server, ascending order (ASC) is the default sort direction if neither ASC nor DESC is explicitly specified."},{"id":257,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What wildcard character in the LIKE operator matches any string of zero or more characters?","options":["A) _ (underscore)","B) % (percent)","C) * (asterisk)","D) ? (question mark)"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In T-SQL LIKE pattern matching, '%' matches zero or more characters, while '_' matches exactly one single character."},{"id":258,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What wildcard in the LIKE operator matches any single character within a specified range or set?","options":["A) [a-z]","B) (a-z)","C) {a-z}","D) %a-z%"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Square brackets '[a-z]' match any single character within the specified character set or range (e.g., '[0-9]' or '[A-F]')."},{"id":259,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What does the following predicate match?\nWHERE Phone LIKE '[^0-9]%'","options":["A) Phone numbers starting with a digit 0 through 9","B) Phone numbers starting with any character other than a digit 0 through 9","C) Phone numbers containing exactly 9 digits","D) Phone numbers ending in zero"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The caret '^' inside brackets negates the set, so '[^0-9]%' matches any string whose first character is NOT a numeric digit."},{"id":260,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What does the predicate 'WHERE Price BETWEEN 10 AND 20' evaluate to?","options":["A) Price > 10 AND Price < 20 (exclusive)","B) Price >= 10 AND Price <= 20 (inclusive)","C) Price = 10 OR Price = 20","D) Price = 15"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The BETWEEN operator in T-SQL is inclusive of both boundary values, equivalent to 'Price >= 10 AND Price <= 20'."},{"id":261,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"In operator precedence, which logical operator is evaluated first when parentheses are absent?","options":["A) OR","B) AND","C) NOT","D) XOR"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Logical operator precedence is: 1. NOT, 2. AND, 3. OR. Parentheses should always be used to ensure correct grouping and clarity."},{"id":262,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What is the purpose of the WITH TIES option when used with TOP (N) in an ORDER BY query?","options":["A) It combines duplicate rows into one","B) It returns additional rows that have the same sort values as the Nth row","C) It encrypts the top rows","D) It forces the query to use an index"],"answer":"B","explanation":"TOP (N) WITH TIES returns additional rows if they share identical values in the ORDER BY columns with the last row in the top-N cut, preventing arbitrary tie-breaking."},{"id":263,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"Which T-SQL paging clause skips the first 20 rows and returns the next 10 rows?","options":["A) LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20","B) SKIP 20 TAKE 10","C) ORDER BY Col OFFSET 20 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY","D) TOP (10) FROM ROW 20"],"answer":"C","explanation":"SQL Server 2012+ introduced standard OFFSET-FETCH: 'ORDER BY Column OFFSET 20 ROWS FETCH NEXT 10 ROWS ONLY'. An ORDER BY clause is strictly mandatory."},{"id":264,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"How do you search for an actual literal percent sign ('%') in a column using LIKE?","options":["A) WHERE Discount LIKE '%%'","B) WHERE Discount LIKE '!%' ESCAPE '!'","C) WHERE Discount LIKE '\\%'","D) Percent signs cannot be searched in LIKE"],"answer":"B","explanation":"You can define an escape character using the ESCAPE clause: LIKE '!%' ESCAPE '!' treats the percent sign following '!' as a literal character rather than a wildcard."},{"id":265,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"Where do NULL values sort by default in an ORDER BY ASC query in SQL Server?","options":["A) At the very end (after all values)","B) At the very beginning (before all values)","C) They are omitted from results","D) In arbitrary positions"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In SQL Server, NULL values are treated as the lowest possible values and appear first when sorted in ASC order, and last when sorted in DESC order."},{"module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What is the difference between an Index Seek and an Index Scan?","options":["A) Index Seek navigates the B-Tree directly to find specific qualifying rows using SARGable predicates; Index Scan traverses all leaves of the index from start to end","B) Index Scan is always faster than Index Seek","C) Index Seek requires a clustered index; Index Scan is for heaps","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An Index Seek traverses the B-Tree root and branch pages directly to locate matching leaf rows efficiently. An Index Scan touches every leaf page in the index, acting like a table scan.","id":266,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What does the query 'SELECT * FROM Products ORDER BY UnitPrice OFFSET 10 ROWS;' do?","options":["A) Skips the first 10 rows and returns all remaining rows from row 11 to the end","B) Returns only the first 10 rows","C) Raises a syntax error because FETCH NEXT is required","D) Deletes 10 rows"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In T-SQL, the FETCH NEXT clause is optional with OFFSET. Specifying 'OFFSET 10 ROWS' skips the first 10 rows and returns all remaining rows.","id":267,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"What is 'Sort Stability' in SQL Server ORDER BY queries?","options":["A) If ORDER BY values contain ties, the relative order of tied rows is non-deterministic and can vary between query runs unless tied columns are uniquely sorted","B) Sorts are always guaranteed to be identical across runs","C) Sorted queries cannot be aborted","D) Stability refers to disk page locks"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SQL Server sorting is not stable for tied values. If multiple rows have the same ORDER BY value, their order is arbitrary unless unique tie-breaking columns are added.","id":268,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"How do you search for strings containing square brackets '[]' in a LIKE query?","options":["A) Escape the bracket: WHERE Code LIKE '![%]!' ESCAPE '!' or WHERE Code LIKE '[[]%'","B) Brackets cannot be matched in LIKE","C) WHERE Code LIKE '[]'","D) WHERE Code LIKE '\\['"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Because square brackets are wildcard characters in LIKE, a literal bracket must either be enclosed in brackets ('[[]') or escaped via an ESCAPE character.","id":269,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 5: Sorting & Filtering Data","question":"Which predicate correctly finds all customers whose CompanyName starts with any letter between 'A' and 'C'?","options":["A) WHERE CompanyName LIKE '[A-C]%'","B) WHERE CompanyName LIKE 'A-C%'","C) WHERE CompanyName = 'A' OR 'B' OR 'C'","D) WHERE CompanyName BETWEEN 'A%' AND 'C%'"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'[A-C]%' uses range matching inside square brackets to match any string whose first character is A, B, or C.","id":270,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":271,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What is the key difference between CHAR(10) and VARCHAR(10)?","options":["A) CHAR is variable-length; VARCHAR is fixed-length","B) CHAR is fixed-length (pads unused spaces up to 10 bytes); VARCHAR is variable-length (stores only actual characters plus 2 bytes overhead)","C) CHAR stores Unicode; VARCHAR stores ASCII","D) CHAR supports up to 2 GB of text"],"answer":"B","explanation":"CHAR(n) is fixed-length and right-pads with spaces to exactly 'n' bytes. VARCHAR(n) is variable-length, storing only the actual string length plus 2 bytes of length tracking."},{"id":272,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"Why should you use NVARCHAR instead of VARCHAR?","options":["A) To store Unicode characters (such as Arabic, Chinese, emojis) requiring 2 bytes per character","B) Because NVARCHAR takes half the storage space of VARCHAR","C) Because NVARCHAR supports mathematical calculations directly","D) Because VARCHAR is deprecated in SQL Server 2016"],"answer":"A","explanation":"NVARCHAR stores UTF-16 Unicode data (prefix N'string'), supporting international multi-lingual characters, whereas VARCHAR stores 1-byte code-page characters."},{"id":273,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"In the data type DECIMAL(8, 2), what do the numbers 8 and 2 represent?","options":["A) 8 decimal places and 2 integer digits","B) Precision of 8 (total digits) and Scale of 2 (digits to the right of the decimal point)","C) Minimum 8 bytes and maximum 2 bytes","D) 8 rows and 2 columns"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Precision (p = 8) is the total number of digits stored (both left and right of decimal). Scale (s = 2) is the number of digits to the right of the decimal point."},{"id":274,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"Which date/time data type includes timezone offset awareness (e.g. +03:00)?","options":["A) DATETIME","B) DATETIME2","C) DATETIMEOFFSET","D) TIME"],"answer":"C","explanation":"DATETIMEOFFSET includes the date, time of day (fractional seconds precision), and an offset from UTC (Coordinated Universal Time), making it timezone-aware."},{"id":275,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What storage size does the INT data type occupy in SQL Server?","options":["A) 1 byte","B) 2 bytes","C) 4 bytes","D) 8 bytes"],"answer":"C","explanation":"INT occupies 4 bytes (storing values from -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647). TINYINT is 1 byte, SMALLINT is 2 bytes, and BIGINT is 8 bytes."},{"id":276,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"Why should FLOAT and REAL be avoided for financial and monetary calculations?","options":["A) They are approximate numeric data types that introduce rounding discrepancies due to binary floating-point representation","B) They cannot store negative numbers","C) They only store integers","D) They are limited to values below $1,000"],"answer":"A","explanation":"FLOAT and REAL are approximate numeric types. For financial and accounting applications, exact numeric types like DECIMAL/NUMERIC or MONEY must be used to prevent rounding errors."},{"id":277,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What data type is used to store Globally Unique Identifiers (GUIDs) in SQL Server?","options":["A) UNIQUEIDENTIFIER","B) UUID","C) GUID_TYPE","D) VARBINARY(16)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UNIQUEIDENTIFIER is a 16-byte binary value initialized typically via the NEWID() or NEWSEQUENTIALID() functions to create globally unique values across systems."},{"id":278,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What literal prefix must precede a string literal to designate it as Unicode in T-SQL?","options":["A) U'text'","B) N'text'","C) E'text'","D) B'text'"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The uppercase letter 'N' prefix (e.g. N'مرحبا' or N'John') stands for National Language and informs SQL Server to treat the string as Unicode (NVARCHAR)."},{"id":279,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What does the (MAX) specifier mean in VARCHAR(MAX) or VARBINARY(MAX)?","options":["A) Maximum length is 8,000 bytes","B) Maximum length is up to 2 gigabytes (2^31 - 1 bytes) stored as a Large Object (LOB)","C) It automatically sizes to the largest record in the table","D) It can only hold 65,535 characters"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The (MAX) specifier replaces older TEXT/IMAGE types, allowing columns to store up to 2 GB of variable-length character, Unicode, or binary data."},{"id":280,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What happens during 'Implicit Conversion' when an INT column is compared with a VARCHAR string containing digits in a WHERE clause?","options":["A) SQL Server automatically converts the VARCHAR to INT according to data type precedence","B) SQL Server converts the INT to VARCHAR","C) The query fails immediately with a syntax error","D) The comparison always returns UNKNOWN"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SQL Server uses a defined Data Type Precedence hierarchy. INT has higher precedence than VARCHAR, so the VARCHAR value is implicitly converted to INT."},{"module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What is the numeric storage range of the TINYINT data type in SQL Server?","options":["A) 0 to 255 (1 byte unsigned)","B) -128 to 127","C) 0 to 65,535","D) -32,768 to 32,767"],"answer":"A","explanation":"TINYINT is an unsigned 1-byte integer storing whole numbers from 0 to 255. It cannot store negative numbers.","id":281,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What is the maximum fractional second precision supported by the DATETIME2 data type?","options":["A) 7 decimal places (100 nanoseconds precision: DATETIME2(7))","B) 3 decimal places (milliseconds: 3.33ms)","C) 0 decimal places","D) 9 decimal places"],"answer":"A","explanation":"DATETIME2 supports configurable precision from 0 to 7 digits, where DATETIME2(7) tracks fractional seconds down to 100 nanoseconds (0.0000001s).","id":282,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"Why is SMALLDATETIME limited compared to modern date types?","options":["A) It only stores date and time to the minute (seconds are always :00) and its date range is limited from 1900-01-01 to 2079-06-06","B) It cannot store dates after the year 2000","C) It occupies 16 bytes","D) It only works on 32-bit systems"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SMALLDATETIME occupies 4 bytes, has a range of 1900-01-01 through 2079-06-06, and rounds seconds to the nearest whole minute.","id":283,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"When should CHAR(2) be chosen over VARCHAR(2) for a CountryCode column?","options":["A) When data is always a fixed length; CHAR(2) uses exactly 2 bytes without the 2-byte length-offset tracking overhead of VARCHAR","B) Because CHAR supports Unicode","C) Because VARCHAR cannot store 2 characters","D) Only when indexing is disabled"],"answer":"A","explanation":"For strictly fixed-length codes (like 2-letter ISO country codes), CHAR(2) uses 2 bytes, whereas VARCHAR(2) incurs 2 bytes of data plus 2 bytes of offset array metadata in the row header.","id":284,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 6: SQL Server Data Types","question":"What is the SQL_VARIANT data type in SQL Server?","options":["A) A special data type that can store values of various SQL Server data types (except text, ntext, image, varchar(max), and xml)","B) A pointer to a C# object","C) An encrypted variant of INT","D) A JSON data type"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SQL_VARIANT stores values of almost any SQL Server base data type alongside internal metadata recording the base type and precision.","id":285,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":286,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"Which statement is used to add new rows of data to a database table?","options":["A) ADD ROW","B) INSERT INTO","C) APPEND","D) UPDATE"],"answer":"B","explanation":"INSERT INTO is the standard DML statement used to insert one or more rows into a table or view."},{"id":287,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"What is the key functional difference between DELETE and TRUNCATE TABLE?","options":["A) DELETE is DML, can have a WHERE clause, and logs individual row deletions; TRUNCATE is DDL, deallocates entire pages with minimal logging, and cannot have a WHERE clause","B) TRUNCATE fires AFTER DELETE triggers on the table","C) DELETE resets the IDENTITY seed, while TRUNCATE preserves it","D) TRUNCATE can be used on tables referenced by foreign keys"],"answer":"A","explanation":"TRUNCATE deallocates data pages with minimal logging, resets the IDENTITY seed, and does not fire DELETE triggers. It cannot have a WHERE clause and cannot be run on tables referenced by active foreign keys."},{"id":288,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"Which function returns the last identity value generated in the current session AND within the current execution scope?","options":["A) @@IDENTITY","B) SCOPE_IDENTITY()","C) IDENT_CURRENT('tablename')","D) IDENT_SEED()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"SCOPE_IDENTITY() returns the last identity value generated within the current session and current scope (protecting against values generated by triggers). @@IDENTITY returns the last value in the session regardless of scope."},{"id":289,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"What happens if you execute an UPDATE statement without a WHERE clause?","options":["A) Only the first row is updated","B) Every row in the table is updated with the new values","C) SQL Server raises an error demanding confirmation","D) No rows are updated"],"answer":"B","explanation":"An UPDATE statement without a WHERE clause applies the SET modifications to every single row in the target table."},{"id":290,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"What does the OUTPUT clause in an INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE statement do?","options":["A) Prints debug messages to the Windows Event Log","B) Returns information from modified rows using the 'INSERTED' and 'DELETED' virtual tables","C) Exports data directly to an Excel file","D) Formats numbers with currency symbols"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The OUTPUT clause returns data from the rows affected by DML statements via the special 'INSERTED' (new values) and 'DELETED' (old values) memory tables."},{"id":291,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"Which statement combines INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations into a single atomic operation based on matching criteria with a source table?","options":["A) UPSERT","B) MERGE","C) COMBINE","D) SYNC"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The MERGE statement performs conditional INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations against a target table in a single atomic statement based on WHEN MATCHED and WHEN NOT MATCHED clauses."},{"id":292,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"What must terminate a MERGE statement in T-SQL?","options":["A) A GO command","B) A semicolon (;)","C) END MERGE","D) COMMIT"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In T-SQL, the MERGE statement must strictly terminate with a semicolon (;) or a syntax error is raised."},{"id":293,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"How do you explicitly insert an explicit value into a column that has the IDENTITY property enabled?","options":["A) SET IDENTITY_INSERT TableName ON;","B) ALTER TABLE TableName DISABLE IDENTITY;","C) UPDATE TableName SET ID = value;","D) It is physically impossible to insert explicit values into identity columns"],"answer":"A","explanation":"To supply explicit values for an IDENTITY column, execute 'SET IDENTITY_INSERT TableName ON' before the INSERT statement, and turn it OFF afterward."},{"id":294,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"Which syntax copies data and creates a new permanent table on the fly based on the result set of a query?","options":["A) INSERT INTO NewTable SELECT * FROM OldTable;","B) SELECT * INTO NewTable FROM OldTable;","C) CREATE TABLE AS SELECT * FROM OldTable;","D) COPY TABLE OldTable TO NewTable;"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'SELECT ... INTO NewTable FROM ...' creates the destination table based on the schema and column types of the query result set and copies the data into it."},{"id":295,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"In an UPDATE statement, which virtual table in the OUTPUT clause contains the values as they were BEFORE the update?","options":["A) INSERTED","B) DELETED","C) PRIOR","D) OLD"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In an UPDATE operation, the 'DELETED' table holds the pre-update row values, and the 'INSERTED' table holds the post-update values."},{"module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"In a MERGE statement, what does the '$action' column in the OUTPUT clause return?","options":["A) An NVARCHAR(10) string indicating the action performed on each row: 'INSERT', 'UPDATE', or 'DELETE'","B) The execution duration in milliseconds","C) The transaction ID","D) The user login name"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In a MERGE statement's OUTPUT clause, '$action' is a system virtual column returning 'INSERT', 'UPDATE', or 'DELETE' indicating which operation affected that specific row.","id":296,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"How can you capture rows returned from a stored procedure directly into an existing table?","options":["A) INSERT INTO TargetTable EXEC StoredProcName;","B) SELECT * FROM EXEC(StoredProcName) INTO TargetTable;","C) TargetTable.Load(StoredProcName);","D) IMPORT StoredProcName INTO TargetTable;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'INSERT INTO TargetTable EXEC ProcedureName' captures the tabular output of a stored procedure directly into a pre-existing target table with matching columns.","id":297,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"How do you update a table based on data joined from another table in T-SQL?","options":["A) UPDATE t SET t.Price = s.NewPrice FROM TargetTable t INNER JOIN SourceTable s ON t.ID = s.ID;","B) UPDATE TargetTable JOIN SourceTable ON ... SET Price = NewPrice;","C) UPDATE TargetTable SET Price = (SELECT NewPrice);","D) MERGE is the only way to update from another table"],"answer":"A","explanation":"T-SQL supports updating via an explicit FROM clause: 'UPDATE t SET t.Col = s.Col FROM Target t JOIN Source s ON t.ID = s.ID'.","id":298,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"How can you delete a fixed number of rows from a table in batches to avoid locking escalation?","options":["A) DELETE TOP (5000) FROM Orders WHERE Status = 'Archived';","B) TRUNCATE TOP (5000) TABLE Orders;","C) DELETE FROM Orders LIMIT 5000;","D) DROP 5000 ROWS FROM Orders;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'DELETE TOP (N) FROM Table WHERE ...' removes rows in chunks, allowing developers to execute batch deletions in a loop to avoid full-table lock escalation and log truncation bottlenecks.","id":299,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 7: Using DML to Modify Data","question":"Why cannot TRUNCATE TABLE be executed on a table that is referenced by a FOREIGN KEY constraint, even if the child table has 0 rows?","options":["A) SQL Server validates schema metadata: any table referenced by an active foreign key is prohibited from truncation regardless of whether child rows exist","B) Because foreign keys require clustered indexes","C) Because TRUNCATE only works on views","D) Because TRUNCATE deletes foreign keys permanently"],"answer":"A","explanation":"TRUNCATE does not scan individual rows to verify referential integrity; it deallocates pages directly. Therefore, it is blocked if any FOREIGN KEY constraint references the table.","id":300,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":301,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What is the difference between a deterministic function and a non-deterministic function?","options":["A) Deterministic functions always return the same result given the same input parameters; non-deterministic functions (like GETDATE()) can return different results on each call","B) Deterministic functions can only be run once per day","C) Non-deterministic functions cannot be used in stored procedures","D) Deterministic functions do not take arguments"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Deterministic functions (e.g. SQUARE, ABS) always return the identical result given the same input parameters. Non-deterministic functions (e.g. GETDATE(), NEWID()) return different values each time."},{"id":302,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What is the advantage of TRY_CONVERT or TRY_CAST over standard CONVERT or CAST?","options":["A) They run twice as fast","B) If the conversion fails, they return NULL instead of terminating the query with a runtime error","C) They support XML serialization","D) They automatically decrypt data"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Standard CAST and CONVERT fail with runtime conversion errors on invalid data. TRY_CAST and TRY_CONVERT return NULL if the conversion is invalid, allowing queries to continue gracefully."},{"id":303,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does COALESCE('arg1', 'arg2', 'arg3') return?","options":["A) The concatenation of all arguments","B) The first non-NULL expression among its arguments","C) The count of non-null arguments","D) NULL"],"answer":"B","explanation":"COALESCE is an ANSI SQL standard function that evaluates the arguments in order and returns the current value of the first expression that does not evaluate to NULL."},{"id":304,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"How does ISNULL(check_expression, replacement_value) differ from COALESCE?","options":["A) ISNULL is proprietary to T-SQL and accepts only 2 arguments, adopting the data type of the first argument; COALESCE is ANSI standard, accepts multiple arguments, and returns the highest precedence data type","B) ISNULL works only with strings","C) COALESCE cannot handle NULLs","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ISNULL is T-SQL specific, accepts exactly 2 arguments, and determines result type from the first argument. COALESCE is ANSI standard, accepts N arguments, and promotes to the highest precedence type."},{"id":305,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does the function DATEDIFF(day, '2024-01-01', '2024-01-10') return?","options":["A) 10","B) 9","C) 8","D) -9"],"answer":"B","explanation":"DATEDIFF calculates the count of specified datepart boundaries crossed between startdate and enddate (10 - 1 = 9 days)."},{"id":306,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"Which function returns the last day of the month containing the specified date?","options":["A) LAST_DAY()","B) EOMONTH()","C) MONTH_END()","D) ENDOFMONTH()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"EOMONTH(start_date [, month_to_add]) returns the last day of the month containing the specified date as a DATE data type."},{"id":307,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does NULLIF(val1, val2) return if val1 is equal to val2?","options":["A) TRUE","B) 0","C) NULL","D) val1"],"answer":"C","explanation":"NULLIF returns NULL if its two arguments are equal; otherwise, it returns the first argument. It is commonly used to prevent division by zero: val / NULLIF(divisor, 0)."},{"id":308,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does the IIF function in T-SQL do?","options":["A) Serves as a shorthand for writing a CASE expression: IIF(condition, true_value, false_value)","B) Declares an index in an inline function","C) Validates if an IP address is valid","D) Compares two tables for row identity"],"answer":"A","explanation":"IIF is a shorthand syntactic wrapper around a simple boolean CASE expression: IIF(boolean_expression, true_value, false_value)."},{"id":309,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"Which string function extracts 5 characters from 'Microsoft' starting at position 6?","options":["A) SUBSTRING('Microsoft', 6, 5)","B) MID('Microsoft', 6, 5)","C) SUBSTR('Microsoft', 5, 5)","D) LEFT('Microsoft', 5)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SUBSTRING(expression, start, length) uses 1-based indexing. SUBSTRING('Microsoft', 6, 5) starts at the 6th character ('s') and takes 5 characters ('soft')."},{"id":310,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does the CHOOSE function do in T-SQL?","options":["A) Returns the item at the specified 1-based index from an ordered list of values: CHOOSE(2, 'A', 'B', 'C') returns 'B'","B) Randomly selects a row from a table","C) Selects the best index for a query","D) Picks the smallest number from a list"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CHOOSE(index, val_1, val_2, ... val_n) acts like an array indexer in T-SQL, returning the value at the 1-based index position."},{"module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does the STRING_SPLIT function introduced in SQL Server 2016 return?","options":["A) A table with a single column named 'value' containing substrings split by the specified delimiter character","B) An array in memory","C) A comma-separated string","D) An XML document"],"answer":"A","explanation":"STRING_SPLIT(string, separator) is an inline table-valued function that parses a delimited string and returns rows in a single column named 'value'.","id":311,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"Why should the FORMAT() function be used cautiously in large query result sets?","options":["A) It relies on the .NET CLR runtime internally and introduces significant CPU performance overhead compared to CONVERT()","B) It drops database connections","C) It truncates dates to 1900","D) It only formats currency"],"answer":"A","explanation":"FORMAT() is implemented via the .NET Common Language Runtime, resulting in high CPU overhead and context switching. For large queries, CONVERT() with style codes is significantly faster.","id":312,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What does the optional third argument in ROUND(123.456, 1, 1) do?","options":["A) When non-zero, it truncates the number instead of rounding, returning 123.400","B) Rounds to the nearest hundred","C) Converts the result to integer","D) Divides the result by 10"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In ROUND(numeric_expression, length [, function]), if 'function' is non-zero, SQL Server truncates the expression to the specified decimal places rather than rounding.","id":313,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What is the difference between CEILING(4.2) and FLOOR(4.8)?","options":["A) CEILING(4.2) returns 5; FLOOR(4.8) returns 4","B) CEILING(4.2) returns 4; FLOOR(4.8) returns 5","C) Both return 5","D) Both return 4"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CEILING returns the smallest integer greater than or equal to the numeric expression (5). FLOOR returns the largest integer less than or equal to the numeric expression (4).","id":314,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 8: Using Built-In Functions","question":"What is the difference between GETDATE() and GETUTCDATE()?","options":["A) GETDATE() returns the current date/time of the host operating system; GETUTCDATE() returns the current Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)","B) GETUTCDATE() includes timezone offset","C) GETDATE() returns date only; GETUTCDATE() returns time only","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GETDATE() returns the local datetime of the database server host. GETUTCDATE() returns the current datetime in UTC (GMT).","id":315,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":316,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What rule governs the SELECT list when a query contains a GROUP BY clause?","options":["A) All columns in the SELECT list must either be listed in the GROUP BY clause or enclosed within an aggregate function","B) The SELECT list cannot contain any columns other than the primary key","C) Aggregate functions cannot be used in the SELECT list","D) The GROUP BY clause must match the ORDER BY clause exactly"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Every column appearing in the SELECT list that is not encapsulated within an aggregate function (COUNT, SUM, AVG, etc.) must be explicitly included in the GROUP BY clause."},{"id":317,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What is the key difference between the WHERE clause and the HAVING clause?","options":["A) WHERE filters individual rows before grouping; HAVING filters aggregated groups after grouping","B) HAVING can only be used with numbers","C) WHERE is evaluated after GROUP BY","D) HAVING can modify the data"],"answer":"A","explanation":"WHERE filters rows before they enter the grouping phase. HAVING filters the resulting aggregated groups and can evaluate aggregate functions (e.g. HAVING COUNT(*) > 5)."},{"id":318,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"How do standard aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX) handle NULL values?","options":["A) They treat NULLs as zeros","B) They ignore NULL values completely when computing the result","C) They return NULL if any single row is NULL","D) They throw a runtime exception"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Aggregate functions (with the exception of COUNT(*)) eliminate NULL values before calculating their results. (A warning 'Null value is eliminated by an aggregate or other SET operation' is emitted)."},{"id":319,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What is the difference between COUNT(*) and COUNT(column_name)?","options":["A) COUNT(*) counts all rows including those with NULLs; COUNT(column_name) counts only rows where column_name is NOT NULL","B) COUNT(*) only counts indexed columns","C) COUNT(column_name) counts duplicates only once","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"COUNT(*) counts every row in the group regardless of contents. COUNT(column_name) counts only rows where the specified column contains a non-NULL value."},{"id":320,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"How do you count the number of distinct cities represented in a Customers table?","options":["A) COUNT(DISTINCT City)","B) DISTINCT(COUNT(City))","C) COUNT(UNIQUE City)","D) SUM(DISTINCT City)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Placing the DISTINCT keyword inside the aggregate function: COUNT(DISTINCT City) de-duplicates the city values before counting."},{"id":321,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What will AVG(Score) return if the table contains four rows with Scores: 10, 20, 30, and NULL?","options":["A) 15 (60 / 4)","B) 20 (60 / 3)","C) NULL","D) 60"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Because aggregate functions ignore NULLs, the NULL row is omitted from both the sum and the count: (10 + 20 + 30) / 3 = 60 / 3 = 20."},{"id":322,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"Can an aggregate function be used directly inside a WHERE clause without a subquery (e.g. WHERE COUNT(*) > 5)?","options":["A) Yes, if GROUP BY is also present","B) No, aggregate functions cannot appear in a WHERE clause because WHERE executes before grouping occurs","C) Yes, in SQL Server 2016 and later","D) Only for MIN and MAX"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Aggregates summarize groups of rows, which do not exist when the WHERE clause executes. Aggregate conditions must be placed in the HAVING clause or a subquery."},{"id":323,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"How does GROUP BY handle rows where the grouping column is NULL?","options":["A) Discards all NULL rows","B) Places all NULL rows together into a single group","C) Creates a separate group for each individual NULL row","D) Converts NULLs to 0"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In T-SQL grouping, all NULL values are considered equal for grouping purposes and are collected into a single combined group."},{"id":324,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What does the query 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders HAVING COUNT(*) > 100;' return if there are only 50 orders in the table?","options":["A) 0","B) NULL","C) An empty result set (0 rows)","D) 50"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The query aggregates all rows into a single group with COUNT(*) = 50. The HAVING clause evaluates 50 > 100 as FALSE, filtering out the group and returning an empty result set."},{"id":325,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"Which aggregate function returns the population standard deviation for all values in a numeric expression?","options":["A) STDEV","B) STDEVP","C) VAR","D) VARP"],"answer":"B","explanation":"STDEVP computes the standard deviation for the entire population. STDEV computes the sample standard deviation."},{"module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What is the performance difference between COUNT(1) and COUNT(*) in SQL Server?","options":["A) None; the query optimizer parses them identically into the exact same execution plan","B) COUNT(1) is twice as fast because it does not read columns","C) COUNT(*) requires reading every column off disk","D) COUNT(1) ignores NULLs"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In SQL Server, COUNT(*) and COUNT(1) are functionally and performance-wise completely identical. The optimizer treats both as counting rows.","id":326,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"Which aggregate function concatenates string values from multiple rows into a single delimited string?","options":["A) STRING_AGG(column, delimiter) [WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ...)]","B) CONCAT_ROWS()","C) GROUP_CONCAT()","D) TEXT_JOIN()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"STRING_AGG (introduced in SQL Server 2017) aggregates string expressions across rows into a delimited string with optional WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY) ordering.","id":327,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"Can a query contain a HAVING clause without an explicit GROUP BY clause?","options":["A) Yes; the entire result set is treated as a single group","B) No; HAVING strictly requires GROUP BY or a syntax error is raised","C) Only if WHERE is omitted","D) Only in views"],"answer":"A","explanation":"If HAVING is used without GROUP BY, the entire table or filtered result set is treated as a single aggregate group (e.g. 'SELECT 1 HAVING COUNT(*) > 10').","id":328,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"What do MIN() and MAX() return when applied to a character/string column?","options":["A) The lowest and highest alphabetical strings based on collation sort rules","B) The string with shortest and longest length","C) An error because MIN and MAX only work on numbers","D) NULL"],"answer":"A","explanation":"MIN and MAX evaluate character columns using collation sort order, returning the string that sorts first (lowest) and last (highest) alphabetically.","id":329,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 9: Grouping & Aggregating Data","question":"Can you group by a complex expression (such as YEAR(OrderDate)) in a GROUP BY clause?","options":["A) Yes, expressions can appear in GROUP BY, and identical expressions can be selected in the SELECT list","B) No, GROUP BY only accepts raw column names","C) Only if defined in a CTE first","D) Only in SQL Server Enterprise"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GROUP BY allows expressions (e.g. 'GROUP BY YEAR(OrderDate), MONTH(OrderDate)'). The exact same expression can be projected in the SELECT list.","id":330,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":331,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What is a Scalar Subquery?","options":["A) A subquery that returns a single column and multiple rows","B) A subquery that returns exactly one single value (one row and one column)","C) A subquery that returns an entire table","D) A subquery that cannot contain a WHERE clause"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A scalar subquery returns exactly one row with one column. Because it evaluates to a single scalar value, it can be used anywhere a single expression is valid."},{"id":332,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What happens at runtime if a scalar subquery used in an equality comparison returns multiple rows?","options":["A) SQL Server picks the first row automatically","B) A runtime error is raised: 'Subquery returned more than 1 value'","C) The comparison converts to an IN clause automatically","D) It evaluates to NULL"],"answer":"B","explanation":"When an operator expecting a single value (=, <, >) receives multiple rows from a subquery, SQL Server terminates execution with runtime error 512."},{"id":333,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What is a Correlated Subquery?","options":["A) A subquery that runs once before the outer query and caches its results","B) A subquery that references columns from the outer query, executing once for each row processed by the outer query","C) A subquery that joins two unrelated databases","D) A subquery that does not return any rows"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A correlated subquery references one or more columns from the outer query table, creating a dependency where the subquery evaluates in the context of each candidate outer row."},{"id":334,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What does the EXISTS operator return?","options":["A) The row count of the subquery","B) A boolean TRUE if the subquery returns at least one row; FALSE otherwise","C) The primary key of the matching row","D) The elapsed time of the query"],"answer":"B","explanation":"EXISTS tests for the presence of any rows matching the subquery. It short-circuits evaluation as soon as the first matching record is found, returning TRUE."},{"id":335,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"Why is 'SELECT *' inside an EXISTS subquery (e.g. WHERE EXISTS (SELECT * FROM ...)) not a performance hazard?","options":["A) The query optimizer ignores the SELECT list in an EXISTS subquery and only checks for row existence","B) SQL Server only reads the first column anyway","C) EXISTS only works on clustered indexes","D) It is a performance hazard and should never be done"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The query optimizer does not project or transfer columns specified in the SELECT list of an EXISTS predicate; it solely verifies row existence."},{"id":336,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What unexpected result occurs when using 'WHERE ID NOT IN (SELECT ID FROM Table)' if the subquery returns even a single NULL value?","options":["A) All matching rows are returned","B) The entire NOT IN predicate evaluates to UNKNOWN for all outer rows, returning an empty result set (0 rows)","C) The NULL is converted to 0","D) The database crashes"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Because 'val <> NULL' is UNKNOWN, if any value in the NOT IN set is NULL, the AND-chain evaluates to UNKNOWN for all rows, causing NOT IN to return zero rows. Using NOT EXISTS avoids this hazard."},{"id":337,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What does the '> ALL (subquery)' comparison require to evaluate to TRUE for a candidate row?","options":["A) The candidate value must be greater than at least one value returned by the subquery","B) The candidate value must be greater than every single value returned by the subquery (greater than MAX)","C) The subquery must return all rows from the table","D) The candidate value must be equal to the average"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The '> ALL' comparison requires the value to be greater than every value returned by the subquery, meaning it must be strictly greater than the maximum value."},{"id":338,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What does '> ANY (subquery)' or '> SOME (subquery)' mean?","options":["A) The value must be greater than at least one value returned by the subquery (greater than MIN)","B) The value must be greater than all values","C) The value must match the first value only","D) The subquery must return NULL"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'> ANY' (or '> SOME') evaluates to TRUE if the value is greater than at least one value in the subquery result set (i.e. greater than the minimum value)."},{"id":339,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"Where can subqueries be placed in a T-SQL query?","options":["A) Only in the WHERE clause","B) In SELECT, FROM, WHERE, and HAVING clauses","C) Only in the FROM clause","D) Only in the ORDER BY clause"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Subqueries can appear in multiple clauses: as scalar expressions in SELECT, derived tables in FROM, filters in WHERE, and group criteria in HAVING."},{"id":340,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What is a Self-Contained (or Non-Correlated) Subquery?","options":["A) A subquery that has no dependency on the outer query and can be executed independently","B) A subquery that references its own table name only","C) A subquery inside a stored procedure","D) A subquery that contains a TRANSACTION"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A self-contained subquery does not reference any columns from the outer query. It can be highlighted and executed completely on its own, running once for the entire query."},{"module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What is 'Subquery Unnesting' (or flattening) performed by the SQL Server query optimizer?","options":["A) An internal optimization where the optimizer transforms subqueries (like EXISTS or IN) into equivalent relational JOIN operators to explore better join algorithms","B) Deleting subqueries from memory","C) Converting correlated subqueries into stored procedures","D) Running subqueries on client machines"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Subquery unnesting is an optimization where the engine rewrites subqueries into relational joins (semi-joins or anti-semi-joins), allowing hash, merge, or loop join algorithms.","id":341,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"Where can a correlated subquery be used to return a dynamic calculated value for each row of the outer query?","options":["A) In the SELECT projection list","B) Only in the GROUP BY clause","C) Only in the ORDER BY clause","D) Correlated subqueries cannot be used in SELECT"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A scalar correlated subquery can appear in the SELECT list (e.g. 'SELECT c.Name, (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders o WHERE o.CustID = c.CustID) AS OrderCount FROM Customers c').","id":342,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"How can you return only the single most recent order date for each customer using a correlated subquery in SELECT?","options":["A) (SELECT TOP (1) o.OrderDate FROM Orders o WHERE o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID ORDER BY o.OrderDate DESC)","B) (SELECT o.OrderDate FROM Orders o WHERE o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID)","C) (SELECT FIRST(o.OrderDate) FROM Orders o)","D) Correlated subqueries cannot use ORDER BY"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A subquery in the SELECT list must be scalar (returning 1 value). Pairing 'TOP (1)' with an 'ORDER BY' ensures exactly one row is returned.","id":343,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"Why does EXISTS generally perform better than IN when testing existence against a subquery that could contain NULLs?","options":["A) EXISTS uses two-valued boolean logic and short-circuits on the first matching row, while NOT IN evaluates to UNKNOWN if any NULL is present","B) IN requires creating temporary tables on disk","C) EXISTS encrypts data","D) IN is deprecated"],"answer":"A","explanation":"EXISTS evaluates row existence directly using two-valued logic (True/False). NOT IN evaluates three-valued logic and collapses to 0 rows if a single NULL is returned.","id":344,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 10: Using Subqueries","question":"What is a Table Subquery?","options":["A) A subquery that returns multiple rows and multiple columns, typically used in the FROM clause as a derived table","B) A subquery that creates a physical table","C) A subquery that modifies table schema","D) A subquery inside a trigger"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A table subquery returns a multi-row, multi-column tabular result set, most commonly utilized in the FROM clause with an alias as a derived table.","id":345,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":346,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What is a View in SQL Server?","options":["A) A physical copy of data stored in a separate table","B) A named, saved SELECT query definition stored in the database catalog that acts as a virtual table","C) A graphical dashboard in SSMS","D) A temporary table in tempdb"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A view is a stored query definition. When queried, SQL Server expands the view definition and queries the underlying base tables directly (unless it is an indexed view)."},{"id":347,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What is a Common Table Expression (CTE)?","options":["A) A temporary named result set defined within the execution scope of a single SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE statement using the WITH keyword","B) A global temporary table shared by all users","C) A table stored in the master database","D) An index defined on multiple columns"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A CTE is a named temporary table expression defined using 'WITH CTE_Name AS (SELECT ...)' that exists only for the duration of the statement that follows it."},{"id":348,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What are the two distinct parts of a Recursive CTE?","options":["A) Primary member and Foreign member","B) Anchor member and Recursive member (combined with UNION ALL)","C) Master query and Detail query","D) Initial loop and Final loop"],"answer":"B","explanation":"A recursive CTE consists of an Anchor member (which returns the base rows), a UNION ALL operator, and a Recursive member that references the CTE itself until an empty set is produced."},{"id":349,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What is a Derived Table in T-SQL?","options":["A) A subquery defined in the FROM clause of an outer query and assigned a mandatory correlation alias","B) A table created by inheritance in C#","C) A table generated from a backup file","D) A partitioned table on SAN storage"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A derived table is an inline subquery in the FROM clause: 'FROM (SELECT col1, col2 FROM MyTable) AS dt'. Giving it an alias is mandatory."},{"id":350,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What is an Inline Table-Valued Function (iTVF)?","options":["A) A function that returns a table using a single SELECT statement without a BEGIN/END block, essentially acting as a parameterized view","B) A scalar function returning integer counts","C) A stored procedure returning output parameters","D) A trigger on a view"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An inline TVF contains a single RETURN SELECT statement. Because it has no multi-statement procedural logic, SQL Server can inline its definition directly into the calling query execution plan."},{"id":351,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"Why does an ORDER BY clause without TOP or OFFSET-FETCH cause an error inside a View or CTE definition?","options":["A) Because views and CTEs represent mathematical relations, and relations have no intrinsic ordering","B) Because sorting is not supported in T-SQL","C) Because views can only sort alphabetically","D) Because ORDER BY requires administrative permissions"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A view or table expression defines a relational set, which by definition is unordered. ORDER BY is disallowed in view definitions unless TOP or OFFSET-FETCH is used to restrict rows."},{"id":352,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What option on CREATE VIEW prevents modifying base table schemas in ways that would invalidate the view?","options":["A) WITH ENCRYPTION","B) WITH SCHEMABINDING","C) WITH CHECK OPTION","D) WITH RECOMPILE"],"answer":"B","explanation":"WITH SCHEMABINDING locks the underlying base tables against structural changes (altering or dropping columns/tables) that would break the view definition."},{"id":353,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What does the WITH CHECK OPTION clause do when added to an updatable View?","options":["A) It verifies table permissions for the current user","B) It ensures that any INSERT or UPDATE through the view conforms to the filter conditions defined in the view's WHERE clause","C) It encrypts the view definition","D) It checks database integrity daily"],"answer":"B","explanation":"WITH CHECK OPTION prevents DML modifications through the view that would result in rows disappearing from the view (i.e. failing the view's WHERE predicate)."},{"id":354,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What query hint controls the maximum number of recursion levels allowed for a recursive CTE to prevent infinite loops?","options":["A) OPTION (MAXRECURSION n)","B) OPTION (RECURSE_LIMIT n)","C) OPTION (LOOP_COUNT n)","D) OPTION (FAST n)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The MAXRECURSION hint (e.g., OPTION (MAXRECURSION 100)) sets the maximum recursion depth. If the recursion exceeds this number, an error is raised and the query aborts."},{"id":355,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"Can multiple CTEs be defined in a single WITH statement?","options":["A) Yes, by separating them with commas (e.g. WITH CTE1 AS (...), CTE2 AS (...))","B) No, each CTE requires its own separate WITH keyword","C) Only if they reference identical tables","D) Only up to 2 CTEs per query"],"answer":"A","explanation":"You can define multiple CTEs consecutively using a single WITH keyword by separating each CTE definition with a comma."},{"module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What is an Indexed View (or Materialized View) in SQL Server?","options":["A) A view on which a unique clustered index has been created, causing the view result set to be physically computed and stored on disk like a table","B) A view that has only non-clustered indexes","C) A view stored in the master database","D) An index on tempdb"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Creating a UNIQUE CLUSTERED INDEX on a view (with SCHEMABINDING) physically materializes the view data to disk, updated automatically as underlying tables change.","id":356,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What causes a Recursive CTE to terminate its recursion naturally?","options":["A) When the recursive member query returns an empty result set (0 rows)","B) When the MAXRECURSION limit is reached","C) When the anchor member completes","D) When the transaction commits"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A recursive CTE continues iterating until the recursive member returns an empty result set. If it never terminates, MAXRECURSION terminates it with an error.","id":357,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"Can a CTE reference another CTE defined previously in the same WITH clause?","options":["A) Yes, subsequent CTEs can reference preceding CTEs in the same WITH statement","B) No, CTEs cannot reference other CTEs","C) Only if separated by GO","D) Only in stored procedures"],"answer":"A","explanation":"You can chain CTEs: 'WITH CTE1 AS (...), CTE2 AS (SELECT * FROM CTE1) SELECT * FROM CTE2;' Preceding CTEs are accessible to subsequent ones.","id":358,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"What are the two syntactic methods for assigning column aliases to a Derived Table?","options":["A) Inline aliasing (SELECT col AS Alias) and External aliasing (FROM (...) AS dt(Alias1, Alias2))","B) Header aliasing and Footer aliasing","C) Static and Dynamic aliasing","D) There is only one method"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Derived tables allow inline aliasing in the SELECT list or external column aliasing in the correlation clause: 'FROM (SELECT c1, c2 FROM T) AS dt(colA, colB)'.","id":359,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 11: Using Table Expressions","question":"Why do Multi-Statement Table-Valued Functions (MSTVFs) often cause poor query performance compared to Inline TVFs?","options":["A) SQL Server historically uses fixed cardinality estimates (1 row in older versions, 100 in 2014) for MSTVFs, leading to suboptimal query execution plans","B) MSTVFs cannot be indexed","C) MSTVFs do not support parameters","D) MSTVFs lock the entire database"],"answer":"A","explanation":"MSTVFs use table variables whose cardinality cannot be accurately estimated during compilation, often resulting in bad plans. Inline TVFs are inlined directly like views.","id":360,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":361,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"What is the primary difference between UNION and UNION ALL?","options":["A) UNION eliminates duplicate rows from the combined result set; UNION ALL retains all rows including duplicates","B) UNION ALL is slower because it sorts rows","C) UNION allows different numbers of columns","D) UNION ALL only works on integer data types"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UNION performs a distinct sort operation to eliminate duplicate rows across sets. UNION ALL simply concatenates the sets without sorting or de-duplication, making it significantly faster."},{"id":362,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"What are the two mandatory requirements for queries combined by set operators (UNION, EXCEPT, INTERSECT)?","options":["A) They must have the same number of columns, and corresponding columns must have compatible data types","B) They must reference the same table","C) They must have identical column names and indexes","D) They must use the same WHERE clause"],"answer":"A","explanation":"All set operators require: 1. Both queries must return the exact same number of columns. 2. The data types of corresponding columns in order must be implicitly or explicitly compatible."},{"id":363,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"What does the INTERSECT operator return?","options":["A) Only rows that appear in both the first and second query result sets","B) Rows that appear in the first query but not in the second query","C) All rows from both queries with duplicates","D) A Cartesian cross-product"],"answer":"A","explanation":"INTERSECT returns only distinct rows that are present in both input query result sets."},{"id":364,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"What does the query 'QueryA EXCEPT QueryB' return?","options":["A) Rows that appear in QueryA but do NOT appear in QueryB","B) Rows that appear in QueryB but not QueryA","C) Rows that appear in either QueryA or QueryB but not both","D) All rows except NULLs"],"answer":"A","explanation":"EXCEPT returns distinct rows from the left (first) query that do not exist in the right (second) query result set."},{"id":365,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"How do set operators (UNION, EXCEPT, INTERSECT) treat NULL values when comparing rows?","options":["A) They treat two NULL values as equal to each other for de-duplication purposes","B) They treat comparisons with NULL as UNKNOWN and discard them","C) They convert NULLs to empty strings","D) They raise a syntax error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Unlike standard equality predicates in WHERE clauses, set operators treat two NULL values in corresponding columns as matching/equal for set comparison."},{"id":366,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"Where can an ORDER BY clause be placed when using set operators?","options":["A) In each individual query before the set operator","B) Only once at the very end of the entire statement to sort the combined result","C) Set operations cannot have an ORDER BY clause","D) Immediately after the first SELECT"],"answer":"B","explanation":"When using set operators, an ORDER BY clause can only appear once at the very end of the compound query, sorting the final combined result set."},{"id":367,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"Which column names are assigned to the output of a UNION query?","options":["A) The column names or aliases defined in the first query","B) The column names from the second query","C) System-generated column names like Col1, Col2","D) The user must define an alias on the UNION keyword"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The column names in the final result set of a set operation are determined solely by the column names or aliases specified in the first SELECT query."},{"id":368,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"What is the purpose of the CROSS APPLY operator in T-SQL?","options":["A) It invokes a table-valued function or correlated subquery for each row of an outer table, returning only rows where the inner expression produces results (like an INNER JOIN)","B) It creates a Cartesian product between two physical tables","C) It applies a mathematical matrix transform","D) It converts rows to JSON"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CROSS APPLY evaluates a right-side table-valued expression (such as a TVF or correlated subquery) for each row of the left table and returns matching combined rows, analogous to an INNER JOIN."},{"id":369,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"How does OUTER APPLY differ from CROSS APPLY?","options":["A) OUTER APPLY preserves all rows from the left table even if the right-side expression produces no rows (returning NULLs, like a LEFT OUTER JOIN)","B) OUTER APPLY only returns non-matching rows","C) OUTER APPLY cannot take arguments","D) OUTER APPLY runs in parallel"],"answer":"A","explanation":"OUTER APPLY behaves like a LEFT OUTER JOIN: it preserves left-side rows even when the right-side table expression returns an empty set, supplying NULLs for right-side columns."},{"id":370,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"Why does UNION ALL generally deliver higher performance than UNION?","options":["A) Because it bypasses the expensive sorting and hashing operations required to identify and eliminate duplicate rows","B) Because UNION ALL uses uncommitted read isolation","C) Because UNION ALL compresses the data","D) Because UNION ALL runs on the GPU"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UNION requires a sort or hash distinct operation in the execution plan to detect duplicate rows. UNION ALL omits this step, streaming rows directly."},{"module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"In standard T-SQL operator precedence, which set operator has HIGHER precedence when combining multiple queries without parentheses?","options":["A) INTERSECT has higher precedence than UNION and EXCEPT","B) UNION has higher precedence than INTERSECT","C) EXCEPT has higher precedence than all others","D) All set operators have identical precedence from left to right"],"answer":"A","explanation":"INTERSECT precedes UNION and EXCEPT in precedence. 'Q1 UNION Q2 INTERSECT Q3' evaluates 'Q2 INTERSECT Q3' first, then unions with Q1.","id":371,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"Do the corresponding column names in queries combined by UNION need to match?","options":["A) No, only the data types must be compatible; column names in the output are taken solely from the first query","B) Yes, column names must be identical character for character","C) Column names are replaced by numbers","D) Aliases cannot be used"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Column names do not need to match across queries in a set operation. The column names of the final result set are determined by the first SELECT query.","id":372,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"How can you pass columns from an outer table as arguments into an Inline Table-Valued Function?","options":["A) Using the CROSS APPLY or OUTER APPLY operator","B) Using an INNER JOIN with ON clause","C) Using a subquery in GROUP BY","D) Using UNION ALL"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Standard JOINs cannot pass left-table columns as parameters into right-side functions. The APPLY operator (CROSS/OUTER APPLY) evaluates the function per outer row with parameters.","id":373,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"What happens to rows from the left table when using CROSS APPLY if the right-side table expression returns zero rows?","options":["A) The left-table row is eliminated from the result set (like an INNER JOIN)","B) The left-table row is returned with NULLs","C) An error is raised","D) The query restarts"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CROSS APPLY drops left rows that produce 0 right rows. To preserve left rows with NULLs when the right side is empty, use OUTER APPLY.","id":374,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 12: Using Set Operators","question":"Why does UNION ALL not perform any sorting or duplicate removal?","options":["A) By design, UNION ALL is a pure concatenation operation that streams input sets directly into the result","B) Because UNION ALL only accepts sorted tables","C) Because UNION ALL runs in single-user mode","D) Because UNION ALL uses clustered indexes only"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UNION ALL concatenates streams without de-duplication, avoiding memory and CPU costs associated with Distinct Sort or Hash Aggregate operators.","id":375,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":376,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What clause is required to define a window for window functions in T-SQL?","options":["A) GROUP BY","B) OVER()","C) WINDOW()","D) PARTITION()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The OVER() clause defines the window or subset of rows over which the window function (ranking, offset, or aggregate) operates."},{"id":377,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What does the PARTITION BY subclause inside the OVER() clause do?","options":["A) Divides the query result set into independent partitions where the window function calculates independently for each partition","B) Physically divides the hard disk storage","C) Filters rows from the query","D) Sorts the overall query output"],"answer":"A","explanation":"PARTITION BY divides rows into groups or partitions. The window function calculates over each partition separately and resets for the next partition."},{"id":378,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What is the difference between RANK() and DENSE_RANK() when duplicate values occur in the order?","options":["A) RANK() skips subsequent rank numbers after ties (e.g. 1, 2, 2, 4); DENSE_RANK() does not skip numbers (e.g. 1, 2, 2, 3)","B) DENSE_RANK() skips numbers, whereas RANK() does not","C) RANK() returns decimal values","D) DENSE_RANK() only works with dates"],"answer":"A","explanation":"RANK() leaves gaps in ranking numbers after ties (1, 2, 2, 4). DENSE_RANK() assigns consecutive rankings without any gaps (1, 2, 2, 3)."},{"id":379,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"Which window function assigns a sequential unique integer starting at 1 to each row within a partition?","options":["A) ROW_NUMBER()","B) RANK()","C) NTILE()","D) DENSE_RANK()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ROW_NUMBER() returns an incrementing unique integer starting at 1 for each row within the partition according to the specified ORDER BY."},{"id":380,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What does NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY Salary DESC) do to a result set of 100 employees?","options":["A) Selects only the top 4 employees","B) Divides the 100 rows into 4 equal quartiles of 25 rows each, assigning bucket numbers 1 through 4","C) Multiplies salaries by 4","D) Skips every 4th row"],"answer":"B","explanation":"NTILE(n) distributes sorted rows across 'n' roughly equal groups (buckets), returning the bucket number (1, 2, 3, 4) for each row."},{"id":381,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"Which offset window function accesses data from a subsequent row in the same result set without using a self-join?","options":["A) LAG()","B) LEAD()","C) FIRST_VALUE()","D) NEXT_VALUE()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"LEAD(column, offset) accesses data from a subsequent row at a specified physical offset ahead of the current row. LAG() accesses data from a preceding row."},{"id":382,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What does the default framing clause 'ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW' compute when used with SUM(Amount) OVER (ORDER BY OrderDate)?","options":["A) A running cumulative total of Amount ordered by OrderDate","B) The total sum of the entire table on every row","C) Only the sum of the first row","D) The average amount"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW' aggregates from the very start of the partition up to the current row, producing a cumulative running total."},{"id":383,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"Can window functions be placed directly inside a WHERE or HAVING clause?","options":["A) Yes, if prefixed with OVER","B) No, window functions are evaluated after WHERE and HAVING; to filter on them, wrap the query in a CTE or derived table","C) Yes, in SQL Server 2016+","D) Only ROW_NUMBER() can be used in WHERE"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In logical query processing, window functions evaluate during the SELECT phase (after WHERE and HAVING). To filter on a window function value, place it in a CTE or derived table first."},{"id":384,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"Which window function returns the value of an expression from the very first row of the window frame?","options":["A) FIRST_VALUE()","B) TOP_VALUE()","C) HEAD()","D) LEAD_FIRST()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"FIRST_VALUE(expression) returns the value of the specified expression evaluated against the first row in the window frame."},{"id":385,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What is the key difference between the ROWS frame specification and the RANGE frame specification?","options":["A) ROWS specifies frame boundaries by exact physical row offsets; RANGE specifies frame boundaries by logical value differences","B) RANGE cannot use UNBOUNDED PRECEDING","C) ROWS only works with numbers","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ROWS specifies framing based on a physical count of rows (e.g. 1 PRECEDING). RANGE specifies framing based on logical value equality in the ordering column."},{"module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What common trap occurs when using LAST_VALUE(column) OVER (ORDER BY col) without an explicit ROWS framing clause?","options":["A) The default frame is 'RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', meaning LAST_VALUE returns the current row value rather than the last row in the partition","B) It throws a syntax error","C) It returns NULL always","D) It reverses sort order"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The default frame stops at CURRENT ROW. Therefore, LAST_VALUE() evaluates against the window up to the current row, returning the current row value. Adding 'ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING' fixes it.","id":386,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What does the CUME_DIST() window function calculate?","options":["A) The cumulative distribution of a value within a partition, calculating the relative position of a value as a number between 0 and 1","B) The distance between two GPS coordinates","C) The count of duplicate values","D) The geometric mean"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CUME_DIST() computes the relative rank of a row within a partition, defined as (number of rows with values <= current row) / (total rows in partition).","id":387,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"Can multiple window functions in the same SELECT query have different PARTITION BY and ORDER BY definitions?","options":["A) Yes, each window function can have its own independent OVER() clause specification","B) No, all window functions in a query must share the exact same OVER definition","C) Only if they use the same aggregation","D) Only up to 2 window functions per query"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Every window function specifies its own independent OVER() clause, allowing multiple ranking, offset, and aggregate windows to coexist in the same SELECT list.","id":388,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What does an empty OVER() clause (e.g. COUNT(*) OVER ()) compute?","options":["A) Aggregates over the entire query result set as a single window without collapsing rows","B) Returns 0","C) Causes a syntax error","D) Groups rows by primary key"],"answer":"A","explanation":"OVER() with empty parentheses treats the entire result set as a single partition with no specific order, returning the overall table aggregate alongside each detail row.","id":389,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 13: Window Functions","question":"What does the third argument in LAG(col, offset, default_value) specify?","options":["A) The replacement value to return if the offset falls outside the partition boundary instead of NULL","B) The maximum recursion depth","C) The column sorting order","D) The frame boundary"],"answer":"A","explanation":"LAG(column, offset, default_value) returns 'default_value' (e.g. 0) when the offset references a non-existent row before the partition start, avoiding NULLs.","id":390,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":391,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What does the T-SQL PIVOT relational operator do?","options":["A) Rotates unique values from rows into multiple columns while aggregating remaining column values","B) Rotates columns into rows","C) Sorts rows diagonally","D) Encrypts column headers"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The PIVOT operator rotates row-based data into column-based format, turning unique values from one column into multiple output columns with aggregations."},{"id":392,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What does the UNPIVOT operator do?","options":["A) Rotates columns of a table into rows","B) Deletes pivoted tables","C) Converts numeric columns to strings","D) Merges duplicate rows"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UNPIVOT is the reverse of PIVOT: it transforms column-oriented data into row-oriented pairs (attribute and value columns)."},{"id":393,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"Why should you use a derived table or CTE containing only the necessary columns as input to a PIVOT operator?","options":["A) Any column not specified in the aggregate or PIVOT IN clause is implicitly treated as a grouping column","B) PIVOT cannot accept physical table names","C) To avoid locking the entire database","D) To bypass syntax checking"],"answer":"A","explanation":"PIVOT implicitly groups by every column present in the input table expression that is not explicitly used as the pivoted column or the aggregated column."},{"id":394,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What does GROUP BY GROUPING SETS((Year, Month), (Year), ()) produce?","options":["A) Multiple distinct aggregations in a single query: subtotals by Year & Month, subtotals by Year, and a grand total across all rows","B) Three separate physical tables","C) An error because empty parentheses () are invalid","D) A cross join of Year and Month"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GROUPING SETS allows defining multiple group combinations in one query. () represents the empty grouping set (the grand total)."},{"id":395,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What grouping sets does 'GROUP BY ROLLUP(Year, Quarter, Month)' generate?","options":["A) Hierarchical aggregations: (Year, Quarter, Month), (Year, Quarter), (Year), and () grand total","B) All possible permutations of the three columns","C) Only the grand total ()","D) Only (Year, Quarter, Month)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ROLLUP creates hierarchical grouping sets, progressively stripping columns from right to left: (Year, Quarter, Month), (Year, Quarter), (Year), and ()."},{"id":396,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What grouping sets does 'GROUP BY CUBE(Department, Location)' generate?","options":["A) (Department, Location), (Department), (Location), and () grand total (all 2^N combinations)","B) Only (Department, Location)","C) Only the grand total","D) 3 combinations"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CUBE produces all 2^N multidimensional combinations of the specified columns: both columns together, each column independently, and the grand total."},{"id":397,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What function differentiates whether a NULL in a ROLLUP/CUBE result represents an original NULL data value or a subtotal row generated by the grouping operation?","options":["A) GROUPING() or GROUPING_ID()","B) ISNULL()","C) COALESCE()","D) NULLIF()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The GROUPING(column) function returns 1 if the column is currently aggregated (acting as a subtotal) and 0 if the row represents a regular data row."},{"id":398,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"In a PIVOT operator, what must be specified inside the 'IN (...)' clause?","options":["A) The exact list of hardcoded values that will become the new column names","B) A SELECT query returning column names","C) A table alias","D) A data type"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Standard static PIVOT syntax requires an explicit, hardcoded comma-separated list of values in the IN clause: FOR PivotCol IN ([2022], [2023], [2024])."},{"id":399,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"How can you pivot on dynamic values that are not known until runtime?","options":["A) Construct and execute dynamic SQL using sp_executesql","B) Use the DYNAMIC PIVOT keyword","C) Use an index hint","D) Static PIVOT automatically discovers dynamic columns"],"answer":"A","explanation":"To pivot on dynamic columns, you must query distinct values from the table, concatenate them into a comma-delimited string, construct a dynamic SQL query, and execute it with sp_executesql."},{"id":400,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What does GROUPING_ID(col1, col2) return?","options":["A) An integer bitmap where each bit indicates whether the corresponding column was aggregated in that grouping set","B) The identity value of the group","C) The count of rows in the group","D) A unique GUID"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GROUPING_ID computes an integer bitmask (e.g. 0, 1, 2, 3) representing the combination of columns aggregated, simplifying filtering of specific subtotal levels."},{"module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"How does the UNPIVOT operator handle NULL values in the rotated columns by default?","options":["A) UNPIVOT automatically eliminates rows where the unpivoted value is NULL","B) UNPIVOT converts NULLs to zeros","C) UNPIVOT throws a runtime error on NULLs","D) UNPIVOT creates empty strings"],"answer":"A","explanation":"UNPIVOT filters out rows with NULL values in source columns by default, returning only rows corresponding to existing values.","id":401,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What is the key advantage of GROUP BY GROUPING SETS over multiple UNION ALL queries?","options":["A) SQL Server scans the base table only once and computes multiple aggregation levels efficiently in a single pass","B) GROUPING SETS allows unindexed queries to run faster","C) GROUPING SETS automatically creates permanent tables","D) GROUPING SETS can only be used in SSIS"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Instead of scanning the underlying table multiple times with UNION ALL, GROUPING SETS allows SQL Server to optimize data access and compute subtotals in a single pass.","id":402,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"How many grouping sets are produced by 'GROUP BY ROLLUP(A, B, C)' vs 'GROUP BY CUBE(A, B, C)'?","options":["A) ROLLUP produces 4 grouping sets (N + 1); CUBE produces 8 grouping sets (2^N)","B) ROLLUP produces 8; CUBE produces 4","C) Both produce 8","D) Both produce 3"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ROLLUP produces N + 1 hierarchical levels: (A,B,C), (A,B), (A), (). CUBE produces all 2^N combinations: 2^3 = 8 multidimensional combinations.","id":403,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"What does GROUPING_ID(col1, col2) return for a grand total row where both col1 and col2 are aggregated?","options":["A) 3 (binary 11)","B) 0 (binary 00)","C) 1","D) -1"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GROUPING_ID sets bit 1 for col1 and bit 0 for col2. For the grand total row, both are aggregated (1 and 1), giving binary 11 = decimal 3.","id":404,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 14: Pivoting & Grouping Sets","question":"Why must dynamic SQL be used to construct pivot queries when pivoted column names depend on database data?","options":["A) Static PIVOT requires column names to be hardcoded in the IN clause at compile time","B) PIVOT does not support character data","C) Dynamic SQL runs on the client","D) Static PIVOT is deprecated"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Static PIVOT syntax strictly requires literal column headers inside the IN clause. When headers vary dynamically (e.g. dynamic years or months), dynamic SQL must construct the query string.","id":405,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":406,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What command is used to execute a stored procedure in T-SQL?","options":["A) CALL","B) EXECUTE (or EXEC)","C) RUN","D) START"],"answer":"B","explanation":"EXECUTE or EXEC is the T-SQL statement used to execute a stored procedure, scalar user-defined function, or dynamic SQL string."},{"id":407,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"How do you define a parameter in a stored procedure that returns a value back to the caller?","options":["A) @param INT OUTPUT","B) @param INT RETURN","C) @param INT OUT_ONLY","D) @param INT AS REF"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The OUTPUT keyword specifies that the parameter can return a value to the calling program. The caller must also specify OUTPUT when executing the procedure."},{"id":408,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What does the RETURN statement inside a stored procedure return to the caller?","options":["A) A single integer status/return code (typically 0 for success, non-zero for error)","B) A complete table result set","C) A string message","D) A GUID"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The RETURN statement exits the procedure unconditionally and returns an integer return code (by convention, 0 signifies success; non-zero indicates an error)."},{"id":409,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"Which system stored procedure is recommended for executing dynamic SQL statements with parameterization?","options":["A) sp_executesql","B) xp_cmdshell","C) sp_runquery","D) sp_dynamic"],"answer":"A","explanation":"sp_executesql supports parameterized dynamic SQL execution, which promotes query execution plan reuse in the cache and protects against SQL injection."},{"id":410,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What is the primary danger of concatenating user input directly into dynamic SQL executed via EXEC(@sql)?","options":["A) SQL Injection vulnerability","B) Memory buffer overflow in the network card","C) High disk fragmentation","D) It converts numbers to text"],"answer":"A","explanation":"String concatenation allows attackers to inject malicious T-SQL code. Dynamic SQL should always use parameterized queries with sp_executesql."},{"id":411,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What is an advantage of Stored Procedures over ad-hoc T-SQL queries?","options":["A) Security encapsulation (granting EXEC permissions without direct table access), reduced network traffic, and cached execution plans","B) Stored procedures never recompile","C) Stored procedures eliminate the need for backups","D) Stored procedures bypass database constraints"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Stored procedures encapsulate business logic, permit granular security access without granting table-level SELECT/INSERT rights, reduce network bandwidth, and reuse compiled plans."},{"id":412,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What clause on CREATE PROCEDURE forces SQL Server to generate a fresh execution plan every time the procedure is executed?","options":["A) WITH RECOMPILE","B) WITH FRESH_PLAN","C) WITH OPTIMIZE","D) WITH NEW_EXEC"],"answer":"A","explanation":"WITH RECOMPILE causes SQL Server to discard the existing execution plan and compile a new plan each time the procedure runs, useful when parameters vary drastically."},{"id":413,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"How can default values be defined for stored procedure parameters?","options":["A) In the parameter declaration: @City NVARCHAR(30) = 'London'","B) In the body using DEFAULT @City = 'London'","C) Stored procedures cannot have default parameter values","D) In the caller's EXEC statement"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Default parameter values are declared in the procedure header: '@ParamName DataType = DefaultValue'. If the caller omits that parameter, the default is used."},{"id":414,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What command passes parameters by name to a stored procedure?","options":["A) EXEC GetOrders @CustID = 5, @Year = 2024;","B) EXEC GetOrders(5, 2024);","C) EXEC GetOrders @CustID -> 5;","D) EXEC GetOrders :CustID = 5;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Parameters can be passed by name using '@ParameterName = value', allowing parameters to be supplied in any order and optional parameters to be skipped."},{"id":415,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"Which system procedure displays the text definition of an unencrypted stored procedure or view?","options":["A) sp_helptext","B) sp_showcode","C) sp_viewtext","D) sp_inspect"],"answer":"A","explanation":"sp_helptext 'ProcedureName' displays the definition text of an unencrypted stored procedure, view, function, or trigger."},{"module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What is 'Parameter Sniffing' in SQL Server stored procedures?","options":["A) SQL Server inspects parameter values passed during the initial compilation to create an execution plan optimized for those specific values","B) A network security vulnerability that intercepts passwords","C) Reading parameters from the Windows registry","D) Validating parameter data types"],"answer":"A","explanation":"When a procedure compiles, SQL Server 'sniffs' the current parameter values to estimate cardinality and build a plan. If subsequent calls use drastically different values, performance can degrade.","id":416,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What query hint instructs SQL Server to compile a stored procedure query without parameter sniffing, using average distribution statistics?","options":["A) OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN)","B) OPTION (FAST 10)","C) OPTION (IGNORE PARAMETERS)","D) OPTION (NO SNIFF)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN) directs the optimizer to use statistical density vector averages rather than sniffing the runtime parameter values, providing a stable, average plan.","id":417,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What does 'SET NOCOUNT ON;' do at the beginning of a stored procedure?","options":["A) Suppresses the '(N rows affected)' informational messages sent to the client after each DML statement, reducing network traffic","B) Prevents counting rows in queries","C) Disables transaction logs","D) Cancels long-running queries"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SET NOCOUNT ON stops the transmission of DONE_IN_PROC tokens ('X rows affected') to the client for each statement, reducing network latency and preventing client driver parsing issues.","id":418,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What keyword is required when passing a Table-Valued Parameter (TVP) into a stored procedure?","options":["A) READONLY","B) OUTPUT","C) CONSTANT","D) STATIC"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Table-valued parameters must be declared with the READONLY modifier in the stored procedure signature; modifications (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) are not permitted on TVPs.","id":419,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 15: Executing Stored Procedures","question":"What is the benefit of sp_executesql over EXEC(@sql) regarding query execution plan reuse?","options":["A) Parameterized statements in sp_executesql permit execution plans to be cached and reused across different parameter values, reducing compilation overhead","B) sp_executesql runs on background threads","C) EXEC(@sql) is deprecated","D) sp_executesql bypasses user permissions"],"answer":"A","explanation":"sp_executesql separates the query template from parameter values. SQL Server caches the parameterized plan and reuses it for different arguments, avoiding recompilations.","id":420,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":421,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What does the GO command signify in SSMS and SQLCMD?","options":["A) It is not a T-SQL keyword; it is a client-side command separator signaling the end of a batch of T-SQL statements","B) It begins a transaction","C) It commits data to disk immediately","D) It compiles the database"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GO is not an engine T-SQL statement. It is a utility command recognized by SSMS, sqlcmd, and PowerShell to divide T-SQL scripts into batches sent separately to the server."},{"id":422,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What is the lifetime and scope of a T-SQL local variable declared with DECLARE @VarName INT?","options":["A) The entire database session","B) Limited to the batch or stored procedure in which it is declared","C) Global across all connections","D) Persisted until server reboot"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Local variables are scoped strictly to the batch, stored procedure, or block in which they are declared. Once the batch ends (e.g. at a GO command), the variable ceases to exist."},{"id":423,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What initial value does a declared local variable hold before explicit assignment?","options":["A) 0","B) NULL","C) An empty string ''","D) Undefined error"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In T-SQL, all local variables are initialized to NULL when declared, unless assigned an inline default value (e.g. DECLARE @x INT = 10)."},{"id":424,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What looping construct is supported natively in T-SQL for programmatic control of flow?","options":["A) FOR loop","B) WHILE loop","C) DO...UNTIL loop","D) FOREACH loop"],"answer":"B","explanation":"T-SQL provides the WHILE statement to repeat statement execution while a boolean condition remains true. (BREAK and CONTINUE control execution flow inside the loop)."},{"id":425,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What do the BREAK and CONTINUE statements do inside a WHILE loop?","options":["A) BREAK exits the loop entirely; CONTINUE skips the remaining statements and starts the next iteration","B) CONTINUE exits the loop; BREAK restarts it","C) BREAK deletes the loop counter","D) Both exit the database connection"],"answer":"A","explanation":"BREAK exits the innermost WHILE loop immediately. CONTINUE skips the remaining statements in the current loop iteration and re-evaluates the WHILE condition."},{"id":426,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What is required if an IF or ELSE branch needs to execute multiple T-SQL statements?","options":["A) Enclosing the statements in a BEGIN ... END block","B) Enclosing the statements in parentheses (...)","C) Adding a GO statement after each line","D) Using curly braces {...}"],"answer":"A","explanation":"BEGIN and END enclose a block of multiple T-SQL statements so they execute as a single programmatic unit under an IF, ELSE, or WHILE clause."},{"id":427,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What is a Synonym in SQL Server?","options":["A) An alternative single-part alias name for a database object (table, view, stored proc) that abstracts its physical location or schema","B) A duplicate copy of a table","C) A word with similar meaning in full-text search","D) An encrypted backup"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A Synonym (CREATE SYNONYM SynName FOR Server.Database.Schema.Object) provides an abstraction layer so code references a single alias rather than hardcoded four-part object names."},{"id":428,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What is the key danger when using 'SELECT @var = Column FROM Table' if the query returns multiple rows?","options":["A) A runtime error is raised immediately","B) The variable silently retains the value from the last processed row, masking a potential logic bug","C) The variable is set to NULL","D) The table is dropped"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Unlike SET @var = (subquery) which fails if multiple rows are returned, 'SELECT @var = col' does not error; it repeatedly overwrites @var, silently leaving it with the value of the last processed row."},{"id":429,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"How do you define a Table Variable in T-SQL?","options":["A) DECLARE @MyTable TABLE (ID INT, Name VARCHAR(50));","B) CREATE TABLE #MyTable (ID INT);","C) VAR Table MyTable = new Table();","D) DECLARE TABLE @MyTable;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Table variables are declared using 'DECLARE @VariableName TABLE (...)'. They exist in tempdb and are scoped to the executing batch, transaction, or stored procedure."},{"id":430,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What statement pauses the execution of a batch or stored procedure for a specified duration or until a specific time of day?","options":["A) SLEEP","B) WAITFOR (e.g. WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:05' or WAITFOR TIME '23:00:00')","C) PAUSE","D) HALT"],"answer":"B","explanation":"WAITFOR blocks batch execution either for a specific duration ('WAITFOR DELAY 'hh:mm:ss'') or until an exact time ('WAITFOR TIME 'hh:mm:ss'')."},{"module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What does the @@ROWCOUNT system function return?","options":["A) The number of rows affected by the statement immediately preceding it","B) The total rows in the database","C) The row count of the current table","D) The active session count"],"answer":"A","explanation":"@@ROWCOUNT returns the number of rows affected or returned by the most recent statement. It resets after every statement (including IF tests), so it must be captured immediately.","id":431,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What does the system function @@TRANCOUNT report?","options":["A) The number of active BEGIN TRANSACTION statements issued in the current session","B) The total transactions committed since server startup","C) The transaction log size","D) The count of open tables"],"answer":"A","explanation":"@@TRANCOUNT increments by 1 with each BEGIN TRAN. COMMIT decrements it by 1. A ROLLBACK statement unconditionally resets @@TRANCOUNT to 0, rolling back all nested levels.","id":432,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What happens when a ROLLBACK TRANSACTION statement is executed inside a nested transaction (where @@TRANCOUNT > 1)?","options":["A) It rolls back all inner and outer transactions completely and resets @@TRANCOUNT to 0","B) It rolls back only the innermost transaction and decrements @@TRANCOUNT by 1","C) It commits the outer transaction","D) A syntax error is raised"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SQL Server does not support true autonomous nested transactions. Any ROLLBACK without a savepoint rolls back the entire transaction stack down to @@TRANCOUNT = 0.","id":433,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"What statement defines a branch label in T-SQL for unconditional jumps using GOTO?","options":["A) LabelName:","B) GOTO LabelName","C) #LabelName","D) LABEL LabelName"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A label is defined by an identifier followed by a colon (e.g. 'Cleanup:'). 'GOTO Cleanup;' unconditionally jumps execution to that label.","id":434,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 16: Programming with T-SQL","question":"Why should table variables (@table) generally be avoided for multi-million row datasets?","options":["A) Table variables lack column distribution statistics and histograms, leading the query optimizer to assume 1 row (or fixed low count) and select poor query plans","B) Table variables cannot be joined","C) Table variables do not support indexes","D) Table variables crash the server"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Table variables do not maintain statistics. For large datasets, temporary tables (#tables) are preferred because they support statistics, parallel plans, and dynamic re-indexing.","id":435,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":436,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What construct provides structured exception handling in T-SQL?","options":["A) BEGIN TRY ... END TRY BEGIN CATCH ... END CATCH","B) TRY ... EXCEPT","C) ON ERROR GOTO","D) CATCH ... FINALLY"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SQL Server 2005+ supports structured exception handling using 'BEGIN TRY ... END TRY' followed immediately by 'BEGIN CATCH ... END CATCH'."},{"id":437,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"Which function returns the human-readable text description of the error that caused a CATCH block to execute?","options":["A) ERROR_MESSAGE()","B) ERROR_NUMBER()","C) ERROR_TEXT()","D) @@ERROR"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ERROR_MESSAGE() returns the complete message text of the error that triggered the CATCH block."},{"id":438,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What function returns the severity level of the error caught in a CATCH block?","options":["A) ERROR_LEVEL()","B) ERROR_SEVERITY()","C) ERROR_STATUS()","D) ERROR_GRADE()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"ERROR_SEVERITY() returns the severity level (0-25) of the error that caused the CATCH block to run."},{"id":439,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What is the recommended modern statement for raising custom errors or re-throwing caught exceptions in SQL Server 2012+?","options":["A) RAISERROR","B) THROW","C) ERROR","D) ABORT"],"answer":"B","explanation":"THROW is the ANSI-compliant modern statement. Inside a CATCH block, 'THROW;' without parameters re-throws the original exception preserving details. Unlike RAISERROR, THROW always honors XACT_ABORT."},{"id":440,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What happens if a statement preceding a THROW statement is not terminated with a semicolon?","options":["A) The THROW statement executes normally","B) A syntax error is raised because the statement before THROW must end with a semicolon","C) The error is written to tempdb","D) The server crashes"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In T-SQL, the statement immediately preceding a THROW statement MUST terminate with a semicolon (;), otherwise a parsing syntax error occurs."},{"id":441,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What does the function XACT_STATE() return?","options":["A) 1 if an active committable transaction exists; -1 if an uncommittable 'doomed' transaction exists; 0 if no transaction is active","B) The elapsed time of the current transaction","C) The transaction log file size","D) The session ID"],"answer":"A","explanation":"XACT_STATE() reports the status of the current user transaction: 1 (valid and committable), -1 (uncommittable; must be rolled back), or 0 (no active transaction)."},{"id":442,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What is the behavior of SET XACT_ABORT ON?","options":["A) SQL Server automatically rolls back the entire transaction and terminates batch execution if any run-time error occurs","B) Ignores all errors and continues execution","C) Commits transactions automatically","D) Suppresses all error messages"],"answer":"A","explanation":"When SET XACT_ABORT is ON, any statement that raises a runtime error causes the entire transaction to be terminated and rolled back immediately."},{"id":443,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What system variable holds the error number of the most recently executed statement prior to SQL Server 2005?","options":["A) @@ERROR","B) @@ERR_NUM","C) @@STATUS","D) @@EXCEPTION"],"answer":"A","explanation":"@@ERROR returns the error number for the last statement executed. Because it resets to 0 after any successful statement, it had to be captured immediately into a variable."},{"id":444,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"Can compile-time syntax errors and object-resolution errors be caught by a TRY...CATCH block in the same batch?","options":["A) Yes, all errors are caught","B) No, syntax errors and deferred name resolution errors occur at compile time before the batch starts executing","C) Only if XACT_ABORT is OFF","D) Only if inside a stored procedure"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Compile-time syntax errors and statement-level recompilation errors prevent the batch from executing at all, so they cannot be trapped by a TRY...CATCH block in the same batch."},{"id":445,"subject":"SQL Server","module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"Which function returns the line number within the routine or batch where the error occurred?","options":["A) ERROR_LINE()","B) ERROR_ROW()","C) LINE_NUMBER()","D) ERROR_POSITION()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ERROR_LINE() returns the exact source line number where the exception occurred that caused the CATCH block to execute."},{"module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What is SQL Server error number 1205?","options":["A) Transaction was deadlocked on lock resources with another process and has been chosen as the deadlock victim","B) Division by zero error","C) Primary key violation error","D) Syntax error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Error 1205 is the classic SQL Server deadlock error: 'Transaction (Process ID ...) was deadlocked on lock resources with another process and has been chosen as the deadlock victim. Rerun the transaction.'","id":446,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What must you do if XACT_STATE() returns -1 inside a CATCH block?","options":["A) The transaction is uncommittable (doomed) and MUST be rolled back; attempting to commit raises an error","B) The transaction can be committed normally","C) Execute another query to fix the error","D) Restart SQL Server"],"answer":"A","explanation":"XACT_STATE() = -1 indicates a doomed, uncommittable transaction caused by an error under XACT_ABORT ON. The only permissible action is ROLLBACK TRANSACTION.","id":447,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"What does the WITH NOWAIT clause do when raising a message with RAISERROR?","options":["A) Sends the message immediately to the client application without waiting for output buffers to fill","B) Terminates the query immediately","C) Disables transaction logging","D) Halts the operating system"],"answer":"A","explanation":"RAISERROR ('message', 0, 1) WITH NOWAIT flushes the message immediately to the client console without buffering.","id":448,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"Which system stored procedure registers custom user-defined error messages in the sys.messages catalog for use with RAISERROR or THROW?","options":["A) sp_addmessage","B) sp_register_error","C) sp_newmessage","D) sp_custom_error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"sp_addmessage adds custom error messages (with message numbers >= 50001) to sys.messages, supporting multi-language error strings.","id":449,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"module":"Module 17: Implementing Error Handling","question":"Which function inside a CATCH block returns the name of the stored procedure or trigger where the error occurred?","options":["A) ERROR_PROCEDURE()","B) ERROR_ROUTINE()","C) ERROR_SOURCE()","D) ERROR_ORIGIN()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ERROR_PROCEDURE() returns the name of the stored procedure or trigger in which the error occurred, or NULL if the error happened in an ad-hoc batch.","id":450,"subject":"SQL Server"},{"id":451,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is the correct and official DOCTYPE declaration for an HTML5 document?","options":["A) <!DOCTYPE html>","B) <!DOCTYPE HTML5 PUBLIC>","C) <!DOCTYPE html SYSTEM \"HTML5.dtd\">","D) <doctype html5>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HTML5 drastically simplified the document type declaration to case-insensitive '<!DOCTYPE html>', which triggers standard rendering mode across all modern browsers."},{"id":452,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"Which meta tag is used to specify the UTF-8 character encoding in an HTML5 document?","options":["A) <meta charset=\"utf-8\">","B) <meta encoding=\"utf-8\">","C) <meta http-equiv=\"character-set\" content=\"utf-8\">","D) <meta type=\"charset/utf-8\">"],"answer":"A","explanation":"In HTML5, character encoding is declared compactly using '<meta charset=\"utf-8\">', placed early in the <head> element."},{"id":453,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is the recommended method for linking an external CSS stylesheet to an HTML document?","options":["A) <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">","B) <style src=\"styles.css\"></style>","C) <stylesheet>styles.css</stylesheet>","D) <import href=\"styles.css\">"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '<link>' tag inside the <head> element with attributes 'rel=\"stylesheet\"' and 'href=\"path/to/file.css\"' is the standard way to attach an external stylesheet."},{"id":454,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is the main drawback of applying inline CSS styles directly on HTML elements via the 'style' attribute?","options":["A) Inline styles are ignored by modern browsers","B) They tightly couple presentation with structure, make site-wide maintenance difficult, and cannot be cached by the browser","C) Inline styles only work for font colors","D) Inline styles require JavaScript to execute"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Inline styles violate separation of concerns, lead to duplicated code, are difficult to maintain globally, and cannot be cached separately like external CSS files."},{"id":455,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"Which HTML element is the root element of an HTML page?","options":["A) <body>","B) <head>","C) <html>","D) <!DOCTYPE>"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The '<html>' element is the top-level root element that wraps all other HTML tags (both <head> and <body>) in the document."},{"id":456,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What CSS syntax consists of a selector, property, and value?","options":["A) Selector { property: value; }","B) Selector = (property -> value);","C) [property = value] => Selector;","D) Selector : { property = value };"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A standard CSS rule-set consists of a selector pointing to the HTML element to style, followed by a declaration block enclosed in curly braces containing 'property: value;' pairs."},{"id":457,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"Where should the <title> tag be placed in an HTML document?","options":["A) Directly inside the <body> tag","B) Inside the <head> container tag","C) Before the <!DOCTYPE html> tag","D) In the <footer> tag"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The <title> tag must be located inside the <head> block, defining the title displayed in the browser's tab or title bar."},{"id":458,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is an HTML 'void' or self-closing element that does not have an end tag?","options":["A) <div>","B) <img>","C) <p>","D) <span>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Void elements cannot contain child nodes or closing tags. Examples include <img>, <input>, <br>, <hr>, and <meta>."},{"id":459,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"Which CSS property is used to change the background color of an element?","options":["A) color","B) background-color","C) bgcolor","D) fill-color"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'background-color' sets the background color of an element in CSS. The 'color' property sets the foreground text color."},{"id":460,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"How do you write comments in HTML vs CSS?","options":["A) HTML: <!-- comment --> | CSS: /* comment */","B) HTML: // comment | CSS: <!-- comment -->","C) HTML: /* comment */ | CSS: // comment","D) HTML: # comment | CSS: # comment"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HTML comments use '<!-- comment -->' delimiters. CSS comments use '/* comment */' delimiters."},{"module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is the primary purpose of the '<meta name=\"description\">' tag in an HTML document?","options":["A) Provides a concise summary of the page for search engine results pages (SERP snippet) and social shares","B) Configures CSS font styles","C) Connects to a database description table","D) Defines internal browser memory limits"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The meta description tag provides search engines with a summary of the page, frequently used as the snippet preview under the title in search engine results pages (SEO).","id":461,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is the key difference between '<script defer>' and '<script async>'?","options":["A) 'defer' downloads in parallel and executes in document order after the DOM is fully parsed; 'async' downloads in parallel and executes immediately as soon as downloaded, pausing DOM parsing","B) 'defer' is for external scripts; 'async' is for inline scripts","C) 'async' executes after DOMContentLoaded; 'defer' does not","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'defer' guarantees execution order after HTML parsing finishes. 'async' executes asynchronously as soon as the script arrives, without guaranteeing order.","id":462,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What is the difference between a CSS Reset (e.g. Meyerweb) and Normalize.css?","options":["A) CSS Reset strips all default browser styling to zero; Normalize.css preserves useful browser defaults while correcting bugs and unifying cross-browser inconsistencies","B) Normalize.css removes all styles","C) CSS Reset is built into Chrome only","D) Normalize.css is a JavaScript library"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A CSS Reset resets margins, paddings, and font sizes to a blank slate. Normalize.css preserves useful defaults (like headings and list bullets) while standardizing element styling across different browsers.","id":463,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"What do the components in the CSS color function 'hsl(210, 100%, 50%)' represent?","options":["A) Hue (0-360 degrees on the color wheel), Saturation (percentage), and Lightness (percentage)","B) Height, Size, and Length","C) High, Standard, and Low","D) Hexadecimal, Standard, and Luminescence"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HSL stands for Hue (angle on color wheel: 0=red, 120=green, 240=blue), Saturation (0% grey to 100% full color), and Lightness (0% black to 100% white).","id":464,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 1: Overview of HTML and CSS","question":"Which tag and attribute configure the small icon displayed in the browser tab (Favicon)?","options":["A) <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.ico\">","B) <meta name=\"icon\" content=\"favicon.ico\">","C) <img src=\"favicon.ico\" type=\"tab\">","D) <favicon src=\"favicon.ico\">"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '<link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"favicon.ico\">' element inside the <head> section specifies the favicon displayed next to the page title in browser tabs.","id":465,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":466,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What is the semantic purpose of the HTML5 <header> element?","options":["A) It replaces the <head> tag for metadata","B) It represents introductory content or a set of navigational links for its nearest sectioning ancestor or page","C) It defines the primary heading <h1> of the page only","D) It creates an HTTP request header"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The <header> element represents a container for introductory content (such as a logo, heading, author info) or navigational links for an article or page."},{"id":467,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which HTML5 element represents an independent, self-contained piece of content that could be distributed or syndicated separately (e.g. a blog post, forum post)?","options":["A) <section>","B) <article>","C) <aside>","D) <div>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"<article> represents a self-contained composition in a document, page, application, or site that is intended to be independently distributable or reusable (e.g., news story, blog entry)."},{"id":468,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What does the HTML5 <aside> element represent?","options":["A) Content that is separate and tangentially related to the content around it (like sidebars, pull quotes, or related links)","B) Hidden comments not visible to screen readers","C) Pop-up alert boxes","D) Deprecated code"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <aside> element represents a portion of a document whose content is only tangentially related to the document's main content, commonly presented as a sidebar or callout."},{"id":469,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which element wraps self-contained visual media (like an image, illustration, or code listing) together with an optional <figcaption>?","options":["A) <picture>","B) <figure>","C) <media>","D) <illustration>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The <figure> element specifies self-contained content, frequently with a caption declared using the <figcaption> element."},{"id":470,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What is the Document Object Model (DOM)?","options":["A) A programming interface (API) that represents an HTML document as a tree of objects that can be manipulated programmatically","B) A database management system for web browsers","C) A CSS compilation tool","D) A web hosting provider protocol"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The DOM represents an HTML/XML document in memory as a logical tree hierarchy of nodes (elements, attributes, text), enabling languages like JavaScript to dynamically modify content and structure."},{"id":471,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which HTML5 element represents a section of a page whose purpose is to provide navigation links?","options":["A) <nav>","B) <menu>","C) <links>","D) <dir>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <nav> element represents a major navigation block containing links either to other pages or to parts within the current page."},{"id":472,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What is the semantic role of the <main> element in HTML5?","options":["A) It specifies the dominant, central content unique to the document, excluding headers, footers, and sidebars","B) It designates the main JavaScript execution entry point","C) It wraps the entire <html> body","D) It can appear multiple times inside an <article>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <main> element represents the dominant, unique content of the <body> of a document. It should not contain content repeated across pages (like headers, footers, or navigation)."},{"id":473,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"In the DOM tree, what type of node represents the actual text inside an element (e.g. the text 'Hello' in <p>Hello</p>)?","options":["A) Element Node","B) Text Node","C) Attribute Node","D) Comment Node"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In the W3C DOM model, plain text contained inside elements is represented by Text nodes (nodeType 3), which are child nodes of the parent Element node."},{"id":474,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What is the purpose of semantic HTML elements over generic <div> and <span> tags?","options":["A) They provide clear meaning to search engines, screen readers, assistive devices, and developers about the content's role","B) They render with built-in 3D GPU animations","C) They prevent web pages from being downloaded","D) They speed up internet bandwidth"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Semantic tags describe their meaning and purpose to the browser, assistive technologies (screen readers for accessibility), search engine bots (SEO), and human maintainers."},{"id":475,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which HTML5 element represents a thematic grouping of content, typically with a heading?","options":["A) <section>","B) <span>","C) <group>","D) <block>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"<section> represents a standalone generic section of a document that does not have a more specific semantic element to represent it, typically starting with a heading element (h1-h6)."},{"module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What is the semantic purpose of the HTML5 '<time>' element?","options":["A) Encodes a machine-readable date/time using the 'datetime' attribute while presenting human-readable text","B) Displays an analog clock on the page","C) Starts a JavaScript countdown timer","D) Records user time spent on the page"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <time> element provides machine-readable dates and times (via the 'datetime' attribute in ISO format, e.g. datetime=\"2026-09-14\") to search engines and calendar apps.","id":476,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which HTML5 element represents text highlighted for reference or notation purposes due to its relevance in another context?","options":["A) <mark>","B) <highlight>","C) <b>","D) <em>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <mark> element represents text marked or highlighted for reference purposes (such as search result term matches), typically rendered with a yellow background.","id":477,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which HTML5 pair creates a native disclosure widget that the user can click to expand and collapse content without any JavaScript?","options":["A) <details> and <summary>","B) <accordion> and <tab>","C) <collapse> and <header>","D) <toggle> and <item>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <details> element creates an interactive disclosure widget, with <summary> defining the visible heading. Clicking the summary toggles the visibility of the remaining details content.","id":478,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"What is the difference between the HTML5 '<progress>' element and the '<meter>' element?","options":["A) <progress> represents task completion progress (0% to 100%); <meter> represents a scalar measurement within a known range (like disk space, battery level, or exam grade)","B) <meter> is animated; <progress> is static","C) <progress> only works in forms","D) There is no distinction"],"answer":"A","explanation":"<progress> denotes the progress of an ongoing task. <meter> denotes a gauge or scalar measurement within a known minimum/maximum range (e.g. CPU usage, storage space).","id":479,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 2: HTML5 Document Structure & DOM","question":"Which DOM properties allow navigating to an element's parent, child, and sibling elements excluding whitespace text nodes?","options":["A) parentElement, children, nextElementSibling, previousElementSibling","B) parentNode, childNodes, nextSibling, previousSibling","C) topElement, subElements, siblingLeft, siblingRight","D) getParent(), getChildren(), getSibling()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Element traversal properties (parentElement, children, nextElementSibling, previousElementSibling) only return Element nodes (nodeType 1), ignoring whitespace text nodes.","id":480,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":481,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"Which CSS selector targets an element by its unique ID attribute?","options":["A) .header","B) #header","C) *header","D) @header"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In CSS, the hash symbol '#' denotes an ID selector (e.g. #header matches <div id=\"header\">). The period '.' denotes a class selector."},{"id":482,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What is the difference between the descendant combinator (div p) and the child combinator (div > p)?","options":["A) 'div p' matches any <p> nested at any depth inside a <div>; 'div > p' matches only <p> elements that are DIRECT children of a <div>","B) 'div > p' matches all paragraphs; 'div p' matches only the first paragraph","C) 'div > p' matches siblings only","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The space character is the descendant selector (matching nested descendants at any depth). The '>' symbol is the child combinator, matching only direct immediate children."},{"id":483,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"Which attribute selector matches an element whose attribute value begins with a specific string?","options":["A) [href^=\"https\"]","B) [href$=\"https\"]","C) [href*=\"https\"]","D) [href~=\"https\"]"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'[attr^=val]' matches values starting with 'val'. '[attr$=val]' matches values ending with 'val'. '[attr*=val]' matches values containing the substring 'val'."},{"id":484,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What is the CSS specificity order from HIGHEST to LOWEST?","options":["A) Inline style > ID selector > Class/Attribute/Pseudo-class > Element/Pseudo-element","B) Element > Class > ID > Inline style","C) ID selector > Inline style > Element > Class","D) Class > ID > Inline style > Element"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CSS specificity is calculated in categories: Inline styles (1,0,0,0) > IDs (0,1,0,0) > Classes, attributes, and pseudo-classes (0,0,1,0) > Elements and pseudo-elements (0,0,0,1)."},{"id":485,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What does the adjacent sibling combinator '+' select in 'h2 + p'?","options":["A) All <p> elements inside an <h2>","B) The very first <p> element that immediately follows an <h2> at the same hierarchy level","C) All <p> elements that share a parent with <h2>","D) Both <h2> and <p> elements together"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The adjacent sibling combinator '+' matches an element that immediately follows the specified element and shares the same direct parent."},{"id":486,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What happens when '!important' is added to a CSS property value declaration?","options":["A) It overrides standard cascade specificity and applies the rule over other normal declarations","B) It causes a compilation error in modern browsers","C) It encrypts the CSS rule","D) It only applies when the page is printed"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Adding '!important' gives the declaration priority over normal specificity rules in the cascade, overriding other author styles."},{"id":487,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"Which pseudo-class applies when the user hovers a pointing device over an element without activating it?","options":["A) :focus","B) :hover","C) :active","D) :visited"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The ':hover' CSS pseudo-class matches when the user interacts with an element with a pointing device (like hovering a mouse cursor over a button or link)."},{"id":488,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What is the difference between a pseudo-class (e.g. :hover) and a pseudo-element (e.g. ::before)?","options":["A) Pseudo-classes select elements based on state; pseudo-elements style specific parts of an element or create virtual elements","B) Pseudo-elements are written with single colons only","C) Pseudo-classes only work on input fields","D) There is no distinction in CSS3"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Pseudo-classes (:hover, :first-child, :checked) select elements based on state or DOM position. Pseudo-elements (::before, ::after, ::first-line) target sub-parts or generate decorative content."},{"id":489,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What does the general sibling combinator '~' select in 'h1 ~ p'?","options":["A) All <p> elements that follow an <h1> element anywhere as siblings under the same parent","B) Only the immediate next sibling","C) All child paragraphs","D) Paragraphs with identical font size"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The general sibling combinator '~' selects all subsequent siblings of an element that match the selector, not just the immediately adjacent one."},{"id":490,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"Which pseudo-class selects every odd row in a table body?","options":["A) tbody tr:nth-child(odd)","B) tbody tr:nth-row(1)","C) tbody tr:odd","D) tbody tr:first-child(2)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"':nth-child(odd)' (or ':nth-child(2n+1)') targets all odd-numbered elements in a sequence, creating zebra-striped tables."},{"module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What does the ':not()' pseudo-class do in CSS?","options":["A) Matches elements that do NOT match the specified selector: input:not([type=\"checkbox\"])","B) Disables CSS styles","C) Inverts the color palette","D) Hides elements from screen readers"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The negation pseudo-class ':not(selector)' targets every element that does not match the selector passed as its parameter.","id":491,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"How do you declare and consume a CSS Custom Property (CSS variable)?","options":["A) Declaration: --brand-color: #38bdf8; Consumption: color: var(--brand-color);","B) Declaration: $brand-color: #38bdf8; Consumption: color: $brand-color;","C) Declaration: @brand-color: #38bdf8; Consumption: color: @brand-color;","D) Declaration: &brand-color = #38bdf8; Consumption: color: get(&brand-color);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CSS variables are declared with two leading hyphens (e.g. '--main-color: blue;') and consumed using the 'var(--main-color)' function, cascading down the DOM hierarchy.","id":492,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"What is the difference between ':is()' and ':where()' pseudo-classes regarding CSS specificity?","options":["A) ':is()' takes the specificity of its most specific argument; ':where()' always has a specificity score of 0","B) ':where()' has higher specificity than ':is()'","C) ':is()' cannot accept class selectors","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Both :is() and :where() group selectors concisely, but :where() deliberately zeroes out specificity (0-0-0), making rules easier to override downstream.","id":493,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"Which pseudo-element styles the portion of a document that has been selected/highlighted by the user?","options":["A) ::selection","B) ::highlight","C) ::active-text","D) ::selected"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '::selection' pseudo-element applies styles (such as background and color) to the portion of text that has been highlighted or selected by the user.","id":494,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 3: CSS Selectors & Cascading","question":"Which selector matches any <button> element that has the 'disabled' attribute present, regardless of its value?","options":["A) button[disabled]","B) button:disabled-attribute","C) button.disabled()","D) button->disabled"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'button[disabled]' matches any button with the attribute present. ':disabled' is also available as a pseudo-class for disabled form controls.","id":495,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":496,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which HTML5 input type automatically validates that the entered value conforms to an email address format upon form submission?","options":["A) <input type=\"mail\">","B) <input type=\"email\">","C) <input type=\"text\" validate=\"email\">","D) <input type=\"address\">"],"answer":"B","explanation":"<input type=\"email\"> provides built-in browser validation, verifying that the input contains a valid email format, and displays an optimized email keyboard on mobile devices."},{"id":497,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"What attribute displays light grey hint text inside an input field that disappears when the user starts typing?","options":["A) value","B) placeholder","C) tooltip","D) hint"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'placeholder' attribute specifies a short hint (such as a sample format) that describes the expected value of an input field before the user enters text."},{"id":498,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which boolean attribute prevents a form from being submitted if the input field is empty?","options":["A) validate","B) mandatory","C) required","D) notnull"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The 'required' attribute specifies that an input field must be filled out before the browser permits the enclosing form to be submitted."},{"id":499,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which HTML5 element provides a predefined list of autocomplete options for an <input> element via its 'list' attribute?","options":["A) <select>","B) <datalist>","C) <autocomplete>","D) <optionlist>"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The <datalist> tag contains <option> items that offer suggested autocomplete values for an associated <input list=\"id\"> without restricting the user from typing custom text."},{"id":500,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which input type provides a graphical slider control for selecting a numeric value within a range?","options":["A) <input type=\"slider\">","B) <input type=\"range\">","C) <input type=\"number\">","D) <input type=\"scroll\">"],"answer":"B","explanation":"<input type=\"range\"> renders a visual slider bar control for choosing an imprecise numeric value between 'min' and 'max' bounds."},{"id":501,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"What attribute allows you to enforce a custom regular expression pattern on an <input> element?","options":["A) regex","B) pattern","C) match","D) validate"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'pattern' attribute specifies a JavaScript regular expression that the input field's value must match against for the input to be considered valid upon submission."},{"id":502,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which attribute automatically focuses the cursor on a specified input field when the web page finishes loading?","options":["A) active","B) autofocus","C) focus-on-load","D) selected"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The 'autofocus' boolean attribute automatically places the input cursor into the target element as soon as the page loads (only one element per page should have it)."},{"id":503,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"What input type renders a native date picker control allowing the user to select day, month, and year?","options":["A) <input type=\"calendar\">","B) <input type=\"date\">","C) <input type=\"datetime-local\">","D) <input type=\"time\">"],"answer":"B","explanation":"<input type=\"date\"> displays a browser-native date picker widget for inputting dates formatted in the ISO YYYY-MM-DD standard."},{"id":504,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"What attributes define the minimum value, maximum value, and increment intervals for an <input type=\"number\">?","options":["A) low, high, and interval","B) min, max, and step","C) start, end, and skip","D) floor, ceiling, and delta"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'min' defines the lower bound, 'max' defines the upper bound, and 'step' sets the legal numeric step interval (e.g. step=\"0.5\")."},{"id":505,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which attribute can be added to a <form> or submit button to bypass native HTML5 browser validation upon submit?","options":["A) novalidate (or formnovalidate)","B) skip-validation","C) disable-check","D) no-pattern"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Adding 'novalidate' to the <form> element or 'formnovalidate' to a submit button instructs the browser to skip constraint validation when submitting the form."},{"module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"What input type provides an optimized virtual telephone keypad on mobile devices?","options":["A) <input type=\"tel\">","B) <input type=\"phone\">","C) <input type=\"mobile\">","D) <input type=\"digits\">"],"answer":"A","explanation":"<input type=\"tel\"> designates a telephone number input and triggers a numeric telephone keypad on smartphones and mobile tablets.","id":506,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which attribute on an <input type=\"file\"> allows users to select more than one file for upload?","options":["A) multiple","B) multi-select","C) allow-many","D) batch"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'multiple' boolean attribute allows users to select multiple files in an input type=\"file\" or multiple options in a <select> element.","id":507,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which input type renders a search text box that typically includes a clear (x) button?","options":["A) <input type=\"search\">","B) <input type=\"query\">","C) <input type=\"find\">","D) <input type=\"lookup\">"],"answer":"A","explanation":"<input type=\"search\"> is styled specifically for search queries and frequently renders with an integrated 'x' clear button across browsers.","id":508,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"How do you disable browser autocomplete suggestions on a specific form or input field?","options":["A) autocomplete=\"off\"","B) no-autocomplete","C) suggestions=\"disabled\"","D) disable-cache=\"true\""],"answer":"A","explanation":"Setting 'autocomplete=\"off\"' on a <form> or <input> informs the browser not to suggest or auto-fill previous values entered by the user.","id":509,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 4: HTML5 Forms & Controls","question":"Which attributes define the visible text dimensions of a '<textarea>' element?","options":["A) rows and cols","B) width and height","C) lines and characters","D) size and length"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'rows' specifies the visible number of text lines, and 'cols' specifies the average visible character width of a <textarea>.","id":510,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":511,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"How do you associate a <label> element with an <input> element so screen readers can identify it and clicking the label focuses the input?","options":["A) Set the label's 'for' attribute to match the input's 'id' attribute","B) Set the label's 'name' attribute to match the input's 'name'","C) Set the input's 'label' attribute to the label text","D) Place the label after the input tag with no attributes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'for' attribute of a <label> must match the unique 'id' of the corresponding input control: <label for=\"userEmail\">Email</label><input id=\"userEmail\">."},{"id":512,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which CSS pseudo-class matches form input elements whose values satisfy all validation constraints?","options":["A) :correct","B) :valid","C) :success","D) :accepted"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The ':valid' CSS pseudo-class matches any form control whose current contents pass all HTML5 constraint validations. ':invalid' matches elements failing validation."},{"id":513,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"In the JavaScript Constraint Validation API, what method checks if an element satisfies its validity constraints without submitting the form?","options":["A) checkValidity()","B) testInput()","C) validateNow()","D) verifyRules()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"inputElement.checkValidity() returns true if the element satisfies all validity constraints; if invalid, it fires an 'invalid' DOM event on the element and returns false."},{"id":514,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"What JavaScript method sets a custom validation error message on a form control, marking it invalid?","options":["A) setErrorMessage()","B) setCustomValidity(message)","C) markInvalid(message)","D) addValidationError()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Calling 'setCustomValidity(\"Error text\")' marks the element as invalid and sets the error message displayed in the browser's tooltip. Passing an empty string '\"\"' marks it valid."},{"id":515,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which property of the 'validity' object (input.validity) is true if an input fails its 'pattern' attribute regex?","options":["A) validity.typeMismatch","B) validity.patternMismatch","C) validity.rangeOverflow","D) validity.valueMissing"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'validity.patternMismatch' is true when the input's value does not conform to the regular expression specified in the 'pattern' attribute."},{"id":516,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"What does ARIA stand for in web accessibility?","options":["A) Accessible Rich Internet Applications","B) Advanced Responsive Interface Architecture","C) Automated Rendering Information API","D) Assistive Reader Integration Algorithm"],"answer":"A","explanation":"W3C WAI-ARIA stands for Accessible Rich Internet Applications, defining roles, states, and properties (e.g. aria-required, aria-hidden) to make dynamic web content accessible."},{"id":517,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which ARIA attribute informs screen readers that an input field is currently in an invalid state?","options":["A) aria-invalid=\"true\"","B) aria-error=\"active\"","C) aria-status=\"fail\"","D) aria-alert=\"yes\""],"answer":"A","explanation":"'aria-invalid=\"true\"' announces to screen readers that the value entered into the form field does not conform to the expected format or rules."},{"id":518,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which property on the validity object is true if a required field is left empty?","options":["A) validity.emptyField","B) validity.valueMissing","C) validity.noInput","D) validity.requiredError"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'validity.valueMissing' is true when an element has the 'required' attribute but contains no user value upon validation."},{"id":519,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Why is client-side HTML5 validation NOT sufficient on its own for web security?","options":["A) Because client-side validation can be bypassed, disabled, or intercepted by attackers; all input MUST also be re-validated on the server","B) Because HTML5 validation only works on Chrome","C) Because regex slows down database queries","D) Because client validation drops SSL encryption"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Client-side validation provides immediate, friendly user feedback but can easily be bypassed by manipulating HTTP requests or turning off scripts. Server-side validation is non-negotiable for security."},{"id":520,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"What does the method reportValidity() do in modern browsers?","options":["A) Evaluates constraints, returns a boolean, AND displays the browser's native error bubble tooltip to the user if invalid","B) Sends an email report to the webmaster","C) Clears all invalid form fields","D) Logs validation statistics to Google Analytics"],"answer":"A","explanation":"reportValidity() checks whether the element satisfies its constraints (returning true or false) and actively displays the browser's native validation popup message if invalid."},{"module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which property of 'input.validity' is true if a user types non-numeric characters into an <input type=\"number\">?","options":["A) validity.badInput","B) validity.typeMismatch","C) validity.stepMismatch","D) validity.rangeUnderflow"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'validity.badInput' is true when the browser cannot convert user input into the expected data type (such as entering letters into an input type=\"number\").","id":521,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which property of 'input.validity' is true if a custom validation error was set using setCustomValidity()?","options":["A) validity.customError","B) validity.customInvalid","C) validity.userError","D) validity.manualError"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'validity.customError' returns true whenever setCustomValidity() was called with a non-empty error message string.","id":522,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"What is the difference between 'aria-live=\"polite\"' and 'aria-live=\"assertive\"'?","options":["A) 'polite' waits until the screen reader finishes speaking before announcing updates; 'assertive' interrupts current speech immediately to announce the dynamic change","B) 'polite' uses lower volume","C) 'assertive' plays an alarm sound","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"ARIA live regions notify screen readers of dynamic UI changes. 'polite' queues notifications until speech pauses; 'assertive' interrupts immediately for urgent alerts.","id":523,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"Which HTML elements should be used to group related form controls and assign a caption to the group for accessibility?","options":["A) <fieldset> and <legend>","B) <group> and <title>","C) <panel> and <header>","D) <div> and <span>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <fieldset> element groups related form controls (like a group of radio buttons or address fields), and <legend> provides an accessible caption read by screen readers.","id":524,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 5: Form Validation & Accessibility","question":"What CSS pseudo-class matches optional form inputs that do NOT have the 'required' attribute?","options":["A) :optional","B) :not-required","C) :arbitrary","D) :empty"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The ':optional' pseudo-class matches any form control that does not have the 'required' attribute set.","id":525,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":526,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What is the modern standard method for selecting the FIRST element in the DOM matching a specified CSS selector string?","options":["A) document.querySelector(selector)","B) document.querySelectorAll(selector)","C) document.getElementBySelector(selector)","D) document.findFirst(selector)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"document.querySelector(selector) returns the first Element that matches the specified CSS selector group, or null if no matches are found."},{"id":527,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What does document.querySelectorAll(selector) return?","options":["A) A static NodeList containing all matching Element nodes","B) A live HTMLCollection that updates dynamically","C) A JavaScript Array with map and filter methods","D) A JSON string of attributes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"querySelectorAll returns a static (non-live) NodeList representing a snapshot of all elements in the document matching the given CSS selectors."},{"id":528,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What is the key difference between 'let' and 'var' declarations in JavaScript?","options":["A) 'let' is block-scoped and not hoisted with initialization; 'var' is function-scoped and hoisted with undefined","B) 'let' variables cannot be reassigned","C) 'var' cannot be used inside loops","D) 'let' is only supported in Node.js"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'let' provides block scoping (confined within '{ ... }' blocks) and enforces a Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ). 'var' is hoisted to the top of its enclosing function scope and defaults to undefined."},{"id":529,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"Which property is safest to use when inserting user-provided text into a DOM element to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)?","options":["A) innerHTML","B) textContent","C) outerHTML","D) document.write()"],"answer":"B","explanation":"textContent inserts text as raw text without parsing it as HTML markup, safely escaping characters like '<' and '&' and preventing script injection."},{"id":530,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"How do you add a CSS class 'highlight' to an element using modern DOM methods?","options":["A) element.classList.add('highlight');","B) element.className += ' highlight';","C) element.addClass('highlight');","D) element.style.class = 'highlight';"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'element.classList' API provides clean, standardized methods: add(), remove(), toggle(), and contains() for managing CSS classes without manual string manipulation."},{"id":531,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What method creates a new HTML element node in memory before appending it to the DOM tree?","options":["A) document.createElement('tagName')","B) document.newElement('tagName')","C) document.buildNode('tagName')","D) window.makeElement('tagName')"],"answer":"A","explanation":"document.createElement(tagName) creates the HTML element specified by tagName, ready to have attributes set and be attached to the tree via appendChild() or append()."},{"id":532,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What does the strict equality operator '===' compare in JavaScript?","options":["A) Compares both value and type without performing implicit type coercion","B) Converts types automatically before comparing","C) Compares only object memory addresses","D) Always returns false for strings"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The strict equality operator '===' evaluates both value and data type without type conversion. (e.g. 5 === '5' is false, whereas 5 == '5' is true)."},{"id":533,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What method removes a child element from its parent node in the DOM?","options":["A) parentNode.removeChild(childNode)","B) document.delete(childNode)","C) childNode.destroy()","D) parentNode.detach(childNode)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"parentNode.removeChild(childNode) removes the specified child node from the DOM tree and returns the removed node."},{"id":534,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What happens when declaring a variable with 'const' in JavaScript?","options":["A) The variable binding cannot be reassigned after initialization","B) The properties of an object assigned to const are completely immutable and frozen","C) It can only hold primitive numbers","D) It can be declared without an initial value"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'const' creates an immutable variable binding (it cannot be reassigned with '='). However, if the value is an object or array, its internal properties or items can still be mutated."},{"id":535,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"How do you set an inline style property (e.g. color to blue) on a DOM element in JavaScript?","options":["A) element.style.color = 'blue';","B) element.css('color', 'blue');","C) element.setAttribute('style-color', 'blue');","D) element.color = 'blue';"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inline styles are set via the 'element.style' object using camelCase property names (e.g. element.style.color = 'blue'; element.style.backgroundColor = 'red')."},{"module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"How does the 'this' keyword behave inside an ES6 Arrow Function '() => {}' compared to a standard function?","options":["A) Arrow functions do not bind their own 'this'; they inherit 'this' lexically from their enclosing execution context","B) 'this' in arrow functions always refers to the window object","C) Arrow functions cannot use 'this'","D) 'this' refers to the calling button"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Arrow functions capture the 'this' value of the enclosing lexical scope at the time they are created, eliminating issues where callback functions lose outer context.","id":536,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"How do you access custom data attributes (e.g. '<div data-user-id=\"42\">') on an element in JavaScript?","options":["A) element.dataset.userId","B) element.data.userId","C) element.customData('userId')","D) element.attributes.userId"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'dataset' property exposes all 'data-*' attributes on the element in camelCase: 'data-user-id' becomes accessible via 'element.dataset.userId'.","id":537,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"Which DOM method traverses UP the tree from the current element to find the closest ancestor matching a CSS selector?","options":["A) element.closest(selector)","B) element.findParent(selector)","C) element.parentElementMatching(selector)","D) element.up(selector)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'element.closest(selector)' inspects the current element and traverses up through its ancestors, returning the first element that matches the specified selector, or null.","id":538,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"Which array method transforms each element in an array and returns a new array with the mapped results?","options":["A) Array.prototype.map()","B) Array.prototype.forEach()","C) Array.prototype.filter()","D) Array.prototype.reduce()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"map() calls a provided callback function once for each element in an array and constructs a new array from the returned values.","id":539,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 6: JavaScript Fundamentals & DOM","question":"What feature in ES6 allows embedding expressions and multi-line strings using backticks (`` ` ``)?","options":["A) Template Literals","B) String Formatters","C) Interpolated Text","D) Dynamic Strings"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Template literals (enclosed in backticks) support multi-line strings and string interpolation with embedded expressions: `${expression}`.","id":540,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":541,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What standard method registers an event handler function on a DOM element?","options":["A) element.addEventListener('click', handler);","B) element.attachEvent('onclick', handler);","C) element.registerEvent('click', handler);","D) element.bind('click', handler);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"addEventListener(type, listener [, options]) is the standard W3C method for attaching an event listener to a target DOM node without overwriting existing listeners."},{"id":542,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What are the three sequential phases of DOM event propagation?","options":["A) Capturing phase -> Target phase -> Bubbling phase","B) Bubbling phase -> Target phase -> Capturing phase","C) Capture phase -> Commit phase -> Render phase","D) Dispatch phase -> Execute phase -> Dispose phase"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Event flow travels: 1. Capturing phase (traveling down from Window to the target), 2. Target phase (executing on the target element), 3. Bubbling phase (traveling up from target back to Window)."},{"id":543,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What method prevents the browser's default action associated with an event (such as navigating when clicking a link or submitting a form)?","options":["A) event.preventDefault()","B) event.stopPropagation()","C) event.cancelBubble()","D) event.halt()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"event.preventDefault() cancels the default behavior of the event without stopping its propagation along the DOM tree."},{"id":544,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What method stops an event from bubbling or capturing further up or down the DOM hierarchy?","options":["A) event.stopPropagation()","B) event.preventDefault()","C) event.exit()","D) event.terminate()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"event.stopPropagation() prevents further propagation of the current event in the capturing and bubbling phases."},{"id":545,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What is the difference between 'event.target' and 'event.currentTarget' inside an event handler?","options":["A) event.target is the element that actually triggered the event; event.currentTarget is the element to which the event handler is currently attached","B) event.target is always the document window","C) event.currentTarget is the next element in the DOM","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"event.target refers to the innermost element where the event originated (e.g. a clicked button). event.currentTarget refers to the element that currently owns the active event listener."},{"id":546,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What is 'Event Delegation' in JavaScript?","options":["A) Attaching a single event listener to a common parent element to handle events for all current and future child elements by leveraging event bubbling","B) Handing off event processing to a background server thread","C) Cloning event handlers to every element on the page","D) Using setTimeout to delay event execution"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Event delegation is a design pattern where an event listener is placed on a parent container to manage events triggered by any descendant, relying on bubbling and event.target."},{"id":547,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"How do you configure an event listener to execute during the CAPTURING phase instead of the default bubbling phase?","options":["A) Pass true (or { capture: true }) as the third argument to addEventListener","B) Prefix the event name with 'capture:'","C) Use element.captureEventListener()","D) Set event.phase = 'capturing'"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The third argument of addEventListener specifies options or 'useCapture'. Passing 'true' (or '{ capture: true }') causes the handler to fire during the capture phase."},{"id":548,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"Which event fires when the initial HTML document has been completely parsed and DOM tree built, without waiting for stylesheets and images to finish loading?","options":["A) DOMContentLoaded","B) load","C) readystatechange","D) init"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'DOMContentLoaded' fires as soon as the DOM tree is constructed. The 'load' event fires later, once all stylesheets, images, and sub-resources have completely finished downloading."},{"id":549,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What method removes an event listener previously added with addEventListener?","options":["A) element.removeEventListener(type, handler);","B) element.detachEvent(type, handler);","C) element.deleteEventListener(type);","D) element.unbind(type);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"removeEventListener(type, handler) detaches the listener. It requires an exact reference to the original function handler (anonymous functions cannot be detached this way)."},{"id":550,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What method stops the event immediately and prevents ANY other listeners on the same element from being executed?","options":["A) event.stopImmediatePropagation()","B) event.stopPropagation()","C) event.preventDefault()","D) event.break()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"event.stopImmediatePropagation() stops the event propagation to parent elements AND prevents any remaining event listeners registered on the same element from being invoked."},{"module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"How do you create and dispatch a custom event with custom data in modern JavaScript?","options":["A) const ev = new CustomEvent('userLogin', { detail: { id: 10 } }); element.dispatchEvent(ev);","B) element.fireEvent('userLogin', { id: 10 });","C) document.trigger('userLogin', 10);","D) window.broadcast('userLogin', 10);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'new CustomEvent(name, { detail: data })' initializes a custom event. Calling 'element.dispatchEvent(event)' fires the event through standard DOM event propagation.","id":551,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What does the option '{ once: true }' do when passed to addEventListener?","options":["A) Automatically removes the event listener after it is triggered the first time","B) Prevents other listeners from firing","C) Limits the page to one click per second","D) Disables event bubbling"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passing '{ once: true }' in the addEventListener options object causes the listener to be invoked at most once and then automatically detached.","id":552,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What is the benefit of passing '{ passive: true }' to touch or scroll event listeners?","options":["A) Informs the browser that the handler will NEVER call preventDefault(), allowing the browser to scroll smoothly on the compositor thread without waiting for JS execution","B) Delays execution of the event handler","C) Makes touch events work with a mouse","D) Prevents CPU sleep"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passive event listeners promise that preventDefault() will not be called, enabling browser rendering engines to perform butter-smooth scrolling and touch interactions asynchronously.","id":553,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What method returns the array of all DOM elements traversed by an event during propagation?","options":["A) event.composedPath()","B) event.path","C) event.getElements()","D) event.ancestorList()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'event.composedPath()' returns the event's path as an array of EventTarget objects through which the event bubbles, from source to Window.","id":554,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 7: DOM Event Handling","question":"What is 'Debouncing' an event handler (such as a search input or window resize listener)?","options":["A) Delaying the execution of a function until a specified idle duration has elapsed since the last time the event was triggered","B) Running the function at regular intervals","C) Canceling the event permanently","D) Disabling keyboard input"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Debouncing ensures that a function is not executed until a quiet period has passed since the last event invocation, preventing costly repetitive operations during rapid typing or resizing.","id":555,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":556,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What are the four components of the standard CSS Box Model from inside out?","options":["A) Content -> Padding -> Border -> Margin","B) Content -> Margin -> Border -> Padding","C) Margin -> Border -> Padding -> Content","D) Content -> Outline -> Border -> Margin"],"answer":"A","explanation":"From innermost to outermost, the CSS box model consists of: Content area, Padding (space around content), Border (around padding), and Margin (clearance outside border)."},{"id":557,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What does the CSS property 'box-sizing: border-box' do?","options":["A) Includes padding and border within the specified element's total width and height calculations","B) Adds a 3D border around the box","C) Forces the element to render as a table cell","D) Removes all margins from the element"],"answer":"A","explanation":"With 'border-box', an element's declared width and height include both content, padding, and border, preventing layout breakage when adding padding or borders."},{"id":558,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"Which CSS display property turns a container into a flexible flexbox layout context?","options":["A) display: flex;","B) display: flexbox;","C) flex-container: true;","D) display: grid-flex;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'display: flex' creates a flex container, establishing a flex formatting context for all its direct children."},{"id":559,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"In CSS Flexbox, which property aligns flex items along the MAIN axis?","options":["A) justify-content","B) align-items","C) align-content","D) flex-direction"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'justify-content' aligns flex items along the main axis (e.g. flex-start, center, space-between, space-around). 'align-items' aligns items along the cross axis."},{"id":560,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"Which CSS property rounds the outer corners of an element's border?","options":["A) border-radius","B) corner-curve","C) border-curve","D) box-round"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'border-radius' specifies the radius of the element's corners, allowing rounded corners or pill/circular shapes (e.g. border-radius: 50%)."},{"id":561,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What is the difference between 'display: none' and 'visibility: hidden'?","options":["A) 'display: none' removes the element entirely from document flow; 'visibility: hidden' hides the element while still reserving its physical space in layout","B) 'visibility: hidden' removes the element from the DOM","C) 'display: none' only hides background colors","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'display: none' causes the element not to be rendered at all, taking 0 space. 'visibility: hidden' makes the element invisible, but preserves its width and height in the layout flow."},{"id":562,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"In Flexbox, which property controls whether flex items are forced onto a single line or can wrap onto multiple lines?","options":["A) flex-wrap","B) flex-flow","C) line-wrap","D) white-space"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'flex-wrap: wrap' allows flex items to wrap onto multiple lines as needed, instead of shrinking to fit on a single line (nowrap)."},{"id":563,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"Which CSS property defines the transition animation between different property states?","options":["A) transition","B) transform","C) keyframe","D) morph"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'transition' shorthand property specifies the CSS property to animate, duration, timing function, and delay (e.g. transition: all 0.3s ease-in-out)."},{"id":564,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What CSS property applies 2D or 3D transformations like rotating, scaling, or skewing an element?","options":["A) transform (e.g. transform: rotate(45deg);)","B) transition","C) animation","D) perspective"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'transform' property modifies the visual rendering of an element (rotate, scale, translate, skew) without affecting the normal document flow."},{"id":565,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What CSS property adds shadow effects around an element's frame?","options":["A) box-shadow","B) text-shadow","C) element-shadow","D) border-shadow"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'box-shadow' adds shadow effects around an element's box using offset-x, offset-y, blur-radius, spread-radius, and color."},{"module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"Which CSS Grid declaration creates responsive columns that automatically fit as many minimum 250px columns as possible into the container?","options":["A) grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));","B) grid-columns: auto 250px;","C) display: grid-responsive(250px);","D) grid-template-columns: fit(250px);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr))' generates an adaptive grid layout that fits as many columns of at least 250px into the available space, expanding them to fill remaining room.","id":566,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What is the CSS 'gap' property used for in Flexbox and Grid containers?","options":["A) Defines the spacing (gutters) between grid cells or flex items without applying margin to outer edges","B) Sets empty space around the outside of the page","C) Creates holes inside text","D) Sets font letter spacing"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'gap' (row-gap and column-gap) property sets gutter spacing between adjacent flex or grid items, replacing hacky margin offsets.","id":567,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What is a Stacking Context in CSS?","options":["A) A 3D conceptualization of HTML elements along an imaginary Z-axis, determining which elements visually overlap in front of others","B) The order of CSS stylesheets in <head>","C) A JavaScript call stack","D) A flexbox direction model"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Stacking contexts manage element layering on the z-axis. They are formed by conditions like position (relative/absolute) with z-index, opacity < 1, transform, or filter.","id":568,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"Which CSS at-rule is used to define keyframe stages in CSS animations?","options":["A) @keyframes animationName { 0% { ... } 100% { ... } }","B) @animation animationName { ... }","C) @frames animationName { ... }","D) @transitions animationName { ... }"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '@keyframes' rule defines the CSS properties that change over the course of an animation cycle using percentage markers (0% to 100% or from/to).","id":569,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 8: CSS3 Box Model & Layout","question":"What is the default value of the CSS 'position' property?","options":["A) static","B) relative","C) absolute","D) fixed"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'static' is the default CSS positioning. Static elements render in normal document flow and ignore top, right, bottom, left, and z-index properties.","id":570,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":571,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What is the purpose of the viewport meta tag '<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">'?","options":["A) Sets the viewport width to match the device's physical screen width and establishes an initial 1:1 zoom ratio on mobile browsers","B) Forces desktop monitor dimensions on smartphones","C) Prevents users from scrolling","D) Enables offline website caching"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The viewport meta tag instructs mobile browsers to render the page at the screen's actual device width and scale it 100%, preventing mobile browsers from shrinking pages down as if on desktop."},{"id":572,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"Which CSS at-rule is used to apply style blocks only when specified device criteria (like screen width or orientation) are met?","options":["A) @media","B) @responsive","C) @device","D) @screen"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '@media' rule declares media queries, conditionally applying CSS rules based on features like min-width, max-width, resolution, or orientation."},{"id":573,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What does the media query '@media screen and (max-width: 768px)' target?","options":["A) Screens with a viewport width of 768 pixels or narrower (typically tablets and mobile phones)","B) Screens wider than 768 pixels only","C) Printers printing pages under 768mm wide","D) Screens with exactly 768 physical pixels"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'(max-width: 768px)' activates the enclosed styles only when the viewport width is less than or equal to 768 pixels."},{"id":574,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What is the 'Mobile-First' responsive design approach?","options":["A) Designing base CSS styles for mobile viewports first, using min-width media queries to progressively enhance the layout for larger tablet and desktop screens","B) Designing only for smartphones and ignoring desktop computers","C) Writing native iOS Objective-C code before HTML","D) Compressing all images to under 10 KB"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Mobile-first establishes base default styles for small screens without media queries, then uses progressive '@media (min-width: ...)' queries to enhance layouts for larger displays."},{"id":575,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What is the difference between 'rem' and 'em' units in CSS typography?","options":["A) 'rem' is relative to the root <html> font size; 'em' is relative to the font size of the immediate parent element","B) 'rem' is absolute pixels; 'em' is relative","C) 'em' is relative to the root; 'rem' is relative to parent","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'rem' (root em) always scales relative to the root <html> element's font size (preventing compounding issues). 'em' scales relative to the immediate element's inherited parent font-size."},{"id":576,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What do the viewport units 'vw' and 'vh' represent?","options":["A) 1% of the viewport width and 1% of the viewport height, respectively","B) Virtual width and virtual height in pixels","C) Vector weight and vector hue","D) Video window measurements"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'1vw' equals 1% of the width of the current viewport, and '1vh' equals 1% of the height of the current viewport."},{"id":577,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"Which media type targets content specifically when being printed or formatted in print preview?","options":["A) @media print","B) @media paper","C) @media printer","D) @media hardcopy"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'@media print' applies styles specifically for printing (such as hiding navigation bars, switching backgrounds to white, and displaying link URLs)."},{"id":578,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"How do you make an image responsive so that it never overflows its container but preserves its aspect ratio?","options":["A) img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }","B) img { width: 100vw; height: 100vh; }","C) img { width: auto; height: 100%; }","D) img { resize: both; }"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Setting 'max-width: 100%; height: auto;' ensures the image scales down proportionally if its container shrinks, without stretching or exceeding container bounds."},{"id":579,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What media feature detects whether a device is in landscape or portrait orientation?","options":["A) orientation (e.g. @media (orientation: landscape))","B) device-aspect","C) screen-direction","D) rotation"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'orientation' media feature matches based on whether the viewport width is greater than its height ('landscape') or height is greater than or equal to width ('portrait')."},{"id":580,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What are 'Breakpoints' in responsive web design?","options":["A) Specific viewport widths (e.g. 576px, 768px, 992px, 1200px) where the layout reorganizes via media queries for optimal viewing","B) Points in JavaScript where the debugger pauses","C) Network disconnection points","D) Page load time timeouts"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Breakpoints are predefined screen width thresholds where media query rules alter navigation, columns, or font sizing to optimize UX across different device classes."},{"module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"Which media query feature detects whether the operating system is set to Dark Mode?","options":["A) @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)","B) @media (theme: dark)","C) @media (color-mode: night)","D) @media (os-appearance: dark)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'prefers-color-scheme: dark' is a CSS media feature that matches when the user's operating system or browser theme is configured for dark appearance.","id":581,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What does the CSS feature query '@supports (display: grid)' do?","options":["A) Conditionally applies CSS blocks only if the user's browser supports the specified CSS property/value pair","B) Downloads polyfills automatically","C) Submits browser bug reports","D) Checks operating system specs"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '@supports' at-rule provides native CSS feature detection, applying styles only if the browser engine supports the queried CSS capability.","id":582,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"Which HTML5 element provides art direction by allowing multiple '<source>' elements with media queries for different image crops on mobile vs desktop?","options":["A) <picture>","B) <responsive-image>","C) <gallery>","D) <canvas>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The <picture> element wraps multiple <source media=\"...\"> definitions alongside a fallback <img>, enabling the browser to pick optimal image crops for varying device viewports.","id":583,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"What does the CSS 'clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem)' function achieve for fluid responsive font sizing?","options":["A) Sets a preferred fluid size (2.5vw) while enforcing an absolute minimum of 1rem and an absolute maximum of 2rem","B) Sets three font sizes for small, medium, and large screens","C) Rounds font sizes to nearest integers","D) Restricts font weight"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'clamp(min, val, max)' restricts a value between lower and upper bounds, popular for fluid typography that scales smoothly with viewport width without growing too large or small.","id":584,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 9: Responsive Web Design","question":"Why should media queries in a Mobile-First responsive stylesheet primarily use 'min-width' rather than 'max-width'?","options":["A) Because base styles target small screens, and 'min-width' progressively applies rules as screen space expands without needing style resets","B) 'max-width' is deprecated in CSS3","C) 'min-width' loads faster on 4G networks","D) 'min-width' disables desktop monitors"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Mobile-first sets baseline mobile styles cleanly, using progressive 'min-width' queries to layer layout enhancements as viewport width increases without overriding mobile rules.","id":585,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":586,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"What attribute must be added to an HTML element to make it draggable using the HTML5 Drag and Drop API?","options":["A) draggable=\"true\"","B) drag=\"enabled\"","C) movable=\"true\"","D) allow-drag=\"yes\""],"answer":"A","explanation":"Setting 'draggable=\"true\"' on an element enables the native HTML5 Drag and Drop capabilities for that element."},{"id":587,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which event fires on the element being dragged as soon as the user starts dragging it?","options":["A) dragstart","B) dragenter","C) draginit","D) dragbegin"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'dragstart' fires on the source element when dragging begins, typically used to initialize data via the dataTransfer object."},{"id":588,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"What MUST be done in the 'dragover' event handler on a drop target to allow elements to be dropped onto it?","options":["A) Call event.preventDefault()","B) Call event.stopPropagation()","C) Return true","D) Set target.allowDrop = true"],"answer":"A","explanation":"By default, browsers reject drops on elements. Calling 'event.preventDefault()' in the 'dragover' event listener overrides this default behavior and designates the element as a valid drop zone."},{"id":589,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which object on the drag event is used to hold and retrieve data transferred during drag operations?","options":["A) event.dataTransfer","B) event.clipboardData","C) event.dragPayload","D) window.dragStore"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'event.dataTransfer' object stores the payload during drag operations via setData(format, data) and retrieves it in the drop event via getData(format)."},{"id":590,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"What API in HTML5 enables web applications to access the geographical location of the user's device?","options":["A) Geolocation API (navigator.geolocation)","B) GPS Service API","C) MapPosition API","D) LocationTracker API"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The W3C Geolocation API is exposed via 'navigator.geolocation', providing asynchronous methods to obtain latitude and longitude."},{"id":591,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which method of navigator.geolocation retrieves the user's current physical position once?","options":["A) getCurrentPosition(successCallback, errorCallback, options)","B) getCoordinates()","C) fetchLocation()","D) readPosition()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(success, error, options)' requests a single snapshot of the device's current coordinates."},{"id":592,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which method of navigator.geolocation continuously monitors and notifies updates whenever the device moves?","options":["A) watchPosition()","B) trackPosition()","C) streamCoordinates()","D) followUser()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'navigator.geolocation.watchPosition()' registers a handler that is called repeatedly whenever the position changes, returning a watch ID that can be cleared with clearWatch()."},{"id":593,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"What privacy protection is strictly enforced by modern browsers when an application calls Geolocation API methods?","options":["A) The browser must explicitly prompt the user for permission to share their location, and it requires a secure context (HTTPS)","B) Geolocation works silently without notifying the user","C) It only works when connected via Ethernet cable","D) It requires payment to the cellular carrier"],"answer":"A","explanation":"To safeguard user privacy, browsers mandate an explicit user permission dialog before disclosing coordinates and restrict Geolocation strictly to secure origins (HTTPS or localhost)."},{"id":594,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Where are latitude and longitude values found in the position object passed to the Geolocation success callback?","options":["A) position.coords.latitude and position.coords.longitude","B) position.location.lat and position.location.lng","C) position.geo.x and position.geo.y","D) position.latitude and position.longitude"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The success callback receives a Geoposition object containing 'coords' (Coordinates object) which exposes latitude, longitude, accuracy, altitude, etc."},{"id":595,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which option can be passed to getCurrentPosition to request high accuracy (e.g. turning on mobile GPS)?","options":["A) { enableHighAccuracy: true }","B) { accuracy: 'maximum' }","C) { useGPS: true }","D) { highPrecision: true }"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passing '{ enableHighAccuracy: true }' in the PositionOptions object hints the browser to use more accurate positioning methods like device GPS, albeit at higher battery consumption."},{"module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which event fires on the dragged element when the user finishes dragging, whether dropped successfully or canceled?","options":["A) dragend","B) dragleave","C) dragfinish","D) dropcomplete"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'dragend' fires on the source draggable element when the drag operation completes (whether successfully dropped or canceled with ESC), used for visual cleanup.","id":596,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"What property on dataTransfer specifies the types of drag operations allowed (e.g. 'copy', 'move', 'link')?","options":["A) dataTransfer.effectAllowed","B) dataTransfer.dropEffect","C) dataTransfer.mode","D) dataTransfer.permission"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'effectAllowed' is set in dragstart on the source element to declare permissible operations ('copy', 'move', 'copyMove'). 'dropEffect' is set on the drop target.","id":597,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Which error code is returned in the Geolocation error callback when the user clicks 'Block' on the browser's location permission dialog?","options":["A) error.PERMISSION_DENIED (code 1)","B) error.POSITION_UNAVAILABLE (code 2)","C) error.TIMEOUT (code 3)","D) error.USER_REFUSED (code 4)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"GeolocationPositionError.PERMISSION_DENIED (value 1) is returned when the user denies permission to share their location or system permissions are turned off.","id":598,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"What does the 'maximumAge' option in Geolocation API calls specify?","options":["A) The maximum age in milliseconds of a cached position that the application is willing to accept instead of querying hardware GPS again","B) The user's age limit","C) How long coordinates remain in localStorage","D) GPS satellite expiration date"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'maximumAge' sets the acceptable age of a cached position in milliseconds. Passing 0 forces the device to retrieve an immediate fresh fix.","id":599,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 10: Drag & Drop and Geolocation","question":"Why does dropping an element onto another element fail by default unless preventDefault() is called in dragover?","options":["A) Default browser behavior for dragover rejects drops on elements to protect against accidental file navigations","B) Because HTML requires an external drop library","C) Because CSS disables drop zones","D) Dropping only works in iframes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Browsers default to not allowing drops on web page elements. Canceling the 'dragover' event via preventDefault() announces that the element is an active drop target.","id":600,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":601,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What is the primary difference between localStorage and sessionStorage?","options":["A) localStorage persists data across browser restarts until explicitly cleared; sessionStorage data is cleared when the browser tab/session closes","B) sessionStorage is stored on the server; localStorage is on the client","C) localStorage only stores numbers; sessionStorage stores strings","D) sessionStorage has a 100 GB limit"],"answer":"A","explanation":"localStorage persists indefinitely until programmatically removed or cleared by the user. sessionStorage is scoped strictly to the current browser tab lifecycle."},{"id":602,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What data type do both localStorage and sessionStorage store for both keys and values?","options":["A) Strings only","B) Binary byte arrays","C) Any JavaScript objects natively","D) JSON documents automatically"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Web Storage APIs store data strictly as key/value pairs of UTF-16 DOMString. Non-string types are automatically converted via .toString()."},{"id":603,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"How do you store a complex JavaScript object in localStorage?","options":["A) Serialize it to a JSON string using JSON.stringify(obj) before saving","B) Pass the object directly into localStorage.setItem() without conversion","C) Use localStorage.setObject()","D) Cast the object to XML"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Because localStorage only stores strings, objects must be serialized using 'JSON.stringify(myObj)' before calling setItem(), and parsed back via 'JSON.parse()' upon retrieval."},{"id":604,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What method completely empties all keys stored in localStorage for the current domain origin?","options":["A) localStorage.clear()","B) localStorage.removeAll()","C) localStorage.reset()","D) localStorage.drop()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'localStorage.clear()' removes all key/value pairs stored in local storage for the calling origin."},{"id":605,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What is the typical storage capacity limit for localStorage per origin in modern browsers?","options":["A) ~5 MB to 10 MB","B) 4 KB","C) 1 GB","D) 100 MB"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Modern browsers allocate approximately 5 MB (some up to 10 MB) of storage per origin for Web Storage, vastly exceeding HTTP cookies (which are capped at 4 KB)."},{"id":606,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"How does Web Storage differ fundamentally from HTTP Cookies regarding network traffic?","options":["A) Cookies are sent with every single HTTP request header to the server; Web Storage data stays purely on the client and is never transmitted automatically","B) Cookies cannot be read by JavaScript","C) Web Storage is transmitted automatically with image downloads","D) Cookies are stored on disk; Web Storage is in RAM only"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cookies are transmitted with every HTTP request header to the server, adding network overhead. Web Storage remains client-side only, accessible solely by local JavaScript."},{"id":607,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"In the HTML5 Application Cache (AppCache), what file must be served with MIME type 'text/cache-manifest' to list resources for offline caching?","options":["A) Cache manifest file (e.g. app.manifest)","B) offline.json","C) cache.xml","D) robots.txt"],"answer":"A","explanation":"AppCache relied on a manifest file (linked via <html manifest=\"app.appcache\">) with the MIME type text/cache-manifest listing files to store offline."},{"id":608,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What are the three core sections in an AppCache manifest file?","options":["A) CACHE, NETWORK, and FALLBACK","B) ONLINE, OFFLINE, and CACHE","C) HEADER, BODY, and FOOTER","D) STATIC, DYNAMIC, and ERROR"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An AppCache manifest contains: CACHE: (files to download and cache locally), NETWORK: (files that always require an internet connection), and FALLBACK: (substitutes when offline)."},{"id":609,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What property on the 'window.navigator' object indicates whether the browser currently has network connectivity?","options":["A) navigator.onLine","B) navigator.connected","C) navigator.isOffline","D) navigator.networkStatus"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'navigator.onLine' returns a boolean (true if the browser has an active network connection, false if offline). Window events 'online' and 'offline' notify state changes."},{"id":610,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What event fires on the window object in OTHER tabs/windows of the same domain whenever a storage entry is modified?","options":["A) storage event","B) dataupdate event","C) sync event","D) changestorage event"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'storage' event fires on 'window' in other open tabs or windows sharing the same origin, providing key, oldValue, newValue, and url to synchronize state."},{"module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What browser technology is the modern, powerful asynchronous transactional database for storing rich structured data offline, replacing Web SQL?","options":["A) IndexedDB","B) SQLite Native","C) AppCache DB","D) CloudStore"],"answer":"A","explanation":"IndexedDB is the W3C standard asynchronous, transactional, object-oriented database in modern browsers for storing significant amounts of structured data, objects, and blobs.","id":611,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What is the modern standard technology that replaced Application Cache (AppCache) for reliable offline web experiences and background sync?","options":["A) Service Workers and Cache API","B) Web Sockets","C) Flash Local Storage","D) WebRTC"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Service Workers provide a programmable proxy layer capable of intercepting network requests, caching fine-grained assets, and providing offline functionality, officially superseding AppCache.","id":612,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What happens if a user duplicates a browser tab regarding sessionStorage?","options":["A) The new tab receives a snapshot copy of the previous tab's sessionStorage, but subsequent changes in either tab remain completely isolated","B) The new tab's sessionStorage is completely empty","C) Both tabs share and synchronize changes in real time","D) The browser throws a security warning"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Duplicating a tab copies the existing sessionStorage snapshot into the new tab, but both tabs diverge independently; mutations in one tab do not affect the other.","id":613,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What properties does the 'storage' event object contain when another tab updates localStorage?","options":["A) key, oldValue, newValue, url, and storageArea","B) target, bubbles, type, and detail","C) id, data, hash, and timestamp","D) action, status, and rows"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The StorageEvent provides key (the modified key), oldValue (previous value), newValue (new value), url (document where change occurred), and storageArea.","id":614,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 11: Offline Support & Web Storage","question":"What API provides insight into how much storage space is currently used and available to the origin?","options":["A) navigator.storage.estimate()","B) window.getStorageQuota()","C) document.diskSpace()","D) localStorage.getCapacity()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The StorageManager API ('navigator.storage.estimate()') returns a Promise resolving to an object containing 'usage' and 'quota' byte values.","id":615,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":616,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"What is the primary conceptual difference between SVG and HTML5 Canvas?","options":["A) SVG is XML-based vector graphics where elements exist as DOM nodes; Canvas is a pixel-based raster bitmap drawn procedurally via JavaScript","B) Canvas can be scaled indefinitely without pixelation; SVG cannot","C) SVG only works in Internet Explorer","D) Canvas requires Flash player to render"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SVG is vector-based: shapes (<circle>, <rect>) are XML DOM elements that can be styled with CSS and receive event handlers. Canvas is a procedural 2D/3D pixel bitmap."},{"id":617,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"What happens to a Canvas drawing when the user zooms in on a browser window?","options":["A) It can become pixelated or blurry because it is a raster bitmap","B) It automatically rescales its vectors with infinite sharpness","C) It redraws itself using SVG nodes","D) The browser throws an error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Because Canvas renders to a fixed resolution bitmap of pixels, zooming in enlarges pixels and results in pixelation unless manually redrawn at higher pixel density."},{"id":618,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"How do you obtain the 2D drawing context on an HTML5 <canvas id=\"myCanvas\"> element?","options":["A) const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');","B) const ctx = canvas.getDrawingContext();","C) const ctx = canvas.createContext2D();","D) const ctx = canvas.graphics;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'canvas.getContext('2d')' returns the CanvasRenderingContext2D object used to issue drawing commands like fillRect, strokeText, and beginPath."},{"id":619,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Can individual shapes drawn inside an HTML5 <canvas> have DOM event listeners attached directly to them (e.g. circle.addEventListener('click'))?","options":["A) No, because once drawn, shapes are just flat pixels on a bitmap; hit testing must be calculated mathematically via coordinates","B) Yes, each shape drawn on canvas is an HTML element","C) Yes, using canvas.attachShapeEvent()","D) Only if the shape is drawn in red"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Canvas has no DOM nodes for drawn objects. Event listeners must be attached to the <canvas> element itself, and developers must compute coordinates to detect clicks on shapes."},{"id":620,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Which technology is best suited for complex UI icons, scalable logos, and interactive vector maps?","options":["A) SVG","B) Canvas","C) WebGL","D) GIF"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SVG is ideal for user interface icons, logos, and charts because it scales crisply across all display resolutions (Retina/High-DPI), supports CSS styling, and allows DOM accessibility."},{"id":621,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Which technology is best suited for fast-paced pixel games, particle simulations, and real-time video manipulation?","options":["A) Canvas","B) SVG","C) Plain HTML tables","D) XML stylesheets"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HTML5 Canvas is ideal for high-frame-rate rendering (games, particle systems) where thousands of objects are redrawn repeatedly without the overhead of thousands of DOM nodes."},{"id":622,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"How do you draw a filled rectangle on a Canvas 2D context?","options":["A) ctx.fillRect(x, y, width, height);","B) ctx.drawRect(x, y, width, height);","C) ctx.rectangle(x, y, width, height, 'fill');","D) ctx.renderBox(x, y, width, height);"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'ctx.fillRect(x, y, width, height)' fills a rectangle using the current fillStyle at the specified canvas coordinates."},{"id":623,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Which SVG elements are used to provide text descriptions for screen readers to make SVG accessible?","options":["A) <title> and <desc>","B) <alt> and <caption>","C) <aria-text> and <meta>","D) <label> and <info>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inside an <svg> element, adding a '<title>' (accessible name) and '<desc>' (extended description) elements ensures assistive screen readers can interpret the graphic."},{"id":624,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"How do you style the interior color and outline border of an SVG circle using CSS?","options":["A) fill: red; stroke: blue; stroke-width: 2px;","B) background-color: red; border: 2px solid blue;","C) color: red; outline: blue;","D) paint: red; border-color: blue;"],"answer":"A","explanation":"SVG shapes use vector styling properties: 'fill' defines interior color, 'stroke' defines the outline color, and 'stroke-width' sets outline thickness."},{"id":625,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Which method in Canvas clears specified pixels to transparent black within a rectangular area?","options":["A) ctx.clearRect(x, y, width, height)","B) ctx.eraseRect(x, y, width, height)","C) ctx.deletePixels(x, y, width, height)","D) ctx.reset()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'ctx.clearRect(x, y, width, height)' clears all pixels in the designated rectangular area, typically called at the beginning of each frame in an animation loop."},{"module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"What SVG element is the most versatile for drawing any complex custom shape using commands like MoveTo (M), LineTo (L), and ClosePath (Z)?","options":["A) <path>","B) <polygon>","C) <polyline>","D) <shape>"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The '<path>' element with its 'd' attribute defines shapes through a sequence of path commands (M = moveto, L = lineto, C = curveto, Z = closepath).","id":626,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"What do the four values in the SVG 'viewBox=\"min-x min-y width height\"' attribute define?","options":["A) The internal coordinate system and aspect ratio mapping onto the SVG viewport","B) The monitor screen resolution","C) The margin and padding in pixels","D) The 3D camera angle"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The viewBox defines the internal user coordinate space of the SVG graphic, allowing it to scale responsively and preserve aspect ratio across any outer container dimensions.","id":627,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"How do you export the current bitmap contents of an HTML5 <canvas> as a Base64 encoded PNG data URL?","options":["A) const dataUrl = canvas.toDataURL('image/png');","B) const dataUrl = canvas.saveAsImage();","C) const dataUrl = canvas.exportPNG();","D) const dataUrl = ctx.getPixelsAsBase64();"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'canvas.toDataURL(type)' returns a data URI containing a representation of the image in the specified format (defaults to image/png).","id":628,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Where is the coordinate origin (0, 0) located by default on both an HTML5 Canvas and an SVG element?","options":["A) Top-left corner (X increases rightward, Y increases downward)","B) Bottom-left corner (Cartesian system)","C) Center of the element","D) Top-right corner"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Computer graphics coordinate systems place (0, 0) at the top-left corner, with the X axis extending to the right and the Y axis extending downwards.","id":629,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 12: Graphics (SVG vs Canvas)","question":"Which Canvas 2D method renders filled text onto the drawing canvas?","options":["A) ctx.fillText(text, x, y [, maxWidth])","B) ctx.drawText(text, x, y)","C) ctx.write(text, x, y)","D) ctx.print(text, x, y)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'ctx.fillText(text, x, y)' fills a given text at the specified coordinates using the current font and fillStyle.","id":630,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":631,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What does AJAX stand for?","options":["A) Asynchronous JavaScript and XML","B) Automated JSON Application eXchange","C) Advanced JavaScript Async Kernel","D) Application Java Access XML"],"answer":"A","explanation":"AJAX stands for Asynchronous JavaScript and XML, a suite of technologies that allows web pages to send and retrieve data from a web server asynchronously in the background."},{"id":632,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"Which core browser object is historically used to initiate HTTP requests in AJAX?","options":["A) XMLHttpRequest","B) HttpRequest","C) WebConnector","D) SocketRequest"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'XMLHttpRequest' (XHR) object is the foundational browser API used to send HTTP/HTTPS requests to servers and receive responses asynchronously."},{"id":633,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"In XMLHttpRequest, which readyState value indicates that the operation is complete ('DONE')?","options":["A) 4","B) 3","C) 1","D) 200"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The readyState values are: 0 (UNSENT), 1 (OPENED), 2 (HEADERS_RECEIVED), 3 (LOADING), and 4 (DONE - request finished and response is ready)."},{"id":634,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"Which HTTP status code signifies that the server successfully processed the request?","options":["A) 200 OK","B) 304 Not Modified","C) 404 Not Found","D) 500 Internal Server Error"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HTTP 200 OK indicates that the client request succeeded and the server returned the requested payload."},{"id":635,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What method parses a JSON formatted string into a JavaScript object?","options":["A) JSON.parse(jsonString)","B) JSON.stringify(object)","C) JSON.toObject(jsonString)","D) eval(jsonString)"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'JSON.parse()' safely parses a JSON string, constructing the corresponding JavaScript value or object. 'JSON.stringify()' converts an object into a JSON string."},{"id":636,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What security policy restricts a web application from making AJAX requests to a different domain, port, or protocol than the origin that served the page?","options":["A) Same-Origin Policy (SOP)","B) Secure Sockets Policy","C) Content Security Encryption","D) Cross-Domain Block Protocol"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Same-Origin Policy (SOP) is an essential browser security mechanism that prohibits scripts from loading resources or querying endpoints on an origin with a different protocol, host, or port."},{"id":637,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What standard HTTP header mechanism allows a server to explicitly permit cross-origin AJAX requests from other domains?","options":["A) CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)","B) JSONP","C) HTTPS Tunneling","D) FTP Proxy"],"answer":"A","explanation":"CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) uses HTTP response headers (such as 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *') to permit browsers to access resources from external domains."},{"id":638,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What order of calls must be made on an XMLHttpRequest object to initiate an asynchronous GET request?","options":["A) const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest(); xhr.open('GET', url, true); xhr.send();","B) xhr.send(); xhr.open();","C) xhr.execute('GET', url);","D) xhr.connect(url); xhr.fetch();"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sequence is: instantiate the object with 'new XMLHttpRequest()', configure request method and URL with 'xhr.open()', wire event listeners, and transmit the request with 'xhr.send()'."},{"id":639,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What property on an XMLHttpRequest object contains the response data as text?","options":["A) xhr.responseText","B) xhr.data","C) xhr.content","D) xhr.body"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'xhr.responseText' contains the text received from the server as a string (or xhr.responseXML if the response is valid XML)."},{"id":640,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What HTTP method should be used when submitting data via AJAX to create a new record on the server?","options":["A) POST","B) GET","C) OPTIONS","D) HEAD"],"answer":"A","explanation":"HTTP POST is used to submit entity data to a specified resource, resulting in state change or record creation on the server."},{"module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What is the key difference between the modern Fetch API and XMLHttpRequest?","options":["A) Fetch is native Promise-based with cleaner syntax; XMLHttpRequest is callback-based and event-driven","B) XMLHttpRequest does not support HTTP POST","C) Fetch only works with XML","D) XMLHttpRequest is faster than Fetch"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Fetch API provides a modern Promise-based interface that integrates seamlessly with async/await, avoiding the verbose event-driven callback model of XMLHttpRequest.","id":641,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"How do you set custom request headers (such as Content-Type) on an XMLHttpRequest before sending?","options":["A) xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');","B) xhr.addHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');","C) xhr.header('Content-Type', 'application/json');","D) xhr.headers['Content-Type'] = 'application/json';"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'xhr.setRequestHeader(header, value)' sets HTTP request headers. It must be invoked after xhr.open() but prior to xhr.send().","id":642,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"What is the difference between HTTP status code 401 Unauthorized and 403 Forbidden?","options":["A) 401 indicates authentication is required or credentials are invalid; 403 indicates authentication succeeded but the authenticated user lacks permissions to access the resource","B) 401 is a server crash; 403 is client offline","C) 403 is for guest users only","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"401 Unauthorized denotes missing or invalid credentials (who are you?). 403 Forbidden denotes that credentials are authenticated, but the user is forbidden access (you cannot do this).","id":643,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"How do you cancel or abort an active Fetch API request in modern JavaScript?","options":["A) Using an AbortController: pass controller.signal in fetch options and call controller.abort()","B) Calling fetch.cancel()","C) Deleting the promise","D) Calling window.stop()"],"answer":"A","explanation":"AbortController allows canceling fetch requests by passing its 'signal' property into the fetch options and calling 'controller.abort()'.","id":644,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 13: Remote Data & AJAX","question":"Why should calls to JSON.parse() be wrapped in a try...catch block?","options":["A) If the incoming string is malformed or invalid JSON, JSON.parse() throws a synchronous SyntaxError exception that can crash execution","B) Because JSON.parse() returns a Promise","C) To prevent memory corruption","D) Because JSON.parse() is asynchronous"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passing invalid, truncated, or non-JSON strings (like HTML error pages returned from servers) into JSON.parse() throws a fatal SyntaxError unless caught in a try/catch block.","id":645,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"id":646,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What fundamental limitation in JavaScript's standard execution model do Web Workers solve?","options":["A) JavaScript's single-threaded nature where heavy computations block the UI thread and freeze user interactions","B) Inability to make network requests","C) Lack of support for mathematical arithmetic","D) Lack of local storage"],"answer":"A","explanation":"JavaScript is single-threaded; long-running synchronous code freezes the main UI thread. Web Workers run scripts in background worker threads concurrently without interrupting the UI."},{"id":647,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"How do you instantiate a dedicated Web Worker in JavaScript?","options":["A) const worker = new Worker('worker.js');","B) const worker = window.createThread('worker.js');","C) const worker = new BackgroundProcess('worker.js');","D) const worker = Thread.fork('worker.js');"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'new Worker(\"script.js\")' creates and starts a dedicated background worker running the specified JavaScript file."},{"id":648,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"How does the main UI thread communicate and exchange data with a Web Worker?","options":["A) By posting serialized messages using postMessage() and receiving them via the 'onmessage' event listener","B) By directly modifying shared global variables in window","C) By reading and writing to the DOM tree","D) Using SQL queries in tempdb"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Communication between workers and the main thread is strictly message-based: 'worker.postMessage(data)' sends messages, and 'worker.onmessage = function(e) { ... }' handles received messages."},{"id":649,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"Which of the following objects is completely INACCESSIBLE inside a Web Worker thread?","options":["A) document and window (the DOM)","B) navigator","C) location","D) setTimeout / setInterval"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Web Workers have no direct access to the DOM, 'window', or 'document' objects to avoid multi-threaded race conditions. Workers can access navigator, location, setTimeout, and XMLHttpRequest/fetch."},{"id":650,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"How does a Web Worker script terminate ITSELF from within the worker script?","options":["A) close();","B) terminate();","C) exit();","D) destroy();"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inside the worker's own scope, calling 'close()' stops the worker immediately. From the main thread, 'worker.terminate()' terminates the worker."},{"id":651,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What event handler catches and handles uncaught runtime exceptions occurring inside a Web Worker?","options":["A) worker.onerror","B) worker.onfail","C) worker.oncatch","D) worker.onexception"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'onerror' event handler on the Worker object receives an ErrorEvent containing filename, lineno, and message when an unhandled error occurs in the worker."},{"id":652,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"How does data passed via postMessage() typically travel between threads in standard Web Workers?","options":["A) It is cloned via the Structured Clone algorithm (deep copy), ensuring memory isolation","B) It shares identical memory pointers directly","C) It is converted to binary and written to hard drive files","D) It is transmitted over WebSockets"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Data sent via postMessage is duplicated using the Structured Clone algorithm, giving each thread its own independent copy to maintain thread isolation (unless using Transferable objects like ArrayBuffer)."},{"id":653,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What is a Shared Worker in JavaScript?","options":["A) A worker that can be accessed and shared across multiple browser tabs, windows, or iframes running under the same origin","B) A worker shared between different computers on a LAN","C) A worker that shares DOM access with the main thread","D) A peer-to-peer torrent client"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A SharedWorker ('new SharedWorker()') can communicate with multiple browsing contexts (different tabs, windows, or iframes) via MessagePorts."},{"id":654,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What function can be used inside a Web Worker to synchronously load and execute external JavaScript libraries?","options":["A) importScripts('library.js');","B) require('library.js');","C) include('library.js');","D) loadScript('library.js');"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The 'importScripts(url1, url2, ...)' function loads and executes external script files synchronously inside the worker's global scope."},{"id":655,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript","module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What global scope object represents the worker's context inside a dedicated Web Worker?","options":["A) self (or DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope)","B) window","C) document","D) parent"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inside a worker, the global execution context is referenced by 'self' (DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope). Calling postMessage() or addEventListener('message', ...) operates on 'self'."},{"module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What are 'Transferable Objects' (such as ArrayBuffer) in Web Worker communication?","options":["A) Objects whose ownership and memory buffer are transferred instantly with zero copying overhead, making them inaccessible in the sending context","B) Objects transferred via Bluetooth","C) Objects converted to text strings","D) Objects saved to the cloud"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Transferable objects transfer memory ownership directly between contexts with zero-copy overhead. Once transferred, the object is detached and becomes unusable in the sender context.","id":656,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What property on the 'window.navigator' object reports the number of logical processor cores available on the user's computer?","options":["A) navigator.hardwareConcurrency","B) navigator.cpuCores","C) navigator.processors","D) navigator.threadCount"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'navigator.hardwareConcurrency' returns the number of logical processors, enabling web applications to spawn an optimal pool of Web Workers without overloading the CPU.","id":657,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"What is the difference between calling 'worker.terminate()' and 'self.close()'?","options":["A) 'worker.terminate()' is called from the main UI thread to kill the worker immediately; 'self.close()' is called by the worker script inside itself to stop its own execution","B) 'terminate' is asynchronous; 'close' is synchronous","C) 'self.close()' restarts the worker","D) There is no difference"],"answer":"A","explanation":"From the parent thread, calling 'worker.terminate()' forcibly stops the worker thread. From inside the worker, calling 'self.close()' stops its own event loop and terminates itself.","id":658,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"Why does an error inside a Web Worker script not trigger the main thread's 'window.onerror' handler automatically?","options":["A) Web Workers run in an isolated execution context with their own global scope (DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope), dispatching errors to 'worker.onerror' on the Worker instance","B) Worker errors are suppressed by browsers","C) Workers cannot have syntax errors","D) Worker errors trigger alert boxes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Because Web Workers execute in separate thread contexts, unhandled exceptions do not bubble to 'window'. Instead, they fire the 'error' event on the Worker instance in the main script.","id":659,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"},{"module":"Module 14: Web Workers","question":"Can Web Workers initiate network requests using fetch() or XMLHttpRequest?","options":["A) Yes, Web Workers have full access to network APIs like fetch() and XMLHttpRequest to download and upload data in the background","B) No, Web Workers are completely disconnected from the network","C) Only via WebSockets","D) Only if running on HTTPS"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Web Workers can freely make network requests using fetch() or XMLHttpRequest to fetch data, parse it in the background thread, and send processed results back to the UI thread.","id":660,"subject":"HTML, CSS & JavaScript"}];